   },
   "id": "il-14-sre-devops-cloud-security-interview-prep-math-logic",
   "mockTask": {
    "q": "Which single law turns NOT (a AND b AND c) into a disjunction?",
    "choices": [
     "De Morgan's law",
     "Absorption",
     "Double negation",
     "Idempotence"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explain": "NOT (a AND b AND c) = (NOT a) OR (NOT b) OR (NOT c) by repeated De Morgan.",
    "id": "il-14-sre-devops-cloud-security-interview-prep-math-logic--mock",
    "type": "choice"
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Propositions and truth values",
     "body": "A proposition is a statement that is either true or false, never both. `p AND q` is true only when both are true; `p OR q` is true when at least one is true; `NOT p` flips the value. Worked example: evaluate `(p AND q) OR (NOT r)` at p=T, q=F, r=T. First `p AND q` = T AND F = F. Then `NOT r` = NOT T = F. Finally `F OR F` = **F**. So the whole expression is **F**. Work inside-out: parentheses first, then NOT, then AND, then OR — the same precedence habit that prevents bugs in code conditions."
    },
    {
     "title": "Truth tables, computed by enumeration",
     "body": "A truth table lists every assignment of the variables. With n variables there are 2^n rows — for n=3 that is **8** rows, computed as 2^3. Worked example: the implication `p -> q`, defined as `(NOT p) OR q`. Its table: p=T,q=T -> T; p=T,q=F -> F; p=F,q=T -> T; p=F,q=F -> T. The only false row is true-premise/false-conclusion. Enumerating all rows mechanically (as the lab does in Python) removes all doubt: no case is forgotten, because the count 2^n proves the table is complete."
    },
    {
     "title": "De Morgan's laws, verified by brute force",
     "body": "De Morgan's laws: `NOT (p AND q)` equals `(NOT p) OR (NOT q)`, and `NOT (p OR q)` equals `(NOT p) AND (NOT q)`. Worked verification: checking all 4 assignments of (p,q) in Python, every row agrees — **True**. This is the law behind simplifying negated conditions: `NOT (admin AND active)` becomes `NOT admin OR NOT active`. In code and in firewall/policy rules, pushing NOT inward with De Morgan turns an unreadable negated conjunction into a plain disjunction."
    },
    {
     "title": "Implication and the contrapositive",
     "body": "`p -> q` (if p then q) is logically equivalent to its contrapositive `(NOT q) -> (NOT p)`. Worked verification: all 4 assignments agree — **True**. The converse `q -> p` is NOT equivalent (check p=T,q=F: implication false, converse true). Practical use: to prove 'if the build passes then tests ran', prove the contrapositive 'if tests did not run then the build fails' — often the easier direction. Confusing implication with converse is one of the most common logic errors in specifications."
    },
    {
     "title": "Predicate logic over finite domains",
     "body": "Predicates add quantifiers: `FORALL x, P(x)` and `EXISTS x, P(x)`. Over a finite domain you can evaluate them by enumeration. Worked example: domain = {2, 3, 4}, statement `EXISTS x, x is prime AND x > 3`. Check: x=2 (prime, not >3, fails), x=3 (prime, not >3, fails), x=4 (not prime, fails). No witness exists, so the statement is **false**. Change `>3` to `>=3`: x=3 is prime and >= 3, so the statement becomes **true**. Existence claims need exactly one witness; universal claims need every case. This discipline maps directly onto testing: 'there exists an input that crashes' vs 'for all inputs it terminates'."
    },
    {
     "title": "Boolean algebra for systems work",
     "body": "Boolean algebra gives simplification laws: absorption `p OR (p AND q) = p`, distribution, idempotence `p AND p = p`. Worked example: simplify `(admin AND active) OR (admin AND NOT active)`. Factor admin: `admin AND (active OR NOT active)` = `admin AND T` = **admin**. Verified by enumerating all 4 assignments of (admin, active): both sides agree on every row. Why it matters: access-policy engines evaluate exactly these expressions. Simplifying before deploying means fewer rules, faster evaluation, and policies a human can audit. Alerting rules are boolean expressions over signals."
    }
   ],
   "objectives": [
    "Evaluate compound propositions by precedence, inside-out.",
    "Build and read truth tables; prove completeness via the 2^n count.",
    "Apply De Morgan's laws and verify equivalences by brute-force enumeration.",
    "Distinguish implication, converse, and contrapositive — and know which are equivalent."
   ],
