  {
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "What are the four golden signals?",
     "back": "Latency, traffic, errors, saturation — the minimum dashboard for any service. In an interview, reach for them first: ‘I’d check the four golden signals’ is the SRE equivalent of ‘let me reproduce it.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "SLI vs SLO vs SLA?",
     "back": "SLI: the measured indicator (e.g. successful requests / total). SLO: the target on the SLI (99.9% over 30 days). SLA: the contract with consequences. The interview line: ‘SLIs are facts, SLOs are promises, SLAs are contracts.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do error budgets govern releases?",
     "back": "Budget remaining → ship freely. Budget exhausted → freeze risky changes until reliability recovers. It replaces arguments about ‘are we reliable enough’ with arithmetic — the interview line: ‘the budget turns reliability into a joint product decision.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you explain burn-rate alerting?",
     "back": "Instead of alerting on raw error counts, alert on how fast the error budget is being consumed relative to the SLO window: fast-burn (page now) vs slow-burn (ticket). Multiwindow alerts catch both without paging on blips."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you measure SLIs with Prometheus?",
     "back": "Counters for requests and errors (rate over windows), histograms/summaries for latency percentiles, recording rules to precompute SLI ratios, and burn-rate alert rules on top. The interview line: ‘the SLI is a PromQL expression with an SLO attached.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you size an error budget on a whiteboard?",
     "back": "99.9% over 30 days → 0.1% of 43,200 minutes = 43.2 minutes of total allowed badness per month. Say it as policy: 'our budget is roughly one real incident a month — while budget remains we ship, when it's spent we harden.' The interview line: 'budgets are arithmetic, not vibes.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you pick the right SLI for a service?",
     "back": "Measure what the user feels: request success ratio and latency percentiles for serving paths — not CPU. The interview line: ‘the SLI is the user’s experience expressed as a number.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What makes an SLO credible in an interview answer?",
     "back": "Tied to user impact, with the error-budget policy attached: who decides when the budget burns, and what stops. The interview line: ‘an SLO without a budget policy is a wish.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "A latency SLI in Prometheus is typically built from:",
     "back": "**Histogram buckets — enabling accurate percentile computation over windows** — Histograms preserve the distribution: any percentile over any window is computable."
    },
    {
     "front": "Burn-rate alerting’s advantage over static error thresholds is:",
     "back": "**It measures budget consumption relative to the SLO — paging only when the objective is actually at risk** — Thresholds don’t know your SLO; burn rate ties paging directly to objective risk."
    },
    {
     "front": "When the error budget is exhausted, the correct policy is:",
     "back": "**Freeze or slow risky releases until the budget recovers** — The budget is the agreed tradeoff dial: spending it all means reliability work comes first."
    },
    {
     "front": "An error budget is:",
     "back": "**1 − SLO: the allowed failure rate, spent deliberately on releases and risk** — 99.9% SLO → 0.1% budget: reliability as a spendable resource balancing velocity and stability."
    }
   ],
