   "handsOnLab": {
    "id": "sre-interview-prep--sre-sli-slo--lab",
    "title": "Operator's brief: SLIs, SLOs, and Error Budgets",
    "brief": "You will write the artifacts a real operator would produce for SLIs, SLOs, and Error Budgets — incident review, SLO spec, and tradeoff analysis — because interviews probe the seams.",
    "steps": [
     "Write a blameless incident review for a realistic SLIs, SLOs, and Error Budgets failure: timeline, impact in numbers, detection, response, root cause, action items with owners.",
     "Specify the SLOs: indicators, objectives, error budget, and what consumes the budget — with the math shown.",
     "Draw the architecture and annotate every tradeoff: what you chose, what you gave up, why it was worth it.",
     "Write the runbook excerpt: the alert, the first five diagnostic commands, the escalation path.",
     "Prepare the war story: a three-minute telling with numbers, your decisions, and what you'd do differently.",
     "Peer-review the brief as a hiring manager: which claims have evidence, which are buzzwords?"
    ],
    "deliverable": "incident-review.md, slo-spec.md (with math), architecture tradeoffs, runbook excerpt, and war-story script for SLIs, SLOs, and Error Budgets.",
    "checkpoint": "Incident review has numbers and owned action items; SLO math is shown; every architecture choice names its tradeoff; war story runs three minutes."
   },
   "id": "sre-interview-prep--sre-sli-slo",
   "mockTask": {
    "id": "sre-interview-prep--sre-sli-slo--mock",
    "type": "choice",
    "q": "Burn-rate alerting’s advantage over static error thresholds is:",
    "choices": [
     "No Prometheus needed",
     "It measures budget consumption relative to the SLO — paging only when the objective is actually at risk",
     "Simpler config",
     "Fewer metrics"
    ],
    "answer": 1,
    "explain": "Thresholds don’t know your SLO; burn rate ties paging directly to objective risk."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **SLI (Service Level Indicator)** — the measured fact: e.g. successful requests ÷ total requests, or the fraction of requests under a latency bound. SLIs are facts, not promises.\n- **SLO (Service Level Objective)** — the target set on an SLI: e.g. 99.9% of requests succeed over a 30-day window. SLOs are promises the team makes.\n- **SLA (Service Level Agreement)** — the contract with consequences: what happens (credits, penalties) when the SLO is missed. SLAs are contracts with customers.\n- **Error budget** — 1 − SLO: the allowed failure rate, spent deliberately on releases and risk. A 99.9% SLO leaves a 0.1% budget.\n- **Four golden signals** — latency, traffic, errors, saturation: the minimum dashboard for any service, and the first SLI candidates.\n- **Burn rate** — how fast the error budget is being consumed relative to the SLO window. Fast burn pages now; slow burn files a ticket.\n- **Multiwindow alerting** — pairing a short window (catches fast burns) with a long window (confirms slow burns) so blips don't page.\n- **Counter** — a Prometheus metric that only increases: the raw material for request/error-rate SLIs via rate() over windows.\n- **Histogram** — a Prometheus metric with buckets: the raw material for latency-percentile SLIs, since any percentile is computable over any window.\n- **Recording rule** — a precomputed PromQL expression: how expensive SLI ratios are calculated once and queried cheaply."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Do this estimation on paper or a whiteboard — it is the single most common SLO whiteboard exercise.\n1. **State the SLO.** 99.9% successful requests over a 30-day rolling window. Write the budget: 1 − 0.999 = 0.001, i.e. 0.1%.\n2. **Convert the window to minutes.** 30 days × 24 hours × 60 minutes = 43,200 minutes.\n3. **Multiply.** 0.1% × 43,200 = 43.2 minutes of total allowed badness per month — downtime, errors, slow responses, all of it drawn from one account.\n4. **Interpret the number.** That is roughly one 45-minute incident per month, or several small ones. Say it aloud: 'our budget is one real incident a month.'\n5. **Add a burn-rate read.** If you burn 2% of that budget in one hour, you're consuming it ~14× faster than the sustainable rate — that is a fast burn, page now. If you burn 2% in a week, that is a slow burn — ticket it.\n6. **Close with the policy.** Budget remaining → ship freely. Budget exhausted → freeze risky changes until reliability recovers. The arithmetic is the policy."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: a one-page SLO proposal for a sample checkout API, written as if you will defend it in a design review.\n- **Choose three SLIs** from the four golden signals (e.g. request success rate, p99 latency under 300 ms, saturation of the checkout workers), and justify each choice in one sentence.\n- **Set SLO targets and a 30-day window** for each, then compute the error budget in minutes the way the Worked estimation shows.\n- **Write the release policy** as a rule: budget remaining → ship; budget exhausted → freeze. Name who decides.\n- **Specify two burn-rate alerts**: one fast-burn (pages) and one slow-burn (tickets), with the metric each watches.\n- **Defend it aloud in 10 minutes**, then write the three hardest questions you were asked and your improved answers.\nReference shelf (module sources): **Google SRE Books** (https://sre.google/) — SLIs/SLOs, incidents, and reliability math from the source. **Prometheus Documentation** (https://prometheus.io/docs/) — metrics, PromQL, and alerting references."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and rehearsing SLO math is not the same as carrying a production error budget. Real budgets are negotiated with product and customers, and real paging policies are tuned against months of alert history you don't have yet. What this module does give you is the interview's language — SLIs are facts, SLOs are promises, SLAs are contracts — and the arithmetic to defend them on a whiteboard. Vendor specifics follow vendor documentation, which changes; check the linked sources as of their retrieval dates."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: A latency SLI in Prometheus is typically built from:",
     "body": "**Problem.** A latency SLI in Prometheus is typically built from:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** Histogram buckets — enabling accurate percentile computation over windows\n- **Why:** Histograms preserve the distribution: any percentile over any window is computable.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Histograms preserve the distribution: any percentile over any window is computable.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In SLIs, SLOs, and Error Budgets:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts SLIs, SLOs, and Error Budgets interviews probe."
    }
   ],
