   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "Walk the incident lifecycle.",
     "back": "Detect (alerts, user reports) → respond (declare, assemble, IC takes command) → mitigate (stop the bleeding — rollback, shed load) → resolve → learn (blameless postmortem). The interview line: ‘mitigate first, understand second.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What are the incident command roles?",
     "back": "Incident Commander (decides, coordinates — doesn’t debug), Communications (status updates, stakeholders), Scribe (timeline), plus subject-matter experts debugging. The IC’s job is the incident, not the keyboard."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you classify incident severity in an SRE interview?",
     "back": "SEV levels by user impact and urgency: SEV-1 (critical — core function down, all hands), SEV-2 (major degradation), SEV-3 (minor, limited blast radius). Severity is impact; priority is scheduling. The interview line: ‘severity describes the fire, not who fights it.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you communicate during an outage?",
     "back": "One channel (#incident), regular cadence (every 15–30 min even with ‘no update’), impact-first language (who’s affected, what we’re doing, when’s the next update), and a single comms owner. The interview line: ‘silence during an outage is a second outage.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What makes a postmortem blameless and useful?",
     "back": "Blameless: systems and process, never people — or nobody will write honestly. Structure: timeline, impact, root causes (plural — ‘five whys’), action items with owners and dates. The interview line: ‘the postmortem’s value is the action items, not the document.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "When do you escalate an incident's severity?",
     "back": "When the impact scope grows: more users affected, a longer blast radius, or the mitigation isn't working. Severity follows impact, not effort — and the paging policy decides who the escalation wakes. The interview line: 'I escalate on impact, early; downgrading is cheaper than discovering the blast radius at the postmortem.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you run the first five minutes of an incident?",
     "back": "Declare, take command, freeze the scope: who is impacted, what is the mitigation hypothesis, who owns comms. The interview line: ‘the first five minutes buy the next fifty.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What goes in a status update during an outage?",
     "back": "Impact (who is affected), what is known, what is being done, when the next update lands — even if it is ‘no change.’ The interview line: ‘silence during an outage is a second incident.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "An ML system design that only covers the model is weak because:",
     "back": "**Production value lives in data, serving, and monitoring — the model is one piece** — Most ML failures are systems failures: skew, drift, latency — not the algorithm."
    },
    {
     "front": "Blameless postmortems work because:",
     "back": "**Removing blame gets honest timelines — people report what really happened** — Honest data requires psychological safety; blame produces cover stories instead of root causes."
    },
    {
     "front": "Status updates during an outage should lead with:",
     "back": "**Customer impact: who’s affected and what’s being done** — Stakeholders need impact and next-update time; deep technical detail follows for those who need it."
    },
    {
     "front": "Severity vs priority differ in that:",
     "back": "**Severity = impact on users; priority = order of fixing (business decision)** — A severe bug in a deprecated feature can be low priority; a trivial bug blocking launch is high priority."
    }
   ],
