    "checkpoint": "Incident review has numbers and owned action items; SLO math is shown; every architecture choice names its tradeoff; war story runs three minutes."
   },
   "id": "sre-interview-prep--sre-incident-command",
   "mockTask": {
    "id": "sre-interview-prep--sre-incident-command--mock",
    "type": "choice",
    "q": "Blameless postmortems work because:",
    "choices": [
     "They skip action items",
     "Removing blame gets honest timelines — people report what really happened",
     "They’re shorter",
     "They’re optional"
    ],
    "answer": 1,
    "explain": "Honest data requires psychological safety; blame produces cover stories instead of root causes."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **Incident** — a declared, time-boxed response to user-impacting degradation: saying the word 'incident' is what starts the machinery.\n- **Incident Commander (IC)** — decides and coordinates; owns the incident, never the keyboard. The IC does not debug.\n- **Communications lead** — owns status updates to stakeholders on a fixed cadence, in impact-first language.\n- **Scribe** — owns the timeline: what happened, when, who did what. The timeline is the postmortem's raw material.\n- **Severity** — the impact axis: how many users, how badly degraded. Separate from priority, which is the fix-ordering business decision.\n- **Mitigation** — stopping the bleeding (rollback, shed load, fail over). Comes before understanding the cause.\n- **Resolution** — the service is healthy and the incident is closed; the learning continues in the postmortem.\n- **Blameless postmortem** — a review of systems and process, never people: timeline, impact, plural root causes, action items with owners and dates.\n- **Paging policy** — which severities wake whom, and how fast. Severity without a paging policy is just vocabulary."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Run this 20-minute mock incident with a partner or a timer — the reps are the point.\n1. **Declare.** Say 'I'm declaring an incident' out loud. Open a dedicated channel. Assign IC, Communications, and Scribe — one person can wear two hats in drill, never all three.\n2. **State impact first.** Scribe writes: what's degraded, who's affected, when it started. Communications posts the first status: impact, what's being done, when the next update lands.\n3. **Mitigate before diagnosing.** IC picks the fastest bleed-stopper available — rollback the last deploy, shed non-critical load, fail over. Narrate the choice: 'mitigate first, understand second.'\n4. **Hold the cadence.** Communications updates every 15 minutes even with 'no update' — silence during an outage is a second outage.\n5. **Resolve explicitly.** IC declares the incident resolved when the SLI recovers, not when the fix feels done. Scribe closes the timeline.\n6. **Schedule the postmortem.** Within 24–48 hours: timeline, impact, root causes (plural, five-whys with evidence), action items with owners and dates. Blameless, always."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: a full mock incident on a sample scenario — the checkout API is returning 500s for 20% of requests — run end to end, then written up.\n- **Run the drill** using the Hands-on procedure: declare, assign roles, mitigate (choose rollback vs shed-load and defend the choice aloud), hold comms cadence.\n- **Produce the timeline** as the scribe would: timestamps, decisions, who made them.\n- **Write the blameless postmortem**: impact statement, five-whys down to a systemic cause, and three action items each with an owner and a date.\n- **Add the severity call**: what severity you declared, why, and who got paged under your paging policy.\n- **Debrief**: what broke down in your process (not the fake system), and the one change you'll make before the next drill.\nReference shelf (module sources): **Google SRE Books** (https://sre.google/) — incident response chapters from the source. **CISA Resources & Tools** (https://www.cisa.gov/resources-tools) — US government guidance on incident response and best practice."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and a rehearsed drill is not production on-call. Real incidents involve tired humans, incomplete telemetry, and business pressure that no simulation reproduces. What transfers is the structure: declare, mitigate first, communicate on cadence, review blamelessly. Never present drill experience as production incident command on a resume or in an interview — describe it as rehearsed process, which is exactly what interviewers want to hear practiced."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: An ML system design that only covers the model is weak because:",
     "body": "**Problem.** An ML system design that only covers the model is weak because:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** Production value lives in data, serving, and monitoring — the model is one piece\n- **Why:** Most ML failures are systems failures: skew, drift, latency — not the algorithm.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Most ML failures are systems failures: skew, drift, latency — not the algorithm.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In Incident Response and Command:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts Incident Response and Command interviews probe."
    }
   ],
