   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "Metrics, logs, traces — the three pillars in an SRE interview?",
     "back": "Metrics: numbers over time (alerts, trends). Logs: discrete events with context (the why). Traces: request journeys across services (the where). The interview line: ‘metrics tell me something’s wrong, traces show me where, logs tell me why.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What are the four golden signals?",
     "back": "Latency, traffic, errors, saturation — the minimum dashboard for any service. In an interview, reach for them first: ‘I’d check the four golden signals’ is the SRE equivalent of ‘let me reproduce it.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "USE vs RED methods?",
     "back": "USE (Utilization, Saturation, Errors) for resources — ‘is the box healthy.’ RED (Rate, Errors, Duration) for services — ‘is the service healthy.’ In an interview, name which lens fits: debugging a host → USE; debugging an API → RED."
    },
    {
     "front": "Why alert on symptoms, not causes?",
     "back": "Users feel symptoms (latency, error rate); causes are legion and change. Symptom alerts (burn rate, golden signals) page on user impact; cause-based alerts (‘disk 90%’) page on things that may not matter — alert fatigue follows."
    },
    {
     "front": "What makes a dashboard that ‘answers questions’?",
     "back": "Golden signals up top, drill-down from symptom to cause, time-range comparisons, and annotations for deploys — organized by the questions on-call asks at 3am, not by data source. The interview line: ‘a dashboard is a UI for incident questions.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you decide what belongs in logs vs metrics?",
     "back": "Metrics get the countable and the alertable — rates, latencies, saturations with bounded labels. Logs get the discrete and the contextual — errors with request IDs, decisions, anomalies to investigate. Traces get the cross-service journey. The interview line: 'if I'd page on it, it's a metric; if I'd investigate with it, it's a log.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you explain cardinality discipline in metrics?",
     "back": "Bounded label values only: high-cardinality labels (user IDs, request IDs) explode storage and cost. The interview line: ‘labels are dimensions, not databases.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What makes an alert actionable?",
     "back": "It fires on a symptom tied to an SLO, names the runbook, and pages only when a human must act now. The interview line: ‘an alert without an action is a notification.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "Putting user_id in a Prometheus label is dangerous because:",
     "back": "**Each unique value creates a new time series — cardinality explosion** — Unbounded label values × metrics = millions of series; memory and query costs follow."
    },
    {
     "front": "Deploy annotations on dashboards help because:",
     "back": "**They correlate behavior changes with releases — the first suspect in any regression** — ‘What changed?’ is the first incident question; annotations answer it at a glance."
    },
    {
     "front": "Alerting on symptoms means paging when:",
     "back": "**User-facing indicators (latency/errors) breach SLO — regardless of underlying cause** — Pages should mean user impact; cause metrics are for dashboards and diagnosis, not paging."
    },
    {
     "front": "The RED method suits:",
     "back": "**Request-driven services: rate, errors, duration** — RED describes service behavior from the requester’s view; USE describes resource internals."
    }
   ],
   "code": "IL-14",
   "crossLinks": [],
