   "handsOnLab": {
    "id": "sre-interview-prep--sre-monitoring--lab",
    "title": "Operator's brief: Monitoring, Tracing, and Alerting",
    "brief": "You will write the artifacts a real operator would produce for Monitoring, Tracing, and Alerting — incident review, SLO spec, and tradeoff analysis — because interviews probe the seams.",
    "steps": [
     "Write a blameless incident review for a realistic Monitoring, Tracing, and Alerting failure: timeline, impact in numbers, detection, response, root cause, action items with owners.",
     "Specify the SLOs: indicators, objectives, error budget, and what consumes the budget — with the math shown.",
     "Draw the architecture and annotate every tradeoff: what you chose, what you gave up, why it was worth it.",
     "Write the runbook excerpt: the alert, the first five diagnostic commands, the escalation path.",
     "Prepare the war story: a three-minute telling with numbers, your decisions, and what you'd do differently.",
     "Peer-review the brief as a hiring manager: which claims have evidence, which are buzzwords?"
    ],
    "deliverable": "incident-review.md, slo-spec.md (with math), architecture tradeoffs, runbook excerpt, and war-story script for Monitoring, Tracing, and Alerting.",
    "checkpoint": "Incident review has numbers and owned action items; SLO math is shown; every architecture choice names its tradeoff; war story runs three minutes."
   },
   "id": "sre-interview-prep--sre-monitoring",
   "mockTask": {
    "id": "sre-interview-prep--sre-monitoring--mock",
    "type": "choice",
    "q": "Deploy annotations on dashboards help because:",
    "choices": [
     "They’re required",
     "They look nice",
     "They speed queries",
     "They correlate behavior changes with releases — the first suspect in any regression"
    ],
    "answer": 3,
    "explain": "‘What changed?’ is the first incident question; annotations answer it at a glance."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **Metrics** — numbers over time: the basis of alerts and trends. 'Something is wrong.'\n- **Logs** — discrete events with context: the basis of diagnosis. 'Why it's wrong.'\n- **Traces** — request journeys across services: the basis of localization. 'Where it's wrong.'\n- **Four golden signals** — latency, traffic, errors, saturation: the minimum dashboard for any user-facing service.\n- **USE method** — Utilization, Saturation, Errors: the resource lens. 'Is the box healthy?'\n- **RED method** — Rate, Errors, Duration: the service lens. 'Is the service healthy?'\n- **Symptom alert** — pages on user impact (latency, error rate vs SLO). The only kind that should wake you.\n- **Cause metric** — a resource reading (disk 90%, CPU spike): belongs on a dashboard for diagnosis, not in a paging rule.\n- **Cardinality** — the number of unique time series: unbounded label values (like user IDs) multiply series and cost.\n- **Deploy annotation** — a marker on dashboards at release time: answers 'what changed?' at a glance."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Design the minimum viable observability for a sample API, on paper, in 30 minutes.\n1. **List the golden signals** for the service and pick the SLI behind each: success rate (errors), p99 latency (latency), requests/sec (traffic), worker queue depth (saturation).\n2. **Draw the dashboard hierarchy.** Golden signals up top; one drill-down layer per signal (signal → per-endpoint → per-instance); deploy annotations on every graph.\n3. **Write the alerting policy as rules.** Page only on symptoms: fast-burn alert on error budget, p99 latency vs SLO. Ticket the slow burns and the cause metrics (disk, CPU) — they inform, they don't wake.\n4. **Choose the lenses per component.** API service → RED. Database host → USE. Write one sentence for each choice.\n5. **Audit cardinality.** List every label you plan; strike any with unbounded values (user IDs, request IDs) — those belong in logs/traces, not metric labels.\n6. **Rehearse the narration.** Explain the whole design aloud in 5 minutes, ending with: 'metrics tell me something's wrong, traces show me where, logs tell me why.'"
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: an alerting-policy document plus dashboard sketch for a sample three-tier service.\n- **Dashboard sketch**: golden signals up top, drill-down paths, deploy annotations, time-range comparisons — organized by the questions on-call asks at 3am.\n- **Alerting policy table**: each alert named, its signal, its threshold or burn rate, and its route (page vs ticket) with a one-sentence justification.\n- **Tracing plan**: which service boundaries get instrumented first and what question the traces answer (latency attribution across services).\n- **Cardinality budget**: the label set with a written rule for what may never become a label.\n- **Present it as a 10-minute design review**, then record the two objections raised and your revised answers.\nReference shelf (module sources): **Prometheus Documentation** (https://prometheus.io/docs/) — metrics, PromQL, and alerting. **Grafana Documentation** (https://grafana.com/docs/) — dashboards and alerting. **OpenTelemetry Documentation** (https://opentelemetry.io/docs/) — vendor-neutral tracing, metrics, and logs."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and a paper dashboard is not a production monitoring system. Real alert tuning takes weeks of paging history; your first thresholds will be wrong, and that's normal — the module teaches the structure (symptoms page, causes inform) that makes tuning converge. Vendor specifics follow vendor documentation, which changes; verify current PromQL, Grafana, and OpenTelemetry syntax against the linked sources as of their retrieval dates."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Putting user_id in a Prometheus label is dangerous because:",
     "body": "**Problem.** Putting user_id in a Prometheus label is dangerous because:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** Each unique value creates a new time series — cardinality explosion\n- **Why:** Unbounded label values × metrics = millions of series; memory and query costs follow.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Unbounded label values × metrics = millions of series; memory and query costs follow.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In Monitoring, Tracing, and Alerting:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts Monitoring, Tracing, and Alerting interviews probe."
    }
   ],
