  {
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "How do you forecast demand for capacity planning?",
     "back": "Historical growth rates plus known events (launches, seasonality, marketing), expressed as ranges not points, translated through the capacity model (requests → resources). The interview line: ‘forecasts are ranges with assumptions, reviewed on a cadence.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "k6 vs JMeter — how do you compare them?",
     "back": "k6: JS-scripted, developer-friendly, great for CI-integrated load tests with thresholds as gates. JMeter: GUI-driven, mature, huge protocol support, heavier. The interview line: ‘k6 for code-reviewed load tests in the pipeline; JMeter where the team already lives in it.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "HPA vs VPA?",
     "back": "HPA: more pods when load rises (horizontal — the cloud-native default). VPA: bigger pods (vertical — for workloads that can’t scale out, needs restarts). The interview caveat: autoscaling isn’t instant — cold starts and scaling lag mean you still need headroom."
    },
    {
     "front": "What’s the utilization wall?",
     "back": "Latency degrades nonlinearly as utilization approaches 100% (queueing theory): a system at 90%+ has no headroom for bursts — the ‘wall’ where small load increases cause large latency spikes. The interview line: ‘I plan headroom against the wall, not against the average.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you use Little’s Law in capacity planning?",
     "back": "L = λW: average items in the system equals arrival rate times average time in system. It turns a latency SLO into a concurrency requirement — and a concurrency limit into a queueing prediction. The interview line: ‘Little’s Law converts between the rate I provision for and the latency I promise.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What do you load-test before a launch, and what makes the test a gate?",
     "back": "The forecast peak (at 1.5× for margin), a soak run for leaks, and a spike run for autoscaling lag — each with pass/fail thresholds written before the test runs. A threshold decided after the results is decoration; decided before, it's a gate. The interview line: 'the load test is a hypothesis test on my capacity model.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you discuss headroom in capacity planning?",
     "back": "Provision for peak plus margin (the forecast at 1.5×), because the utilization wall punishes 90%+ — latency degrades nonlinearly. The interview line: ‘headroom is the price of the p99.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What do interviewers probe in autoscaling lag?",
     "back": "Metrics delay plus pod-startup time means autoscaling reacts late to spikes — pre-warming and an over-provisioned buffer cover the gap. The interview line: ‘autoscaling is reactive; the spike doesn’t wait.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "Spot instances fit batch workloads because:",
     "back": "**Interruption-tolerant work buys spare capacity at deep discounts** — Price follows preemptibility; batch work that checkpoints doesn’t care."
    },
    {
     "front": "Eager loading fixes N+1 by:",
     "back": "**Fetching related rows in bulk (JOIN or batched query) instead of one query per parent** — One bulk fetch replaces N per-row queries; round trips collapse from N+1 to ~2."
    },
    {
     "front": "Running a service at 95% CPU is risky because:",
     "back": "**Queueing effects make latency spike nonlinearly near saturation — no burst headroom** — Near 100% utilization, tiny load increases produce huge queueing delays."
    },
    {
     "front": "Horizontal Pod Autoscaler reacts to load by:",
     "back": "**Adding more pod replicas** — HPA scales replica count on metrics; VPA resizes individual pods."
    }
   ],
