     "id": "sre-interview-prep--sre-capacity--drill-recall",
     "title": "Recall sprint: Capacity Planning and Scaling",
     "items": [
      {
       "prompt": "From memory, in one or two sentences: How do you use Little’s Law in capacity planning?",
       "answer": "L = λW: average items in the system equals arrival rate times average time in system. It turns a latency SLO into a concurrency requirement — and a concurrency limit into a queueing prediction. The interview line: ‘Little’s Law converts between the rate I provision for and the latency I promise.’",
       "explain": "Core recall for this module. If this wasn't fluent, re-read the 'Concepts' note and retry until it is."
      },
      {
       "prompt": "From memory, in one or two sentences: What do you load-test before a launch, and what makes the test a gate?",
       "answer": "The forecast peak (at 1.5× for margin), a soak run for leaks, and a spike run for autoscaling lag — each with pass/fail thresholds written before the test runs. A threshold decided after the results is decoration; decided before, it's a gate. The interview line: 'the load test is a hypothesis test on my capacity model.'",
       "explain": "Core recall for this module. If this wasn't fluent, re-read the 'Concepts' note and retry until it is."
      },
