   },
   "id": "sre-interview-prep--sre-capacity",
   "mockTask": {
    "id": "sre-interview-prep--sre-capacity--mock",
    "type": "choice",
    "q": "Eager loading fixes N+1 by:",
    "choices": [
     "Using NoSQL",
     "Fetching related rows in bulk (JOIN or batched query) instead of one query per parent",
     "Caching the database",
     "Adding more indexes"
    ],
    "answer": 1,
    "explain": "One bulk fetch replaces N per-row queries; round trips collapse from N+1 to ~2."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **Headroom** — spare capacity held deliberately above forecast peak: the margin that absorbs bursts and failures.\n- **N+1** — provision N+1 units for a load needing N: one unit can fail or drain without user impact.\n- **Utilization wall** — queueing theory's warning: latency degrades nonlinearly as utilization nears 100%; the wall is where small load increases cause large latency spikes.\n- **Horizontal scaling** — more units (HPA adds pod replicas): the cloud-native default.\n- **Vertical scaling** — bigger units (VPA resizes pods): for workloads that can't scale out; needs restarts.\n- **Autoscaling lag** — scaling isn't instant: cold starts and metric delays mean you still need headroom.\n- **Load test** — synthetic demand proving the capacity model: k6 for code-reviewed, CI-integrated tests with thresholds as gates.\n- **Spot/preemptible capacity** — spare compute at deep discounts for interruption-tolerant batch work that checkpoints.\n- **N+1 query problem** — the database cousin: N parents fetched, then one query per parent's children; fixed by eager loading (JOIN or batched query)."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Work this capacity estimate with stated assumptions — the interview grades the method, not the numbers.\n1. **State the forecast as a range.** 'Peak 8,000–12,000 requests/sec during the holiday week, based on last year's growth plus the marketing launch.' Ranges with assumptions, reviewed on a cadence.\n2. **Measure per-unit capacity.** From a load test: one instance sustains 500 rps at p99 < 200 ms. Write it down — measured, not guessed.\n3. **Divide, then add headroom.** 12,000 ÷ 500 = 24 instances at 100% — which is the wall. Target 65% utilization: 24 ÷ 0.65 ≈ 37 instances.\n4. **Apply N+1.** 37 + 1 = 38 instances, so one can drain for deploy or fail outright without breaching the SLO.\n5. **Price the headroom.** Those ~14 extra instances are the cost of burst absorption and failure tolerance — narrate it: 'headroom is insurance with a line item.'\n6. **Name the verification.** A k6 test at 1.5× forecast peak with thresholds as CI gates, re-run before every launch — the model is a hypothesis until the test confirms it."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: a one-page capacity plan for a sample API facing a 3× holiday traffic spike.\n- **Forecast**: a demand range with stated assumptions (growth rate, events, seasonality).\n- **The math**: per-unit measured capacity, utilization target (away from the wall), headroom, and the N+1 count — shown the way the Worked estimation shows it.\n- **Scaling policy**: HPA vs VPA choice with the autoscaling-lag caveat, and what stays statically provisioned.\n- **Load-test plan**: k6 scenarios with pass/fail thresholds as gates, and what each scenario proves.\n- **Cost note**: the price of the headroom and the cheaper alternative (spot/preemptible for the batch tier) with its tradeoff.\n- **Defend it in 10 minutes aloud**, ending with the line: 'I plan headroom against the wall, not against the average.'\nReference shelf (module sources): **Google SRE Books** (https://sre.google/) — capacity planning chapters from the source. **Kubernetes Documentation** (https://kubernetes.io/docs/) — HPA/VPA and workload references."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and worked estimates use assumed numbers to teach method. Real capacity numbers come from your system's load tests, not from this module — never quote the example figures as facts about any real service. The transferable skill is the structure: forecast as ranges, measure per-unit capacity, hold headroom off the wall, verify with tests, and narrate the cost."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Spot instances fit batch workloads because:",
     "body": "**Problem.** Spot instances fit batch workloads because:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** Interruption-tolerant work buys spare capacity at deep discounts\n- **Why:** Price follows preemptibility; batch work that checkpoints doesn’t care.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Price follows preemptibility; batch work that checkpoints doesn’t care.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In Capacity Planning and Scaling:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts Capacity Planning and Scaling interviews probe."
    }
   ],
