  {
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "How do you design for multi-region in an interview?",
     "back": "Active-passive (one serves, one stands by) vs active-active (both serve — needs conflict resolution); data replication with RPO/RTO stated; DNS or anycast failover; and the honest cost: complexity and consistency tradeoffs multiply. The interview line: ‘multi-region is a business decision with an engineering price.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What is backpressure, and where do you put it?",
     "back": "When consumers are slower than producers, pressure must go somewhere: bounded queues that block/shed, reactive streams with demand signaling, or Kafka consumer-lag alerting. The interview point: an unbounded queue is a deferred outage."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do caching layers behave under failure?",
     "back": "Cache stampedes on cold start, thundering herd on hot-key expiry, and the deadly combo: cache down → DB overload → cascading failure. Defenses: request coalescing, staggered TTLs, circuit breakers, and graceful degradation (serve stale while revalidating)."
    },
    {
     "front": "What’s load shedding, and when do you use it?",
     "back": "Deliberately dropping low-priority work to protect the core: priority queues, shedding non-critical traffic at the edge, degrading features gracefully. The interview line: ‘shedding is a choice — uncontrolled collapse is not.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What’s the standard system-design walkthrough?",
     "back": "Requirements → estimation → API → data model → happy-path design → scaling/bottlenecks → failure modes → tradeoffs summary. Drive with questions, think aloud, and treat the interviewer as a collaborator, not an examiner."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you defend a caching choice in a design round?",
     "back": "Name what you cache and why, the invalidation story, and the failure story: stampede on cold start, thundering herd on hot-key expiry, and what happens when the cache itself dies (stale-while-revalidate, circuit breaker). The interview line: 'caching is a tradeoff with an invalidation plan, not a speedup.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you design a health-check strategy in a design round?",
     "back": "Layered: liveness (restart me) vs readiness (don’t send traffic yet), with dependency checks that fail fast. The interview line: ‘health checks are the system’s honesty about itself.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What do interviewers probe in graceful degradation?",
     "back": "Defined fallback tiers: full → reduced (cached/stale) → static — each with its trigger condition. The interview line: ‘degradation designed beats degradation discovered.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "In a design round, the interviewer is best treated as:",
     "back": "**A collaborator — ask questions, incorporate feedback, design together** — Design rounds simulate real design work: collaborative, iterative, tradeoff-driven."
    },
    {
     "front": "Load shedding differs from a cascading failure in that it’s:",
     "back": "**Deliberate and prioritized — you choose what degrades instead of everything failing** — Controlled degradation preserves the critical path; cascades take everything down."
    },
    {
     "front": "Serving stale cache during an origin outage is:",
     "back": "**Graceful degradation — slightly old data beats no data** — Stale-while-revalidate as a failure mode keeps the product usable while the origin recovers."
    },
    {
     "front": "A bounded queue with a drop-or-block policy implements:",
     "back": "**Backpressure — the producer feels the consumer’s slowness** — Bounded buffers propagate slowness upstream instead of growing memory until collapse."
    }
   ],
