     "Deliberate and prioritized — you choose what degrades instead of everything failing"
    ],
    "answer": 3,
    "explain": "Controlled degradation preserves the critical path; cascades take everything down."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **RPO (Recovery Point Objective)** — how much data loss is tolerable: bounds backup frequency.\n- **RTO (Recovery Time Objective)** — how fast recovery must complete: bounds downtime.\n- **Active-passive** — one region serves, one stands by: simpler failover, idle cost.\n- **Active-active** — both regions serve: no idle cost, but needs conflict resolution for writes.\n- **Backpressure** — when consumers lag producers, pressure must go somewhere: bounded queues that block or shed, so slowness propagates instead of memory growing until collapse.\n- **Load shedding** — deliberately dropping low-priority work to protect the core: a choice, unlike uncontrolled collapse.\n- **Cache stampede / thundering herd** — cold-start or hot-key-expiry floods that hit the origin at once; defenses include request coalescing and staggered TTLs.\n- **Circuit breaker** — fails fast to a degraded path when a dependency is down, instead of piling up doomed requests.\n- **Graceful degradation** — serving stale or reduced functionality during an outage: slightly old data beats no data."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Run a 45-minute design rep on 'design a multi-region web service', with a timer.\n1. **Requirements (5 min).** Ask: users, read/write ratio, latency target, consistency needs. Write RPO/RTO as the business answers them.\n2. **Estimation (5 min).** Back-of-envelope: requests/sec, storage, bandwidth. Show the arithmetic; say the assumptions.\n3. **Happy path (10 min).** Draw: DNS/anycast → regions → LB → app → cache → DB. Label every arrow with what flows.\n4. **Reliability pass (10 min).** For each component: what breaks, what the blast radius is, the mitigation — active-passive vs active-active with the cost stated, queue backpressure, cache failure story, load-shedding policy.\n5. **Bottlenecks (10 min).** Walk the request path asking 'what breaks first at 10×?' and apply the right lever: shard, cache, or queue.\n6. **Tradeoffs summary (5 min).** Three sentences: what you optimized for, what you paid, when you'd choose differently. End by inviting the follow-up."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: six one-page design briefs — the portfolio you walk an interviewer through.\n- **Multi-region web service**, **HA database**, **queued pipeline with backpressure**, **caching layer with a failure story**, **rate limiting + load shedding**, and **one of your choosing**.\n- Each brief: requirements, estimation, the 2–3 key decisions, the scaling story, the failure modes with mitigations, and the cost-vs-reliability tradeoff stated honestly.\n- **Rehearse each aloud in 15 minutes**, then write the hardest follow-up question you can imagine and answer it in writing.\n- Keep the briefs to one page each: the constraint forces the prioritization that interviews grade.\nReference shelf (module sources): **Google SRE Books** (https://sre.google/) — reliability design chapters. **AWS Documentation** (https://docs.aws.amazon.com/) — service primitives for the designs."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and whiteboard designs are not production architectures. Real multi-region systems carry consistency, cost, and operational burdens that a 45-minute rep compresses away; never present a rep as a shipped design. What the reps train is the interview's actual skill: structured reasoning under ambiguity, defended tradeoffs, and the honesty to say 'multi-region is a business decision with an engineering price.'"
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: In a design round, the interviewer is best treated as:",
     "body": "**Problem.** In a design round, the interviewer is best treated as:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** A collaborator — ask questions, incorporate feedback, design together\n- **Why:** Design rounds simulate real design work: collaborative, iterative, tradeoff-driven.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Design rounds simulate real design work: collaborative, iterative, tradeoff-driven.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In SRE System Design Reps:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts SRE System Design Reps interviews probe."
    }
   ],
