   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "How do you prep for SRE mock rounds (incident, metrics/SLO, system design)?",
     "back": "Incident: run the lifecycle aloud (declare, mitigate first, comms cadence). Metrics/SLO: define SLIs, do the budget math, name burn-rate alerting. Design: requirements → estimation → HA design → failure modes. The interview line: ‘each round has a shape — I rehearse the shape.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do behavioral rounds differ for SREs?",
     "back": "They probe ownership under pressure: on-call stories, incident decisions, disagreements about risk, and learning from outages — all STAR, all blameless. The interview line: ‘SRE behavioral rounds grade how I act when the pager fires.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you score yourself against an interview rubric?",
     "back": "Fixed axes per round type: correctness, complexity analysis, communication, time management, tradeoffs named. Score honestly after every mock; the rubric turns feelings into data. The interview line: ‘I grade myself the way they’ll grade me.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What’s the final two-week taper?",
     "back": "Stop learning new topics; redrill weak patterns from your debrief log, run full timed mocks, fix sleep, and rehearse your story bank aloud. The taper converts knowledge into performance — cramming new material now only adds noise."
    },
    {
     "front": "The interviewer asks you to go deep on 'Mock incident round'. What answer structure makes you sound senior?",
     "back": "Three beats: (1) one-sentence definition, (2) how it works mechanically, (3) when it fits and its key tradeoff — then stop and invite the follow-up. Structure is what separates a senior answer from a recitation."
    },
    {
     "front": "What's on your 48-hour pre-interview checklist?",
     "back": "Logistics confirmed, sleep protected, story bank rehearsed aloud, the two weakest round shapes redrilled once more — and explicitly: no new topics. The checklist converts preparation into performance; cramming converts it into noise. The interview line: 'the last 48 hours are about the performer, not the material.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you prep the incident round specifically?",
     "back": "Run the lifecycle aloud on a past outage: declare, mitigate first, comms cadence — blameless language throughout. The interview line: ‘the incident round grades command, not debugging.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What does the SRE rubric’s ‘judgment under pressure’ axis mean?",
     "back": "Decisions with incomplete data: when you mitigated vs kept digging, when you escalated, what you communicated. The interview line: ‘judgment is visible in the tradeoffs you name.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "The taper period’s rule is:",
     "back": "**No new material — redrill weak spots, mock timed, rest** — Performance comes from consolidation and recovery, not last-minute breadth."
    },
    {
     "front": "Rubric scoring beats gut feeling because:",
     "back": "**It makes weaknesses comparable across mocks — triage needs data** — You can’t triage what you haven’t measured."
    },
    {
     "front": "An SRE behavioral answer should emphasize:",
     "back": "**Decisions under pressure, communication, and systemic learning** — Reliability is a team sport; the graded behaviors are judgment and coordination."
    },
    {
     "front": "Rehearsing each round’s ‘shape’ helps because:",
     "back": "**Interviews grade method under pressure — the shape is the method, made automatic** — Automatic structure frees mental budget for the novel parts of the problem."
    }
   ],
