   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **Mock circuit** — the full simulated loop: incident round, metrics/SLO round, system-design round, behavioral round, run back-to-back under time.\n- **Round shape** — the fixed structure each round type follows: rehearse the shape and the method becomes automatic under pressure.\n- **Interview rubric** — fixed scoring axes per round (correctness, complexity analysis, communication, time management, tradeoffs named): turns feelings into triage data.\n- **Debrief log** — the written record after every mock: what leaked, its category, the redrill plan. Improvement lives here, not in doing more mocks.\n- **Weakness-targeting week** — the week between simulation and the real loop, spent only on the lowest rubric scores: raise the floor, not the ceiling.\n- **Taper** — the final two weeks: no new material, redrill weak patterns, full timed mocks, fix sleep, rehearse the story bank aloud.\n- **48-hour checklist** — logistics, rest, rehearsed stories, no new topics: the last two days convert preparation into performance."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Run one full mock circuit this week — schedule it like a real loop day.\n1. **Book the day.** Four 45-minute rounds with 15-minute breaks: incident, metrics/SLO, system design, behavioral. A partner plays interviewer; trade roles next time.\n2. **Set the rules.** Timed, no pausing, think aloud the whole way. The interviewer scores against the rubric during the round, not after.\n3. **Run the incident round.** Declare, mitigate first, comms cadence — the shape from the incident module, narrated.\n4. **Run the metrics round.** Define SLIs, do the budget math, name the burn-rate alerts — the shape from the SLO module.\n5. **Run the design round.** Requirements → estimation → HA design → failure modes — the shape from the design module.\n6. **Debrief in writing within the hour.** Score each axis, name the lowest two, and write the weakness-targeting plan: what you'll redrill, and how you'll verify. Then rest — the taper has started."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: a scored mock-loop dossier.\n- **The schedule**: your four-round loop day with times, roles, and the rubric each round is scored against.\n- **Score sheets**: one per round, every axis scored honestly — correctness, communication, time management, tradeoffs named.\n- **The debrief**: the two lowest-scoring axes, the category of each miss (not just the instance), and a one-week redrill plan with verification.\n- **The 48-hour checklist**: your personal version — logistics, sleep, story bank, what you will explicitly not study.\n- **Reflection**: one paragraph on which round shape still isn't automatic, and the drill that will make it so.\nReference shelf (module sources): **Google SRE Books** (https://sre.google/) — the reliability material the mocks draw on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and mock scores are not predictions of real outcomes. Partners are kinder than hiring panels, and home whiteboards lack loop-day adrenaline; treat scores as triage data for your preparation, not as forecasts. The honest value of the circuit is narrower and real: automatic round shapes, a debrief habit, and a weakness-targeting plan — which is exactly what converts knowledge into interview performance."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: The taper period’s rule is:",
     "body": "**Problem.** The taper period’s rule is:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** No new material — redrill weak spots, mock timed, rest\n- **Why:** Performance comes from consolidation and recovery, not last-minute breadth.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Performance comes from consolidation and recovery, not last-minute breadth.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In SRE Mock Circuit:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts SRE Mock Circuit interviews probe."
    }
