   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "What’s the case for trunk-based development?",
     "back": "Small, frequent merges to main behind feature flags: integration pain drops with merge frequency, main stays releasable, and long-lived branch divergence dies. The interview line: ‘branches are inventory — trunk-based keeps inventory near zero.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What belongs in CI stages, and in what order?",
     "back": "Fast-first: lint and unit tests (minutes), then build, integration tests, security scans, artifact publish. The interview line: ‘fail fast and cheap — a 40-minute pipeline that fails at minute 39 teaches nothing quickly.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "Why do artifacts and registries matter?",
     "back": "Build once, promote the same immutable artifact through environments — registries (container/image, package) make ‘what’s running where’ answerable. The interview line: ‘if you rebuild per environment, you didn’t test what you shipped.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "Blue-green vs canary vs rolling?",
     "back": "Blue-green: two full environments, instant cutover and rollback — costs double capacity. Canary: new version to a small % first, promote on metrics — safest for risky changes. Rolling: gradual replacement — simplest, slowest rollback. Choose by blast-radius tolerance."
    },
    {
     "front": "What does pipeline-as-code buy you?",
     "back": "The pipeline is versioned, reviewed, and reproducible like the app: workflow files in the repo, changes via PR, history in git. The interview line: ‘if the pipeline isn’t in version control, it’s folklore.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you keep a 40-minute pipeline from failing at minute 39?",
     "back": "Order stages fail-fast: lint and unit tests in the first minutes, then build, integration, scans, publish. Fast, cheap failures first; slow, expensive verification only for code that earned it. The interview line: 'fail fast and cheap — a 40-minute pipeline that fails at minute 39 teaches nothing quickly.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you discuss deployment freezes in an interview?",
     "back": "Change moratoriums around critical periods (holidays, launches) with a defined exception process — reliability as policy. The interview line: ‘freezes are the error budget made calendar.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What do interviewers probe in artifact promotion?",
     "back": "The same immutable artifact moves dev → staging → prod; only config changes per environment. The interview line: ‘promote the artifact, not a rebuild.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "The rollback-vs-fix-forward decision hinges on:",
     "back": "**Blast radius and diagnosis confidence — big blast or uncertain cause means roll back** — Uncertain cause + user impact = roll back first, understand second."
    },
    {
     "front": "Storing CI configuration in the repo enables:",
     "back": "**Review, versioning, and reproducibility of the pipeline itself** — Pipeline changes get the same rigor as code changes: PRs, history, rollback."
    },
    {
     "front": "Canary deployment’s defining feature is:",
     "back": "**Releasing to a small subset first and promoting based on observed metrics** — Progressive exposure with metric gates: problems hit 1% before 100%."
    },
    {
     "front": "Promoting the same immutable artifact through environments ensures:",
     "back": "**What you tested is what you ship — no rebuild skew between staging and prod** — Rebuilding per environment invalidates testing; immutable artifacts preserve it."
    }
   ],
   "code": "IL-14",
