   "handsOnLab": {
    "id": "devops-interview-prep--devops-ci-cd--lab",
    "title": "Operator's brief: CI/CD Systems",
    "brief": "You will write the artifacts a real operator would produce for CI/CD Systems — incident review, SLO spec, and tradeoff analysis — because interviews probe the seams.",
    "steps": [
     "Write a blameless incident review for a realistic CI/CD Systems failure: timeline, impact in numbers, detection, response, root cause, action items with owners.",
     "Specify the SLOs: indicators, objectives, error budget, and what consumes the budget — with the math shown.",
     "Draw the architecture and annotate every tradeoff: what you chose, what you gave up, why it was worth it.",
     "Write the runbook excerpt: the alert, the first five diagnostic commands, the escalation path.",
     "Prepare the war story: a three-minute telling with numbers, your decisions, and what you'd do differently.",
     "Peer-review the brief as a hiring manager: which claims have evidence, which are buzzwords?"
    ],
    "deliverable": "incident-review.md, slo-spec.md (with math), architecture tradeoffs, runbook excerpt, and war-story script for CI/CD Systems.",
    "checkpoint": "Incident review has numbers and owned action items; SLO math is shown; every architecture choice names its tradeoff; war story runs three minutes."
   },
   "id": "devops-interview-prep--devops-ci-cd",
   "mockTask": {
    "id": "devops-interview-prep--devops-ci-cd--mock",
    "type": "choice",
    "q": "Storing CI configuration in the repo enables:",
    "choices": [
     "Review, versioning, and reproducibility of the pipeline itself",
     "More stages",
     "Faster builds",
     "Parallel jobs"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explain": "Pipeline changes get the same rigor as code changes: PRs, history, rollback."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **Trunk-based development** — small, frequent merges to main behind feature flags: branches are inventory, and this keeps inventory near zero.\n- **CI stages** — lint → unit tests → build → integration tests → security scans → artifact publish: fast-first so failures surface in minutes, not at minute 39.\n- **Immutable artifact** — build once, promote the same artifact through environments: what you tested is what you ship.\n- **Registry** — where immutable artifacts live (container/image, package): makes 'what's running where' answerable.\n- **Blue-green** — two full environments, instant cutover and rollback: costs double capacity.\n- **Canary** — new version to a small percentage first, promoted on observed metrics: safest for risky changes.\n- **Rolling** — gradual replacement of old with new: simplest, slowest rollback.\n- **Pipeline-as-code** — the pipeline versioned, reviewed, and reproducible like the app: if it isn't in version control, it's folklore.\n- **Rollback vs fix-forward** — the decision hinges on blast radius and diagnosis confidence: big blast or uncertain cause means roll back."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Design a CI/CD pipeline for a sample containerized API, on paper, in 30 minutes.\n1. **Pick the branching model.** Trunk-based: short-lived branches, merge to main daily, feature flags for incomplete work. Write the one-sentence case for it.\n2. **Order the CI stages fail-fast.** Lint + unit tests (minutes) → build → integration tests → security scans → artifact publish. Mark the expected duration of each stage.\n3. **Define the artifact.** One immutable image per commit, tagged with the git SHA; floating tags never deploy to prod. State where it's stored.\n4. **Choose the deploy strategy per environment.** Rolling to dev, canary (5% → 50% → 100% on metrics) to prod. Justify by blast-radius tolerance.\n5. **Write the rollback plan.** Automated rollback trigger (error-rate or latency SLO breach within 10 minutes of deploy), plus the manual command. State the rollback-vs-fix-forward rule.\n6. **Narrate it in 5 minutes.** Stages, gates, strategy, rollback — ending with: 'if the pipeline isn't in version control, it's folklore.'"
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: a pipeline design document for a sample service, written as a design-review artifact.\n- **Pipeline diagram**: stages in order with durations, gates, and owners; the artifact and its tagging scheme.\n- **Strategy matrix**: blue-green vs canary vs rolling per environment, with the blast-radius reasoning.\n- **Rollback runbook**: triggers, commands, and the decision rule — when you roll back vs fix forward.\n- **Pipeline-as-code**: the workflow file layout in the repo and how changes to it are reviewed.\n- **Present it as a 10-minute design round**, then answer: 'your canary metrics look fine but users complain — what now?' in writing.\nReference shelf (module sources): **GitHub Actions Documentation** (https://docs.github.com/en/actions) — pipeline-as-code reference. **Jenkins Documentation** (https://jenkins.io/doc/) — the classic CI server most interview pipelines were built on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and a paper pipeline is not a production delivery system. Real pipelines accumulate years of edge cases, flaky tests, and 3am lessons that no design doc reproduces. What transfers is the structure: fail-fast stages, immutable artifacts, strategy by blast radius, and a rollback story told before anyone asks. Tool specifics follow vendor documentation, which changes; verify current GitHub Actions and Jenkins syntax against the linked sources as of their retrieval dates."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: The rollback-vs-fix-forward decision hinges on:",
     "body": "**Problem.** The rollback-vs-fix-forward decision hinges on:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** Blast radius and diagnosis confidence — big blast or uncertain cause means roll back\n- **Why:** Uncertain cause + user impact = roll back first, understand second.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Uncertain cause + user impact = roll back first, understand second.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In CI/CD Systems:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts CI/CD Systems interviews probe."
    }
   ],
