   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "Declarative vs imperative infrastructure?",
     "back": "Declarative: state the desired end state, the tool converges (Terraform, K8s manifests) — idempotent and auditable. Imperative: script the steps — flexible but order-dependent and drift-prone. The interview line: ‘I declare the destination; the tool figures out the route.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you explain Terraform’s workflow?",
     "back": "Write declarative HCL, plan to preview the diff, apply to converge. State tracks reality (remote backend, locking!); modules compose reusable units. The interview traps: state drift, and why plan-before-apply is non-negotiable."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you discuss Terraform state tradeoffs?",
     "back": "Remote state with locking (no local state files on a team), state per environment to bound blast radius, and never hand-editing state — import or taint instead. The tradeoff: finer state splits mean safer applies but more orchestration. The interview line: ‘state is the map of reality; guard it like production data.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you structure Terraform modules and composition?",
     "back": "Small composable modules (one concern each: network, compute, data), composed in environment roots with explicit inputs — no deep nesting. The interview line: ‘modules are functions; roots are the programs that call them.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What is infrastructure drift, and how do you fight it?",
     "back": "Reality diverging from declared config (manual console clicks). Detection: scheduled plan runs that alert on diffs; prevention: make the console read-only, everything through code. The interview line: ‘drift is how outages hide.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "Why is 'plan-before-apply' non-negotiable?",
     "back": "The plan is the diff between declared and actual — it's the review, the blast-radius estimate, and the last chance to catch a typo that deletes the database. Applying blind is clicking 'yes' on a dialog you didn't read. The interview line: 'the plan is the change's résumé; I read résumés before hiring.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you discuss secret handling in IaC?",
     "back": "Never in state files or repos in plaintext: secret managers with dynamic references, state encrypted at rest. The interview line: ‘the state file is a secret — treat it like one.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What makes a Terraform module reusable?",
     "back": "Small scope, documented inputs and outputs, no hardcoded environment values — composition in environment roots. The interview line: ‘modules are libraries; roots are applications.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "Enforcing policy at plan time (vs after deploy) is better because:",
     "back": "**Violations are blocked before they exist — prevention beats detection** — Shift-left for governance: the bad change never reaches the cloud."
    },
    {
     "front": "The primary defense against drift is:",
     "back": "**Everything through code + scheduled plan/detect cycles** — If all changes flow through IaC, reality can’t silently diverge."
    },
    {
     "front": "Deep module nesting is an anti-pattern because:",
     "back": "**Inputs tunnel through layers opaquely — debugging and reuse both suffer** — Flat composition keeps the dependency graph legible; nesting hides it."
    },
    {
     "front": "React context is a poor state manager for high-churn state because:",
     "back": "**Every consumer re-renders on any context value change** — Context has no selectors: one value change re-renders all consumers regardless of what they use."
    }
   ],
   "code": "IL-14",
