   "handsOnLab": {
    "id": "devops-interview-prep--devops-iac--lab",
    "title": "Operator's brief: Infrastructure as Code Patterns",
    "brief": "You will write the artifacts a real operator would produce for Infrastructure as Code Patterns — incident review, SLO spec, and tradeoff analysis — because interviews probe the seams.",
    "steps": [
     "Write a blameless incident review for a realistic Infrastructure as Code Patterns failure: timeline, impact in numbers, detection, response, root cause, action items with owners.",
     "Specify the SLOs: indicators, objectives, error budget, and what consumes the budget — with the math shown.",
     "Draw the architecture and annotate every tradeoff: what you chose, what you gave up, why it was worth it.",
     "Write the runbook excerpt: the alert, the first five diagnostic commands, the escalation path.",
     "Prepare the war story: a three-minute telling with numbers, your decisions, and what you'd do differently.",
     "Peer-review the brief as a hiring manager: which claims have evidence, which are buzzwords?"
    ],
    "deliverable": "incident-review.md, slo-spec.md (with math), architecture tradeoffs, runbook excerpt, and war-story script for Infrastructure as Code Patterns.",
    "checkpoint": "Incident review has numbers and owned action items; SLO math is shown; every architecture choice names its tradeoff; war story runs three minutes."
   },
   "id": "devops-interview-prep--devops-iac",
   "mockTask": {
    "id": "devops-interview-prep--devops-iac--mock",
    "type": "choice",
    "q": "The primary defense against drift is:",
    "choices": [
     "Everything through code + scheduled plan/detect cycles",
     "Bigger teams",
     "Manual audits",
     "More dashboards"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explain": "If all changes flow through IaC, reality can’t silently diverge."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **Declarative** — state the desired end state; the tool converges (Terraform, Kubernetes manifests): idempotent and auditable.\n- **Imperative** — script the steps: flexible but order-dependent and drift-prone.\n- **Plan / apply** — Terraform's loop: preview the diff, then converge. Plan-before-apply is non-negotiable.\n- **State** — Terraform's record of reality: lives in a remote backend with locking, never on a laptop.\n- **Module** — a reusable unit of infrastructure (one concern each: network, compute, data): modules are functions, environment roots are the programs that call them.\n- **Drift** — reality diverging from declared config (the manual console click): detected by scheduled plan runs, prevented by making the console read-only.\n- **Policy-as-code** — governance enforced at plan time: violations are blocked before they exist, because prevention beats detection."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Walk a Terraform change end to end on paper — this is the whiteboard exercise.\n1. **Write the intent.** A small module: one VPC with two subnets, inputs for CIDR and environment, outputs for subnet IDs. Flat composition — no nesting.\n2. **Run plan mentally.** List what the plan would show: 3 resources to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy. Say why you read the plan before anything else.\n3. **Apply with state discipline.** Remote backend, state locking enabled: two engineers applying at once can't corrupt state. Say what breaks without locking.\n4. **Simulate drift.** Someone clicked in the console and changed a subnet. Your scheduled plan run shows a diff — narrate the response: alert on the diff, revert through code, never click to fix a click.\n5. **Add policy-as-code.** A plan-time rule: no security group may open 0.0.0.0/0 to SSH. Violations fail the plan — prevention beats detection.\n6. **Close with the line.** 'I declare the destination; the tool figures out the route. Drift is how outages hide.'"
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: an IaC repository design for a sample two-environment setup (staging, prod).\n- **Layout**: modules (network, compute, data) plus environment roots that compose them with explicit inputs — flat, no deep nesting.\n- **State strategy**: remote backend per environment, locking on, and the rule for who may touch state directly (nobody, except break-glass).\n- **Workflow**: branch → plan in CI with the diff posted for review → apply on merge. Plan-before-apply, always.\n- **Drift program**: scheduled plan runs that alert on diffs, and the console policy (read-only for humans).\n- **One policy-as-code rule** with its justification, and the story of the outage it would have prevented.\n- **Defend it in 10 minutes**, ending with the state-management traps you'd warn a junior about.\nReference shelf (module sources): **Terraform Documentation** (https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/docs) — workflow, state, and module references."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and a paper Terraform design is not production infrastructure. Real state files carry years of imports, taints, and 3am applies that no exercise reproduces; the module teaches the discipline that keeps them manageable: remote state, locking, flat modules, everything through code. Provider and tool syntax changes; verify current Terraform behavior against the linked documentation as of its retrieval date."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Enforcing policy at plan time (vs after deploy) is better because:",
     "body": "**Problem.** Enforcing policy at plan time (vs after deploy) is better because:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** Violations are blocked before they exist — prevention beats detection\n- **Why:** Shift-left for governance: the bad change never reaches the cloud.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Shift-left for governance: the bad change never reaches the cloud.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In Infrastructure as Code Patterns:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts Infrastructure as Code Patterns interviews probe."
    }
   ],
