  {
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "How do Docker layers affect your Dockerfile?",
     "back": "Each instruction is a cached layer: order matters — put slow-changing steps (dependency install) before fast-changing ones (code copy) to maximize cache hits. Multi-stage builds keep runtime images small: build in one stage, ship only artifacts."
    },
    {
     "front": "What’s tagging discipline for container registries?",
     "back": "Immutable tags per build (git SHA), floating tags (latest) only for convenience — never deploy ‘latest’ to prod. The interview line: ‘mutable tags make ‘what’s running?’ unanswerable.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "The Kubernetes objects you must know cold?",
     "back": "Pod (smallest unit), Deployment (declarative replicas + rolling updates), Service (stable networking), Ingress (external HTTP routing), ConfigMap/Secret (config separation), PersistentVolume for state. The interview walk: ‘my app runs as a Deployment behind a Service, exposed via Ingress.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do Deployments, Services, and Ingress fit together?",
     "back": "Deployment: declares the desired pods and rolling-update strategy. Service: stable internal endpoint selecting those pods. Ingress: external HTTP routing (host/path → service). The interview line: ‘three layers: run it, find it, expose it.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you handle config vs secrets in Kubernetes?",
     "back": "ConfigMaps for non-sensitive config, Secrets for sensitive (base64-encoded, not encrypted by default — use external secret managers or encryption at rest for real protection). Mount as env or files; never bake into images. The interview line: ‘config travels with the deployment, not the image.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you shrink a 1.2 GB container image?",
     "back": "Multi-stage build (ship only runtime artifacts), minimal base image, dependencies before code copy for cache efficiency, and audit what's inside — build tools and caches don't sail to prod. Smaller images pull faster, scan faster, and shrink the attack surface. The interview line: 'the image should contain the app, not the workshop.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you discuss pod resource requests vs limits?",
     "back": "Requests: the scheduler’s guarantee (bin-packing basis). Limits: the kill threshold (OOMKilled). Set requests from measured p95, limits with headroom. The interview line: ‘requests are reservations; limits are the ceiling.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What do interviewers probe in liveness vs readiness probes?",
     "back": "Liveness: restart the stuck container. Readiness: hold traffic during startup or dependency outages. Wrong probe, cascading restarts. The interview line: ‘the probes are the orchestrator’s senses — calibrate them.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "Helm’s ‘values’ mechanism lets you:",
     "back": "**Deploy the same chart across environments with different configuration** — One chart, many values files: dev/staging/prod differ by values, not by forked manifests."
    },
    {
     "front": "Kubernetes Secrets are base64-encoded, which means:",
     "back": "**They’re obfuscated, not encrypted — anyone with API access can decode them** — Base64 is encoding, not encryption: etcd encryption or external secret managers provide real protection."
    },
    {
     "front": "An Ingress resource needs an Ingress controller because:",
     "back": "**Ingress is just config — the controller is the actual proxy implementing the routing** — The resource declares intent; the controller (nginx, traefik) does the work."
    },
    {
     "front": "A Kubernetes Service provides:",
     "back": "**Stable networking (DNS + load balancing) for a set of pods** — Pods are ephemeral; the Service gives them a stable virtual IP and DNS name."
    }
   ],
   "code": "IL-14",
