   "id": "devops-interview-prep--devops-containers",
   "mockTask": {
    "id": "devops-interview-prep--devops-containers--mock",
    "type": "choice",
    "q": "Kubernetes Secrets are base64-encoded, which means:",
    "choices": [
     "They’re encrypted",
     "They’re safe anywhere",
     "They’re compressed",
     "They’re obfuscated, not encrypted — anyone with API access can decode them"
    ],
    "answer": 3,
    "explain": "Base64 is encoding, not encryption: etcd encryption or external secret managers provide real protection."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **Image layer** — each Dockerfile instruction is a cached layer: order slow-changing steps (dependency install) before fast-changing ones (code copy) to maximize cache hits.\n- **Multi-stage build** — build in one stage, ship only artifacts in the final stage: keeps runtime images small.\n- **Tagging discipline** — immutable tags per build (git SHA); floating tags like 'latest' for convenience only, never deployed to prod.\n- **Pod** — Kubernetes' smallest unit: one or more tightly-coupled containers.\n- **Deployment** — declares desired pods with replica counts and the rolling-update strategy.\n- **Service** — stable networking (virtual IP + DNS) for a set of ephemeral pods.\n- **Ingress** — external HTTP routing (host/path → service): the resource declares intent, the controller (nginx, traefik) does the work.\n- **ConfigMap / Secret** — config separation: non-sensitive config vs sensitive values. Secrets are base64-encoded — obfuscated, not encrypted — so real protection means etcd encryption or an external secret manager.\n- **Helm values** — one chart, many values files: dev/staging/prod differ by values, not by forked manifests."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Containerize a sample app and put it on Kubernetes, on paper, in 30 minutes.\n1. **Write the Dockerfile.** Multi-stage: dependencies installed before code copy for cache hits, minimal final image, non-root user. Annotate each instruction with its layer-cache reasoning.\n2. **Set tagging.** Image tagged with the git SHA at build time; 'latest' never leaves the dev registry. Write the rule: 'mutable tags make what's-running unanswerable.'\n3. **Write the Deployment.** Replicas, image with SHA tag, resource requests/limits, liveness and readiness probes — and say what each probe protects against.\n4. **Add the Service and Ingress.** ClusterIP Service selecting the pods; Ingress with host/path rules; note that the controller is the actual proxy.\n5. **Separate config.** ConfigMap for non-sensitive settings, Secret for credentials — mounted, never baked into the image. Note the base64 caveat and the real protection.\n6. **Narrate the three layers.** 'Run it (Deployment), find it (Service), expose it (Ingress)' — then name where you'd put a Helm chart and why."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: a containerization-and-deploy package for a sample service, review-ready.\n- **Dockerfile** with layer-cache annotations and a written justification for the base image choice.\n- **Manifests or a Helm chart**: Deployment, Service, Ingress, ConfigMap, Secret references — one chart with values per environment if you choose Helm.\n- **Tagging and promotion policy**: SHA tags, how images move dev → staging → prod, and the rule against 'latest'.\n- **Failure notes**: what CrashLoopBackOff, ImagePullBackOff, and OOMKilled each mean for this app, and the first command you'd run for each.\n- **Walk an interviewer through it in 10 minutes**, from `docker build` to the Ingress serving traffic.\nReference shelf (module sources): **Docker Documentation** (https://docs.docker.com/) — image builds and registries. **Kubernetes Documentation** (https://kubernetes.io/docs/) — core objects, networking, failure modes."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and paper manifests are not a production cluster. Real clusters accumulate admission policies, network policies, and upgrade scars that no exercise covers; the module teaches the object model and the discipline (immutable tags, config outside images) that keep them operable. Kubernetes and Docker behavior changes across versions; verify current syntax against the linked documentation as of its retrieval date."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Helm’s ‘values’ mechanism lets you:",
     "body": "**Problem.** Helm’s ‘values’ mechanism lets you:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** Deploy the same chart across environments with different configuration\n- **Why:** One chart, many values files: dev/staging/prod differ by values, not by forked manifests.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *One chart, many values files: dev/staging/prod differ by values, not by forked manifests.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In Containers and Orchestration:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts Containers and Orchestration interviews probe."
    }
   ],
