  {
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "How do you read logs under pressure?",
     "back": "Filter ruthlessly: time window around the incident, the failing service first, errors before info, one clear signal at a time. Correlate with deploys and metric spikes. The interview line: ‘I narrow before I read — logs are guilty until filtered innocent.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you actually run five whys on a system?",
     "back": "Start from the symptom and ask ‘why’ iteratively, following evidence (logs, metrics, deploys) not hunches — until you reach a process/systemic cause with an action item. The discipline: every ‘why’ needs data, and the fifth why usually points at a missing guardrail."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you debug network issues methodically?",
     "back": "Layer by layer: DNS resolves? (dig/nslookup) TCP connects? (curl -v, telnet) TLS handshakes? (openssl s_client) HTTP responds? Each layer’s test isolates the fault — the interview line: ‘I bisect the stack instead of guessing.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What Kubernetes failure modes must you know?",
     "back": "CrashLoopBackOff (bad image/config, failing probes), ImagePullBackOff (registry/auth), OOMKilled (limits vs reality), Pending (no schedulable node/resources), probe misconfiguration (killing healthy pods). The interview line: ‘kubectl describe tells you the story; events are the narrator.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "Why do interviewers grade ‘thinking aloud’ in troubleshooting rounds?",
     "back": "Because silent debugging is unscoreable: narrating hypotheses, what each command tests, and what the output means lets them evaluate your method. The structure: hypothesis → test → observe → update, said out loud."
    },
    {
     "front": "What's your first move when the dashboard is red but nobody knows why?",
     "back": "Ask 'what changed?' — check deploy annotations and recent deploys before touching anything. Most production mysteries are deployments in disguise, and the answer is usually in the last hour's timeline. The interview line: 'I investigate the timeline before I investigate the system.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you discuss the ‘what changed?’ first move?",
     "back": "Deploys, config, traffic, and infra changes in the incident window — most production surprises are changes wearing a costume. The interview line: ‘the timeline of changes is the first log I read.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What makes a troubleshooting hypothesis good?",
     "back": "Falsifiable and cheap to test: one command that rules something out. The interview line: ‘debug by elimination, not by inspiration.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "Narrating while troubleshooting helps the interviewer assess:",
     "back": "**Your diagnostic method — hypotheses, tests, and how you update on evidence** — Method is what’s graded; narration makes the invisible visible."
    },
    {
     "front": "CrashLoopBackOff most often means:",
     "back": "**The container starts then exits — bad command, missing config, or failing startup probe** — The kubelet keeps retrying a container that can’t stay alive; describe/events reveal why it dies."
    },
    {
     "front": "curl -v failing at ‘Could not resolve host’ means:",
     "back": "**DNS failure — the name never became an IP** — Resolution happens before any connection: the fault is in DNS, not the server."
    },
    {
     "front": "Five whys stops being useful when:",
     "back": "**Answers become speculation without evidence — each why needs data** — Whys without evidence are storytelling; the method works when each level is grounded in telemetry."
    }
   ],
