   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **Hypothesis-driven diagnosis** — hypothesis → test → observe → update: the loop that makes debugging gradable when narrated.\n- **Five whys** — asking 'why' iteratively from the symptom, each answer grounded in evidence, until reaching a systemic cause with an action item.\n- **Layer bisection** — DNS resolves? TCP connects? TLS handshakes? HTTP responds? Each layer's test isolates the fault.\n- **CrashLoopBackOff** — the container starts then exits: bad command, missing config, or a failing startup probe. `kubectl describe` tells the story; events are the narrator.\n- **ImagePullBackOff** — the image can't be fetched: registry, tag, or auth problem.\n- **OOMKilled** — the container exceeded its memory limit: limits vs reality.\n- **Thinking aloud** — narrating hypotheses, what each command tests, and what the output means: silent debugging is unscoreable."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Run this 15-minute timed drill on a sample failure: 'the API returns 502s after a deploy.'\n1. **State the hypothesis set (1 min).** Bad deploy, downstream down, config missing. Rank by likelihood and cheapness to test.\n2. **Check what changed (2 min).** Deploy annotations, recent deploys — 'what changed?' is the first incident question.\n3. **Bisect the network (3 min).** `curl -v`: does DNS resolve? Does TCP connect? Does TLS handshake? Name which layer each failure implicates.\n4. **Read the pods (3 min).** `kubectl get pods`, then `describe` on the failing one: CrashLoopBackOff? OOMKilled? Read the events like a narrator.\n5. **Filter the logs (3 min).** Time window around the incident, the failing service first, errors before info — 'narrow before I read.'\n6. **Decide and narrate (3 min).** Mitigation first (rollback?), then the five-whys chain with evidence per 'why' — said out loud, the whole way."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: a troubleshooting drill pack — three timed scenarios with answer keys.\n- **Scenario A**: pods CrashLooping after a config change — the `kubectl describe` reading drill.\n- **Scenario B**: `curl -v` failing at 'Could not resolve host' — the DNS-layer bisection drill.\n- **Scenario C**: 502s after a deploy with healthy pods — the 'what changed' + downstream drill.\n- Each scenario: the symptom, the time box, the expected hypothesis-test-observe chain, and the narrated answer.\n- **Run all three with a partner timing you**, then write the debrief: where your narration broke down and the drill that fixes it.\nReference shelf (module sources): **Kubernetes Documentation** (https://kubernetes.io/docs/) — failure modes and kubectl references. **Grafana Documentation** (https://grafana.com/docs/) — dashboards used in the troubleshooting modules."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and drills are not production incidents. Real failures involve incomplete telemetry, misleading symptoms, and pressure that a 15-minute timer only gestures at. What transfers is the method: hypotheses ranked by cheapness, layer bisection, evidence per 'why', and narration throughout. Command syntax changes across tool versions; verify current kubectl, curl, and openssl usage against the linked documentation as of its retrieval date."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Narrating while troubleshooting helps the interviewer assess:",
     "body": "**Problem.** Narrating while troubleshooting helps the interviewer assess:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** Your diagnostic method — hypotheses, tests, and how you update on evidence\n- **Why:** Method is what’s graded; narration makes the invisible visible.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Method is what’s graded; narration makes the invisible visible.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In Troubleshooting Under Pressure:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts Troubleshooting Under Pressure interviews probe."
    }
   ],
