   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "What does a good platform-build scaffold include?",
     "back": "Repo layout (app + infra + pipeline), VPC via Terraform, cluster bootstrap scripted, CI pipeline, GitOps deploy — each piece documented as a deliverable. The interview line: ‘the scaffold is the platform’s first impression.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What is a ‘paved road’ in platform engineering?",
     "back": "The blessed, supported path: golden-path templates, standard pipelines, and defaults that make the right thing the easy thing. Teams can leave the road, but then they own the maintenance. The interview line: ‘a paved road is opinionated infrastructure with an escape hatch.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What is GitOps, concretely?",
     "back": "Git as the single source of truth for desired state; an agent (Argo CD/Flux) continuously reconciles the cluster to it. Pull-based, auditable, self-healing: drift gets reverted automatically, and every change is a git commit."
    },
    {
     "front": "Why is documentation a deliverable in platform work?",
     "back": "Because platforms have customers: runbooks, ADRs, and onboarding docs are the API of the platform team. The interview line: ‘undocumented automation is a ticket queue with extra steps.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "The interviewer asks you to go deep on 'Repo scaffold: app plus Dockerfile'. What answer structure makes you sound senior?",
     "back": "Three beats: (1) one-sentence definition, (2) how it works mechanically, (3) when it fits and its key tradeoff — then stop and invite the follow-up. Structure is what separates a senior answer from a recitation."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you convince a team to adopt your platform?",
     "back": "Make the paved road the easy road: golden-path templates, self-service deploys, docs that answer before they ask — and never mandate what you can't support. Adoption is the platform's real metric; a platform nobody uses is a hobby. The interview line: 'I measure the platform by its customers' deploys, not by its features.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you discuss IDPs (internal developer platforms)?",
     "back": "Self-service golden paths: provision, deploy, and observe without tickets — the paved road as a product. The interview line: ‘the platform’s customer is the developer.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What do interviewers probe in platform metrics?",
     "back": "Adoption, lead time, and toil reduction — the platform proves itself in developer velocity. The interview line: ‘a platform without metrics is a hobby.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "An Architecture Decision Record (ADR) captures:",
     "back": "**The decision, its context, alternatives considered, and consequences — why, not just what** — Future-you needs the reasoning, not just the outcome; ADRs preserve the why."
    },
    {
     "front": "In GitOps, cluster drift is handled by:",
     "back": "**The agent reconciling actual state back to the git-declared desired state** — Continuous reconciliation closes the loop: declared state wins automatically."
    },
    {
     "front": "Signed integer overflow in C is:",
     "back": "**Undefined behavior — the optimizer may assume it never happens** — Compilers exploit the UB assumption for optimization; ‘works in debug’ proves nothing."
    },
    {
     "front": "Scripting the cluster bootstrap (vs clicking) matters because:",
     "back": "**It’s reproducible and reviewable — the cluster becomes code, not folklore** — Unscripted setup can’t be rebuilt, reviewed, or audited."
    }
   ],
