   "handsOnLab": {
    "id": "devops-interview-prep--devops-platform-build--lab",
    "title": "Operator's brief: Guided Platform Build",
    "brief": "You will write the artifacts a real operator would produce for Guided Platform Build — incident review, SLO spec, and tradeoff analysis — because interviews probe the seams.",
    "steps": [
     "Write a blameless incident review for a realistic Guided Platform Build failure: timeline, impact in numbers, detection, response, root cause, action items with owners.",
     "Specify the SLOs: indicators, objectives, error budget, and what consumes the budget — with the math shown.",
     "Draw the architecture and annotate every tradeoff: what you chose, what you gave up, why it was worth it.",
     "Write the runbook excerpt: the alert, the first five diagnostic commands, the escalation path.",
     "Prepare the war story: a three-minute telling with numbers, your decisions, and what you'd do differently.",
     "Peer-review the brief as a hiring manager: which claims have evidence, which are buzzwords?"
    ],
    "deliverable": "incident-review.md, slo-spec.md (with math), architecture tradeoffs, runbook excerpt, and war-story script for Guided Platform Build.",
    "checkpoint": "Incident review has numbers and owned action items; SLO math is shown; every architecture choice names its tradeoff; war story runs three minutes."
   },
   "id": "devops-interview-prep--devops-platform-build",
   "mockTask": {
    "id": "devops-interview-prep--devops-platform-build--mock",
    "type": "choice",
    "q": "In GitOps, cluster drift is handled by:",
    "choices": [
     "Redeploying everything",
     "Manual fixes",
     "Alerts only",
     "The agent reconciling actual state back to the git-declared desired state"
    ],
    "answer": 3,
    "explain": "Continuous reconciliation closes the loop: declared state wins automatically."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **Platform scaffold** — repo layout (app + infra + pipeline), VPC via Terraform, scripted cluster bootstrap, CI pipeline, GitOps deploy: the platform's first impression.\n- **GitOps** — git as the single source of truth for desired state; an agent (Argo CD/Flux) continuously reconciles the cluster to it. Declared state wins automatically.\n- **Cluster bootstrap** — scripted, reviewable cluster creation: the cluster becomes code, not folklore.\n- **ADR (Architecture Decision Record)** — the decision, its context, alternatives considered, and consequences: the 'why' behind the design.\n- **Runbook** — the operator's procedure for a known failure: the platform team's API to its customers.\n- **Documentation as deliverable** — platforms have customers; undocumented automation is a ticket queue with extra steps."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Build the platform scaffold on paper, in order, in 40 minutes.\n1. **Lay out the repo.** `app/` (code + Dockerfile), `infra/` (Terraform: VPC, cluster), `pipeline/` (CI workflows), `gitops/` (Argo CD app manifests), `docs/` (ADRs, runbooks). Justify each directory.\n2. **Terraform the VPC.** Module with public/private subnets, NAT, route tables — the network the cluster will live on.\n3. **Script the bootstrap.** Every cluster-creation step as a script or IaC: no clicks. State why: reproducible, reviewable, auditable.\n4. **Wire the CI pipeline.** Lint → test → build → scan → push SHA-tagged image — the pipeline from the CI/CD module, reused.\n5. **Add the GitOps deploy.** Argo CD watches the repo; merges to main reconcile the cluster. Narrate the drift story: manual changes get reverted automatically.\n6. **Write one ADR and one runbook.** ADR: why Argo CD over push-based deploys. Runbook: pods CrashLooping — the triage procedure from the troubleshooting module."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: the platform-build portfolio piece — the artifact you walk an interviewer through.\n- **Repo scaffold** with the full layout and a README that explains the whole system in one page.\n- **Terraform for the VPC**, **scripted cluster bootstrap**, **CI pipeline**, **GitOps deploy** — each piece committed as code.\n- **Two ADRs**: the GitOps choice and one infrastructure choice, each with context, alternatives, and consequences.\n- **One runbook**: the CrashLooping triage procedure, written for a teammate at 3am.\n- **The 10-minute walkthrough**: repo → cluster → pipeline → deploy, ending with 'the scaffold is the platform's first impression.'\nReference shelf (module sources): **Terraform Documentation** (https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/docs). **GitHub Actions Documentation** (https://docs.github.com/en/actions). **Kubernetes Documentation** (https://kubernetes.io/docs/)."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and a scaffold is not a production platform. Real platforms serve real developers with real SLAs, and their hardest problems are organizational — adoption, ownership, support load — not YAML. What the build teaches is the shape: automated, documented, reconcilable, and reviewable. Describe it in interviews as a portfolio build with stated simplifications, never as production platform experience you don't have."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: An Architecture Decision Record (ADR) captures:",
     "body": "**Problem.** An Architecture Decision Record (ADR) captures:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** The decision, its context, alternatives considered, and consequences — why, not just what\n- **Why:** Future-you needs the reasoning, not just the outcome; ADRs preserve the why.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Future-you needs the reasoning, not just the outcome; ADRs preserve the why.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In Guided Platform Build:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts Guided Platform Build interviews probe."
    }
   ],
