   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "How do you handle DevOps mock rounds (pipeline design, live troubleshooting)?",
     "back": "Pipeline design: stages in order, artifact promotion, rollback story. Live troubleshooting: hypothesis → test → observe, narrated, time-boxed. The interview line: ‘design shows judgment; troubleshooting shows method.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do behavioral rounds differ for DevOps roles?",
     "back": "They probe ownership under pressure: on-call stories, incident decisions, disagreements about risk and automation, and learning from outages — all STAR, all blameless. The interview line: ‘DevOps behavioral rounds grade how I act when the pager fires.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What’s the STAR structure, and why does it work for behavioral rounds?",
     "back": "Situation (2 sentences of context), Task (your responsibility), Action (what YOU did — the bulk), Result (measurable outcome + lesson). It works because interviewers score evidence of behavior, and STAR forces a complete evidence unit."
    },
    {
     "front": "What questions should you ask the interviewer?",
     "back": "Ones that reveal the job’s reality: ‘what does success look like in 6 months?’, ‘what’s the hardest technical problem the team faces?’, ‘how are on-call and incidents handled?’, ‘why is this role open?’ Never ask what the website answers."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you handle offer and negotiation basics?",
     "back": "Know your market range from data (levels.fyi, peers), let them anchor first when possible, negotiate the whole package (base, equity, bonus, start date) — never just base salary — and get everything in writing. The interview line: ‘I negotiate once, informed and in good faith.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you answer 'tell me about a disagreement with a teammate'?",
     "back": "STAR with the conflict as the situation: what each side wanted, the data or principle that decided it, what you actually did (listened, proposed, conceded or persuaded), and the result — including what you'd do differently. Blameless, specific, and about the work. The interview line: 'disagreements are design reviews with feelings; I treat them that way.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you prep the live-troubleshooting mock?",
     "back": "Narrate hypotheses, test cheaply, read the timeline of changes — thinking aloud is the scored skill. The interview line: ‘silent debugging is unscoreable.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What do you ask a DevOps interviewer that scores?",
     "back": "On-call reality (frequency, toil), deployment confidence, and what breaks most — questions that reveal the job’s truth. The interview line: ‘ask about the pager before you sign up for it.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "Negotiating only base salary is weak because:",
     "back": "**Total compensation includes equity, bonus, and benefits — often larger than base deltas** — The package is the number; single-dimension negotiation leaves money unclaimed."
    },
    {
     "front": "The strongest interviewer question reveals:",
     "back": "**The role’s reality: success criteria, hard problems, team health** — You’re interviewing them too: these answers predict your actual day-to-day."
    },
    {
     "front": "In a STAR answer, the ‘A’ should emphasize:",
     "back": "**Your specific actions and decisions** — ‘We’ stories hide your contribution — interviewers evaluate you, so make your decisions explicit."
    },
    {
     "front": "An SRE behavioral answer should emphasize:",
     "back": "**Decisions under pressure, communication, and systemic learning** — Reliability is a team sport; the graded behaviors are judgment and coordination."
    }
   ],
   "code": "IL-14",
