   "handsOnLab": {
    "id": "devops-interview-prep--devops-mock--lab",
    "title": "Operator's brief: DevOps Mock Circuit",
    "brief": "You will write the artifacts a real operator would produce for DevOps Mock Circuit — incident review, SLO spec, and tradeoff analysis — because interviews probe the seams.",
    "steps": [
     "Write a blameless incident review for a realistic DevOps Mock Circuit failure: timeline, impact in numbers, detection, response, root cause, action items with owners.",
     "Specify the SLOs: indicators, objectives, error budget, and what consumes the budget — with the math shown.",
     "Draw the architecture and annotate every tradeoff: what you chose, what you gave up, why it was worth it.",
     "Write the runbook excerpt: the alert, the first five diagnostic commands, the escalation path.",
     "Prepare the war story: a three-minute telling with numbers, your decisions, and what you'd do differently.",
     "Peer-review the brief as a hiring manager: which claims have evidence, which are buzzwords?"
    ],
    "deliverable": "incident-review.md, slo-spec.md (with math), architecture tradeoffs, runbook excerpt, and war-story script for DevOps Mock Circuit.",
    "checkpoint": "Incident review has numbers and owned action items; SLO math is shown; every architecture choice names its tradeoff; war story runs three minutes."
   },
   "id": "devops-interview-prep--devops-mock",
   "mockTask": {
    "id": "devops-interview-prep--devops-mock--mock",
    "type": "choice",
    "q": "The strongest interviewer question reveals:",
    "choices": [
     "Salary immediately",
     "Stock price",
     "Their personal life",
     "The role’s reality: success criteria, hard problems, team health"
    ],
    "answer": 3,
    "explain": "You’re interviewing them too: these answers predict your actual day-to-day."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **Pipeline-design round** — stages in order, artifact promotion, rollback story: design shows judgment.\n- **Live troubleshooting round** — hypothesis → test → observe, narrated, time-boxed: troubleshooting shows method.\n- **STAR for ops** — Situation, Task, Action (yours, the bulk), Result (measurable + lesson): the evidence unit for behavioral rounds.\n- **Ops behavioral stories** — on-call nights, incident decisions, disagreements about risk, learning from outages: all blameless.\n- **Interviewer questions** — 'what does success look like in 6 months?', 'what's the hardest technical problem?', 'how are on-call and incidents handled?', 'why is this role open?' Never ask what the website answers.\n- **Total-compensation negotiation** — base, equity, bonus, start date: negotiate the whole package, informed and in good faith, and get it in writing."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Run the DevOps mock circuit with a partner, timed.\n1. **Pipeline-design round (45 min).** Given a sample service: stages, artifact, strategy per environment, rollback plan — narrated, with tradeoffs.\n2. **Live troubleshooting round (30 min).** A seeded failure (502s after deploy): hypothesis → test → observe → update, aloud, time-boxed.\n3. **Behavioral round (30 min).** Three STAR stories: an on-call night, a disagreement about risk, an outage you learned from — all blameless.\n4. **Your questions (10 min).** Ask the interviewer the four reality-revealing questions; practice listening for the health of the team in the answers.\n5. **Score it.** Rubric per round: design judgment, diagnostic method, story evidence, communication. Name the lowest axis.\n6. **Write the debrief.** The weakest round becomes next week's drills; the strongest story goes in the bank, rehearsed."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: a DevOps interview dossier.\n- **Pipeline design doc** from a timed round, with the rollback story and the strategy matrix.\n- **Troubleshooting debrief**: the seeded failure, your hypothesis chain, where narration broke down, the fix.\n- **Story bank**: five STAR ops stories (on-call, incident, disagreement, failure, learning) — each with a measurable result.\n- **Your interviewer questions**, with notes on what good vs worrying answers sound like.\n- **Compensation worksheet**: your market range from data, the whole-package checklist, and the 'negotiate once, informed and in good faith' plan.\n- **The taper plan**: no new topics in the final two weeks — redrill, mock, rest.\nReference shelf (module sources): **GitHub Actions Documentation** (https://docs.github.com/en/actions). **Indeed Career Guide: Interviewing** (https://www.indeed.com/career-advice) — STAR format and preparation checklists."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and mock rounds are not real loops. Partners are kinder than panels, and practiced stories must stay truthful: never invent on-call heroics you didn't live — interviewers probe stories, and fabricated ones collapse. Rehearse real experience; frame drill experience as drills. Compensation guidance is general information, not financial advice; market data moves — verify ranges against current sources when you negotiate."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Negotiating only base salary is weak because:",
     "body": "**Problem.** Negotiating only base salary is weak because:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** Total compensation includes equity, bonus, and benefits — often larger than base deltas\n- **Why:** The package is the number; single-dimension negotiation leaves money unclaimed.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *The package is the number; single-dimension negotiation leaves money unclaimed.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In DevOps Mock Circuit:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts DevOps Mock Circuit interviews probe."
    }
   ],
