  {
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "Regions vs Availability Zones?",
     "back": "Region: geographic area. AZ: isolated data center(s) within a region with independent power/network. Design rule: run across ≥2 AZs for HA; regions are for disaster recovery and data residency. The interview line: ‘one AZ is a single point of failure.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What is the shared-responsibility model?",
     "back": "The provider secures OF the cloud (physical, hypervisor, network fabric); you secure what’s IN it (data, IAM, OS, app config). The interview trap: ‘the cloud is secure’ — no, your S3 bucket policy is your job."
    },
    {
     "front": "What does Docker actually give you?",
     "back": "A portable, reproducible runtime: the image bundles code + dependencies + OS userland, so ‘works on my machine’ dies. Containers share the host kernel (lighter than VMs, weaker isolation) — and the interview follow-up is always about image size and layer caching."
    },
    {
     "front": "CapEx vs OpEx in cloud interviews?",
     "back": "CapEx: buy servers upfront (fixed, depreciating). OpEx: pay per use (variable, scales with demand). Cloud’s pitch is OpEx agility — but the honest follow-up is FinOps: without governance, variable spend surprises you."
    },
    {
     "front": "Vendor-neutral vs vendor-specific knowledge?",
     "back": "Neutral: concepts that transfer (VPCs, IAM, queues, object storage). Specific: the console clicks and service quirks. The interview line: ‘I learn the concept once, the console twice — concepts transfer, click-paths don’t.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you answer 'why not just buy servers?'",
     "back": "With the honest math: steady-state 24/7 can be close on price, but the cloud buys elasticity for spiky workloads, managed undifferentiated work (backups, patching, failover), and provisioning speed. The follow-up they want: 'and without FinOps governance, variable spend surprises you.' The interview line: 'CapEx is a bet; OpEx is a meter — I choose by workload shape.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you discuss regions vs AZs for resilience?",
     "back": "AZs: isolated failure domains in a region (power, network). Regions: geographic separation for disasters and compliance. The interview line: ‘AZs survive rack failures; regions survive hurricanes.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What do interviewers probe in elasticity vs scalability?",
     "back": "Scalability: handling more load. Elasticity: scaling up AND down automatically with demand — the cloud’s distinctive trick. The interview line: ‘elasticity is scalability with a down arrow.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "Leading with vendor-neutral concepts in an interview is strong because:",
     "back": "**It proves transferable understanding, not memorized console paths** — Concepts survive provider changes; click-path knowledge expires with the UI."
    },
    {
     "front": "Cloud’s OpEx model primarily buys:",
     "back": "**Agility — spend scales with demand instead of upfront provisioning** — Pay-per-use trades fixed capacity bets for variable spend — with cost governance as the required discipline."
    },
    {
     "front": "Containers differ from VMs in that they:",
     "back": "**Share the host OS kernel instead of virtualizing hardware** — No guest kernel per container: faster startup, denser packing, but a shared kernel attack surface."
    },
    {
     "front": "Under shared responsibility, an open S3 bucket is:",
     "back": "**The customer’s fault — data access control is in the customer’s layer** — The provider secures the service; you configure who can read your objects."
    }
   ],
   "code": "IL-14",
