   "id": "cloud-interview-prep--cloud-concepts",
   "mockTask": {
    "id": "cloud-interview-prep--cloud-concepts--mock",
    "type": "choice",
    "q": "Cloud’s OpEx model primarily buys:",
    "choices": [
     "No billing",
     "Free resources",
     "Lower total cost always",
     "Agility — spend scales with demand instead of upfront provisioning"
    ],
    "answer": 3,
    "explain": "Pay-per-use trades fixed capacity bets for variable spend — with cost governance as the required discipline."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **Region** — a geographic area of cloud data centers: the unit of data residency and disaster recovery.\n- **Availability Zone (AZ)** — isolated data center(s) within a region with independent power and networking: run across ≥2 AZs; one AZ is a single point of failure.\n- **Edge** — points of presence near users for caching and termination: proximity, not resilience.\n- **Shared responsibility** — the provider secures OF the cloud (physical, hypervisor, fabric); you secure what's IN it (data, IAM, OS, app config).\n- **VM** — virtualized hardware with a guest OS: strongest isolation, heaviest footprint.\n- **Container** — shared host kernel, packaged userland: lighter and faster than VMs, weaker isolation.\n- **Serverless** — functions the provider scales to zero: no servers to manage, pay per invocation, mind the cold starts.\n- **CapEx** — buy servers upfront: fixed, depreciating. **OpEx** — pay per use: variable, agile, and in need of FinOps governance.\n- **Vendor-neutral knowledge** — concepts that transfer (VPCs, IAM, queues, object storage) vs vendor-specific click-paths that expire with the UI."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Work the classic 'should we move to the cloud' estimation — the honest version, with assumptions stated.\n1. **State the workload.** A sample internal tool: 4 VMs on-prem, running 24/7, refreshed every 4 years at $8,000 per server (assumed figures for the exercise).\n2. **Compute the CapEx baseline.** 4 × $8,000 = $32,000 over 4 years, plus power, cooling, and your time — write the 'plus' items even without numbers.\n3. **Sketch the OpEx alternative.** Equivalent cloud VMs at an assumed $0.10/hr each: 4 × $0.10 × 730 hrs ≈ $292/month ≈ $3,500/year.\n4. **Add the honest costs.** Data transfer out, managed-service premiums, and the FinOps tax: untracked variable spend surprises you.\n5. **Name the non-price factors.** Elasticity for spiky workloads, managed backups, and speed of provisioning — agility is what OpEx actually buys.\n6. **Deliver the verdict format.** 'On pure 24/7 steady-state cost it's close; the cloud wins on elasticity and managed undifferentiated work — and loses if we lift-and-shift without rightsizing.'"
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: a one-page 'cloud concepts' briefing you'd give a skeptical CTO.\n- **The primitives**: regions/AZs/edge, shared responsibility, and the VM/container/serverless decision tree — each with its 'when it wins'.\n- **The worked estimation** from this module, with assumptions labeled as assumptions.\n- **The honest value props**: what the cloud genuinely buys (elasticity, managed heavy lifting, speed) and what it doesn't (automatic savings, automatic security).\n- **The trap answers**: 'the cloud is secure' (no — your bucket policy is your job), 'it's always cheaper' (no — FinOps or surprise).\n- **Present it in 10 minutes**, then take the hardest pushback question and answer it in writing.\nReference shelf (module sources): **AWS Documentation** (https://docs.aws.amazon.com/) — compute, networking, data, and security primitives. **Google Cloud Documentation** (https://cloud.google.com/docs) — GCP-side primitives for vendor-neutral depth."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and the worked estimation uses assumed prices to teach method. Real cloud prices change constantly and vary by region, commitment, and workload shape — never quote the exercise figures as facts about any real bill. The transferable skill is the structure: CapEx vs OpEx with assumptions stated, shared responsibility drawn as a line, and compute primitives chosen by workload — vendor-neutral first, because concepts transfer and click-paths don't."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Leading with vendor-neutral concepts in an interview is strong…",
     "body": "**Problem.** Leading with vendor-neutral concepts in an interview is strong because:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** It proves transferable understanding, not memorized console paths\n- **Why:** Concepts survive provider changes; click-path knowledge expires with the UI.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Concepts survive provider changes; click-path knowledge expires with the UI.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In Core Cloud Concepts:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts Core Cloud Concepts interviews probe."
    }
   ],
