  {
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "VPC, subnets, route tables — the 60-second version?",
     "back": "VPC: your isolated network. Subnets: public (route to internet gateway) vs private (no direct inbound, NAT for outbound). Route tables decide where packets go; security groups (stateful, instance-level) and NACLs (stateless, subnet-level) filter them."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you design multi-AZ networking in an interview?",
     "back": "VPC spanning AZs, public subnets with NAT gateways per AZ for outbound, private subnets for workloads, and no single-AZ chokepoints — cross-AZ traffic costs money, so place chatty tiers together. The interview line: ‘multi-AZ networking is redundancy you can draw and a bill you can predict.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What do cloud load balancers do beyond spreading traffic?",
     "back": "Health checks (route only to healthy targets), TLS termination, sticky sessions when needed, and L7 routing (path/host rules). The interview line: ‘the LB is the first reliability device in the path.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you discuss DNS in cloud interviews?",
     "back": "Public DNS for the world, private hosted zones for internal service discovery, TTLs controlling change propagation, and health-checked routing policies for failover. The interview line: ‘DNS is the control plane everyone forgets until it breaks.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "NAT vs VPN vs peering?",
     "back": "NAT: private resources reach the internet outbound. VPN: encrypted tunnel extending your network (site-to-site or client). Peering: private VPC-to-VPC connectivity without internet. The interview line: ‘each answers a different ‘how do these networks talk’ question.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "Security group vs NACL — when does the difference actually bite?",
     "back": "Security groups are stateful (return traffic auto-allowed) at the instance level; NACLs are stateless (both directions need rules) at the subnet level. It bites when SSH works through the security group but the NACL blocks the ephemeral-port return — connection hangs, not refused. The interview line: 'stateful forgives; stateless makes you write both directions.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you discuss VPC endpoints (PrivateLink)?",
     "back": "Private connectivity to managed services without internet traversal — no NAT, no public IPs, tighter blast radius. The interview line: ‘endpoints keep cloud traffic off the internet.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What do interviewers probe in transit gateways?",
     "back": "Hub-and-spoke for many VPCs: one gateway instead of N×N peering — with route-table segmentation for isolation. The interview line: ‘peering scales quadratically; transit scales linearly.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "VPC flow logs show:",
     "back": "**Allowed/rejected IP traffic metadata — who tried to reach whom** — Flow logs are the packet-level audit trail for security-group and routing diagnosis."
    },
    {
     "front": "VPC peering is preferred over public IPs for inter-VPC traffic because:",
     "back": "**Traffic stays on the private backbone — no internet exposure** — Private routing beats public routing for both security and latency."
    },
    {
     "front": "A low TTL on a DNS record is used when:",
     "back": "**You expect to change the target soon and need fast propagation** — Low TTL trades query volume for agility: changes propagate as fast as the TTL allows."
    },
    {
     "front": "Load balancer health checks matter because:",
     "back": "**Traffic must avoid dead targets — the LB routes only to passing instances** — Without health checks, the balancer sprays traffic onto broken instances."
    }
   ],
