   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **VPC** — your isolated virtual network: the boundary everything else lives inside.\n- **Subnet** — a slice of the VPC: public subnets route to an internet gateway; private subnets have no direct inbound (NAT for outbound).\n- **Route table** — decides where packets go: the VPC's traffic cop.\n- **Security group** — stateful, instance-level firewall: return traffic is automatically allowed.\n- **NACL** — stateless, subnet-level firewall: both directions need explicit rules.\n- **Load balancer** — spreads traffic, but also health-checks, terminates TLS, and routes L7 by path/host: the first reliability device in the path.\n- **NAT** — lets private resources reach the internet outbound without being reachable inbound.\n- **VPN** — encrypted tunnel extending your network: site-to-site or client.\n- **VPC peering** — private VPC-to-VPC connectivity over the provider backbone: no internet exposure.\n- **Flow logs** — allowed/rejected IP traffic metadata: the packet-level audit trail for security-group and routing diagnosis."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Debug a dead connection layer by layer — the drill that decides cloud interviews. Scenario: 'I can't SSH to the instance.'\n1. **DNS.** Does the name resolve? (`dig`/`nslookup`.) 'Could not resolve host' = DNS failure, not a server problem.\n2. **Routing.** Is there a route to the subnet? Check the route table: public subnet needs the internet gateway route; the instance needs a public IP.\n3. **Security groups.** Stateful: is inbound SSH allowed from your IP? Remember return traffic is automatic — misdiagnosing this wastes an hour.\n4. **NACLs.** Stateless: is inbound SSH allowed AND outbound ephemeral-port return allowed? The classic NACL trap.\n5. **The instance.** Is it running? Is the SSH daemon up? Check system logs — the network can be perfect and the service down.\n6. **Narrate the bisection.** Say each layer's test and what each outcome implicates: 'I bisect the stack instead of guessing.'"
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: a network design + troubleshooting guide for a sample three-tier VPC.\n- **The design**: VPC with public/private subnets across 2 AZs, route tables, NAT, security groups (stateful, least-privilege), NACLs (stateless, explicit both ways), ALB in public, app and data in private.\n- **The rules table**: every security-group and NACL rule with its justification — 'each answers a how-do-these-networks-talk question.'\n- **The dead-connection runbook**: the six-layer bisection from the Hands-on procedure as a checklist.\n- **The DNS section**: public vs private hosted zones, TTL strategy for an upcoming migration, health-checked failover.\n- **Present the design in 10 minutes**, then debug a seeded fault (wrong NACL) live.\nReference shelf (module sources): **AWS Documentation** (https://docs.aws.amazon.com/) — networking primitives. **Google Cloud Documentation** (https://cloud.google.com/docs) — GCP networking for vendor-neutral depth."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and paper networks don't drop packets. Real VPC debugging involves flow logs, overlapping CIDRs from acquisitions, and security-group sprawl that no diagram captures; the module teaches the bisection method and the stateful/stateless distinction that untangles them. Service names and defaults change; verify current behavior against the linked documentation as of its retrieval date."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: VPC flow logs show:",
     "body": "**Problem.** VPC flow logs show:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** Allowed/rejected IP traffic metadata — who tried to reach whom\n- **Why:** Flow logs are the packet-level audit trail for security-group and routing diagnosis.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Flow logs are the packet-level audit trail for security-group and routing diagnosis.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In Cloud Networking:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts Cloud Networking interviews probe."
    }
   ],
