   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "How do you handle file uploads at scale?",
     "back": "Direct-to-object-storage uploads (presigned URLs — the app server never proxies bytes), async processing (thumbnails, virus scan) via queues, CDN in front for reads. The interview line: ‘bytes never touch my app servers.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What do managed SQL services buy you?",
     "back": "Backups, patching, failover, and read replicas handled by the provider — you keep schema design and query tuning. The interview line: ‘I outsource the undifferentiated heavy lifting, not the data model.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you discuss managed NoSQL in a cloud interview?",
     "back": "By access pattern: key-value (DynamoDB-style single-digit-ms lookups), document (flexible JSON), wide-column (massive write scale). The interview line: ‘I choose the NoSQL by the query pattern, then take the managed version of that choice.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you discuss caching in a cloud interview?",
     "back": "What to cache (read-heavy, slow-changing), where (CDN edge, app cache, DB query cache), invalidation strategy, and the failure story (thundering herd, stale reads). The interview line: ‘caching is a tradeoff with an invalidation plan, not a speedup.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you manage data lifecycle and tiering?",
     "back": "Classify by access pattern and compliance needs: hot (frequent, fast storage), warm, cold/archive (cheap, slow retrieval). Automate transitions with lifecycle policies; the interview line: ‘every byte has a cost curve — tiering matches cost to value.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you answer 'SQL or NoSQL?' without sounding religious?",
     "back": "By access pattern: relational integrity and ad-hoc queries → managed SQL; single-digit-ms key lookups → key-value; flexible documents → document store. Then the managed caveat: 'I choose the NoSQL by the query pattern, then take the managed version of that choice.' The interview line: 'the workload picks the store; I just read its requirements.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you discuss backup vs replication?",
     "back": "Replication is availability (survives node loss); backup is recoverability (survives data loss and human error) — you need both. The interview line: ‘replication won’t save you from DELETE FROM.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What do interviewers probe in data residency?",
     "back": "Where data lives and crosses borders: region selection, compliance constraints, and the replication topology that honors them. The interview line: ‘residency is a legal requirement wearing an architecture costume.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "RPO and RTO define:",
     "back": "**How much data loss is tolerable and how fast recovery must complete** — RPO bounds data loss (backup frequency); RTO bounds downtime (recovery speed)."
    },
    {
     "front": "Archiving compliance data to glacier-class storage is justified because:",
     "back": "**Rarely-accessed retention data shouldn’t pay hot-storage prices** — Storage cost should follow access frequency; compliance retention is the classic cold tier."
    },
    {
     "front": "Every caching decision must name:",
     "back": "**The invalidation strategy — stale data is caching’s permanent tax** — Cache without an invalidation story is a future incident."
    },
    {
     "front": "Managed NoSQL’s pitch is:",
     "back": "**Operational burden offloaded — scaling, patching, and failover handled** — You keep the data-model decisions; the provider keeps the cluster healthy."
    }
   ],
