   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **Object storage** — flat, limitless blob storage (the S3 pattern): presigned URLs let clients upload directly so bytes never touch your app servers.\n- **Managed SQL** — backups, patching, failover, and read replicas handled by the provider: you keep schema design and query tuning.\n- **Key-value NoSQL** — single-digit-ms lookups by key: choose by access pattern, then take the managed version.\n- **Document NoSQL** — flexible JSON documents: for evolving schemas and document-shaped access.\n- **Caching** — what to cache (read-heavy, slow-changing), where (edge, app, DB), and the invalidation strategy: stale data is caching's permanent tax.\n- **Lifecycle tiering** — hot/warm/cold-archive matched to access frequency: every byte has a cost curve.\n- **RPO** — how much data loss is tolerable: bounds backup frequency. **RTO** — how fast recovery must complete: bounds downtime."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Work the backup-and-recovery estimation — RPO/RTO as numbers, not vibes.\n1. **State the business answers.** 'We can lose at most 1 hour of orders (RPO = 1h); checkout must be back within 4 hours (RTO = 4h).' Get these from the business, not engineering.\n2. **Derive the backup frequency.** RPO of 1 hour → backups (or continuous replication) at least hourly. Daily backups would violate the RPO by 23 hours — say so.\n3. **Derive the recovery architecture.** RTO of 4 hours → tested restore procedure that completes in under 4 hours: rehearsed restores, not documentation.\n4. **Price the tiers.** Hot data (yesterday's orders) on fast storage; 7-year compliance retention on archive tier — 'rarely-accessed retention data shouldn't pay hot-storage prices.'\n5. **Name the failure test.** 'When did we last restore from backup?' — an untested backup is a hope, not a plan.\n6. **Close with the line.** 'RPO bounds data loss; RTO bounds downtime — and both are business decisions with engineering price tags.'"
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: a data-architecture one-pager for a sample e-commerce service.\n- **Store choices**: object storage for uploads (presigned-URL flow), managed SQL for orders (with read replicas), key-value for sessions, cache layer with invalidation strategy — each chosen by access pattern.\n- **The RPO/RTO table** from the Worked estimation, with the backup schedule and restore test that satisfies it.\n- **Lifecycle policy**: hot → warm → archive transitions with the cost reasoning per tier.\n- **The failure story**: cache outage, primary DB failure, region loss — what degrades, what the customer sees.\n- **Defend it in 10 minutes**, ending with: 'I choose the store by the query pattern, then take the managed version of that choice.'\nReference shelf (module sources): **AWS Documentation** (https://docs.aws.amazon.com/) — data service primitives. **Azure Architecture Center** (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/) — reference architectures and design guidance."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and the worked numbers are illustrative. Real RPO/RTO values come from the business's risk tolerance, and real storage costs shift with tiers, regions, and access patterns — never quote exercise figures as facts. What transfers is the method: choose by access pattern, tier by cost curve, back up to the RPO, restore-test to the RTO, and always have the invalidation story before the cache."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: RPO and RTO define:",
     "body": "**Problem.** RPO and RTO define:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** How much data loss is tolerable and how fast recovery must complete\n- **Why:** RPO bounds data loss (backup frequency); RTO bounds downtime (recovery speed).\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *RPO bounds data loss (backup frequency); RTO bounds downtime (recovery speed).* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In Data and Storage Services:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts Data and Storage Services interviews probe."
    }
   ],
