  {
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "How do you explain defense in depth for cloud?",
     "back": "Layers that fail independently: identity (least-privilege IAM), network (security groups, NACLs, private subnets), data (encryption at rest and in transit, KMS), and detective controls (audit logs, Config rules). The interview line: ‘no single layer is trusted; the stack is the control.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you do IAM least privilege in practice?",
     "back": "Roles over users, policies scoped to specific actions on specific resources, no wildcards in production, permission boundaries and SCPs as guardrails, regular access reviews. The interview line: ‘every permission has a reason and an expiry conversation.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you secure APIs at the cloud edge?",
     "back": "Authentication at the API gateway (not in each service), WAF rules for L7 attacks, throttling and quotas per key, and private APIs behind VPC endpoints for internal traffic. The interview line: ‘the edge is where auth, abuse protection, and rate limits live once, not N times.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you manage secrets in the cloud?",
     "back": "Dedicated secret managers (rotation, audit, fine-grained access) — never env files in repos, never baked into images. Apps fetch at runtime with their workload role. The interview line: ‘secrets are fetched, not shipped.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What are cloud guardrails?",
     "back": "Preventive: SCPs and permission boundaries that make bad actions impossible. Detective: Config rules and CloudTrail audit trails that catch what happened. The interview line: ‘guardrails make the safe path the easy path.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What's the first thing you check in an unfamiliar cloud account?",
     "back": "Identity: who has what access. Users without MFA, wildcard admin policies, and ancient access keys are the fastest wins — most breaches start at identity, not at the firewall. The interview line: 'I audit the front door before the windows.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you discuss KMS envelope encryption?",
     "back": "Data keys encrypt the data; master keys in KMS encrypt the data keys — rotation and audit at the master level. The interview line: ‘envelope encryption makes key rotation cheap.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What do interviewers probe in SCPs vs IAM policies?",
     "back": "SCPs: guardrails on what an account CAN do (even admins). IAM: what an identity may do. The interview line: ‘SCPs set the ceiling; IAM works under it.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "CloudTrail’s primary purpose is:",
     "back": "**An audit log of who did what via the API — the forensic record** — Every API call recorded with identity and time: the basis for incident forensics and compliance."
    },
    {
     "front": "Baking secrets into container images is wrong because:",
     "back": "**Anyone with registry access gets the secrets — images are widely shared artifacts** — Images travel through registries and caches; secrets must be injected at runtime, not build time."
    },
    {
     "front": "Creating the same resource twice with POST vs PUT: the key difference is:",
     "back": "**PUT is idempotent — repeating it has the same effect; POST is not guaranteed to be** — Idempotency is the contract: safe retries depend on it."
    },
    {
     "front": "An IAM policy with Action: ‘*’ on Resource: ‘*’ is:",
     "back": "**A violation of least privilege — blast radius is the whole account** — Wildcard-everything grants total control; compromise of those credentials compromises the account."
    }
   ],
   "code": "IL-14",
