   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **IAM user / role / policy** — users are people, roles are assumable identities for workloads and people, policies are the permission documents: roles over users, always.\n- **Least privilege** — every permission scoped to specific actions on specific resources, with a reason and an expiry conversation: no wildcards in production.\n- **SCP (Service Control Policy)** — preventive guardrail: makes bad actions impossible account-wide.\n- **Encryption at rest / in transit** — data encrypted on disk and on the wire: the baseline, not the differentiator.\n- **Secret manager** — rotation, audit, fine-grained access for credentials: secrets are fetched at runtime, never baked into images or repos.\n- **CloudTrail-style audit log** — who did what via the API, with identity and time: the forensic record.\n- **Detective controls** — Config rules and audit trails that catch what happened: the complement to preventive guardrails."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Audit a sample cloud account's security posture, on paper, in 30 minutes.\n1. **List the identities.** Users, roles, and what each can do. Flag: any user with console access and no MFA; any `Action: *` on `Resource: *`.\n2. **Apply least privilege.** Rewrite the flagged policy scoped to specific actions on specific resources. Write the one-sentence justification per permission.\n3. **Check the secrets.** Hunt for credentials in repos, env files, and images — every find becomes 'move to the secret manager, fetched at runtime.'\n4. **Verify encryption.** At rest and in transit for the data stores; note where the keys live and who can use them.\n5. **Add the guardrails.** One preventive (SCP blocking the worst action) and one detective (Config rule + audit-trail review cadence).\n6. **Write the findings like a consultant.** Risk-ranked, business impact, concrete fix with effort — criticals first, never a 200-item undifferentiated list."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: a cloud security review document for a sample account — the trust-test artifact.\n- **Identity review**: the IAM map, least-privilege rewrites, and the access-review cadence.\n- **Secrets and encryption**: the secrets inventory, the runtime-fetch design, encryption verification.\n- **Guardrails**: preventive SCPs + detective Config rules, each with what it stops and what it catches.\n- **The headline findings**: top 5, risk-ranked, each with business impact and a concrete fix.\n- **Present it in 10 minutes** as if to a CTO, ending with: 'guardrails make the safe path the easy path.'\nReference shelf (module sources): **AWS Documentation** (https://docs.aws.amazon.com/) — IAM and security primitives. **CISA Resources & Tools** (https://www.cisa.gov/resources-tools) — hardening and best-practice guidance."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and a paper audit is not a security assessment. Real accounts carry years of accumulated access, exceptions, and organizational politics that no exercise captures; the module teaches the review structure — identity, secrets, encryption, guardrails — and the consultant's reporting discipline. Never present a classroom audit as professional security-assessment experience. Service specifics change; verify against the linked documentation as of its retrieval date."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: CloudTrail’s primary purpose is:",
     "body": "**Problem.** CloudTrail’s primary purpose is:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** An audit log of who did what via the API — the forensic record\n- **Why:** Every API call recorded with identity and time: the basis for incident forensics and compliance.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Every API call recorded with identity and time: the basis for incident forensics and compliance.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In Cloud Security Controls:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts Cloud Security Controls interviews probe."
    }
   ],
