  {
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "How do you open a cloud architecture design round?",
     "back": "Requirements first (users, data, compliance), then region and AZ placement with the cost and latency reasoning stated, then the reference shape (edge → compute → data), then failure modes. The interview line: ‘I draw the blast radius before I draw the boxes.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "Walk the classic three-tier cloud architecture.",
     "back": "Presentation (ALB + CDN) → application (auto-scaled compute across AZs) → data (managed DB with replicas, caches). Public subnets for the LB, private for app and data. The interview line: ‘tiers are failure and scaling boundaries, not just boxes.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you present a cloud architecture with cost honesty?",
     "back": "IaC for the whole stack (reproducible, reviewable), a cost estimate with assumptions stated (requests, storage, transfer — transfer is the surprise line item), and the tradeoff: ‘here’s what drives the bill and the lever that moves it.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What’s an ADR, and why do architects write them?",
     "back": "Architecture Decision Record: the decision, context, alternatives considered, and consequences — the ‘why’ behind the design. Paired with a failure-mode review (what breaks, how we degrade). The interview line: ‘ADRs are how architecture survives team turnover.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "The interviewer asks you to go deep on 'Requirements and constraints'. What answer structure makes you sound senior?",
     "back": "Three beats: (1) one-sentence definition, (2) how it works mechanically, (3) when it fits and its key tradeoff — then stop and invite the follow-up. Structure is what separates a senior answer from a recitation."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you present architecture tradeoffs without rambling?",
     "back": "Three beats per decision: the choice, what it costs (money, complexity, latency), and when you'd choose differently. Then stop and invite the follow-up. The interview line: 'every architecture is a list of paid prices — I show the receipts.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you present a costed architecture honestly?",
     "back": "State assumptions (requests, data, transfer), name the cost drivers, and show the scaling math — ranges, not false precision. The interview line: ‘a cost estimate without assumptions is fiction.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What makes an ADR worth writing?",
     "back": "The decision, context, alternatives considered, and consequences — the reasoning future-you will need. The interview line: ‘ADRs are the architecture’s memory.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "A failure-mode review asks:",
