    ],
    "deliverable": "incident-review.md, slo-spec.md (with math), architecture tradeoffs, runbook excerpt, and war-story script for Guided Cloud Architecture.",
    "checkpoint": "Incident review has numbers and owned action items; SLO math is shown; every architecture choice names its tradeoff; war story runs three minutes."
   },
   "id": "cloud-interview-prep--cloud-arch-build",
   "mockTask": {
    "id": "cloud-interview-prep--cloud-arch-build--mock",
    "type": "choice",
    "q": "Data transfer is the classic cloud cost surprise because:",
    "choices": [
     "Architectures that chat across AZs/regions pay per GB — it compounds silently",
     "It’s expensive per GB",
     "It’s untracked",
     "It’s fixed"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explain": "Egress and cross-AZ transfer bill by volume; chatty designs pay continuously."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **Three-tier architecture** — presentation (ALB + CDN) → application (auto-scaled compute across AZs) → data (managed DB with replicas, caches): tiers as failure and scaling boundaries.\n- **Reference architecture** — the standard shape you start from and justify deviations against.\n- **Failure-mode review** — 'what breaks, how the system degrades, is that degradation acceptable': what separates architecture from diagramming.\n- **Cost drivers** — the line items that move the bill: transfer is the classic surprise; provisioned capacity the steady burn.\n- **ADR** — Architecture Decision Record: decision, context, alternatives, consequences — how architecture survives team turnover.\n- **IaC for the whole stack** — reproducible, reviewable infrastructure: the architecture exists as code, not as a diagram."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Cost the three-tier architecture honestly — assumptions stated, surprises named.\n1. **List the components.** ALB, app instances across 2 AZs, managed DB with replica, cache, object storage + CDN, data transfer.\n2. **Estimate each line with an assumption.** 'App: 4 instances at assumed $0.10/hr ≈ $292/mo' — every line carries its assumption.\n3. **Hunt the transfer.** Cross-AZ chatter and egress: architectures that chat across AZs/regions pay per GB, compounding silently — call it out as the surprise line item.\n4. **Add the reliability tax.** Multi-AZ and replicas cost more than single-AZ; write what the extra nine costs per month.\n5. **Offer the Honda.** The cheaper alternative (single AZ, smaller instances, no replica) with its tradeoff stated: 'here's the Cadillac and the Honda, and when each wins.'\n6. **Close with the lever.** 'Here's what drives the bill, and here's the lever that moves it' — the sentence architects are hired to say."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: the complete architecture package — the portfolio piece.\n- **Requirements and constraints** in one page: users, scale, latency, compliance, budget.\n- **The reference architecture**: three-tier across 2 AZs, every component justified, every arrow labeled.\n- **IaC for the whole stack**: the module layout that builds it reproducibly.\n- **The cost estimate** from the Worked estimation, assumptions labeled, Cadillac and Honda both shown.\n- **The failure-mode review**: per component — what breaks, how it degrades, whether that's acceptable.\n- **One ADR**: the highest-stakes decision, with alternatives and consequences.\n- **Walk an interviewer through it in 15 minutes**, from requirements to the cost slide.\nReference shelf (module sources): **AWS Well-Architected Framework** (https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/framework/welcome.html) — the six pillars; the architecture interview's rubric. **Terraform Documentation** (https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/docs) — IaC references."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and the costed architecture is a design exercise. Real prices move constantly and the worked figures are illustrative — never quote them as facts about any real deployment. Real architectures also carry organizational constraints (existing contracts, team skills, compliance regimes) that the exercise simplifies away. Present it as a portfolio design with stated assumptions; the graded skill is the method — requirements first, costed honestly, failure-reviewed."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: A failure-mode review asks:",
     "body": "**Problem.** A failure-mode review asks:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** What breaks, how the system degrades, and whether that degradation is acceptable\n- **Why:** Designing for the unhappy path is what separates architecture from diagramming.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Designing for the unhappy path is what separates architecture from diagramming.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In Guided Cloud Architecture:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts Guided Cloud Architecture interviews probe."
    }
   ],
