   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "How do you handle cloud mock rounds (architecture design, deep dives)?",
     "back": "Architecture: requirements → reference design → costed, with tradeoffs narrated. Deep dive: one service’s internals (how the LB, the queue, the DB actually work) — mechanism, not marketing. The interview line: ‘I can draw it and I can explain the boxes.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "‘Design X on AWS’ — how do you narrate cost and tradeoffs?",
     "back": "Name the managed services you’d use and why, state the cost drivers honestly (transfer, provisioned capacity), and offer the cheaper alternative with its tradeoff. The interview line: ‘I’ll show you the Cadillac and the Honda, and tell you when each wins.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do behavioral rounds differ for SREs?",
     "back": "They probe ownership under pressure: on-call stories, incident decisions, disagreements about risk, and learning from outages — all STAR, all blameless. The interview line: ‘SRE behavioral rounds grade how I act when the pager fires.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What’s the final two-week taper?",
     "back": "Stop learning new topics; redrill weak patterns from your debrief log, run full timed mocks, fix sleep, and rehearse your story bank aloud. The taper converts knowledge into performance — cramming new material now only adds noise."
    },
    {
     "front": "The interviewer asks you to go deep on 'Mock architecture-design round'. What answer structure makes you sound senior?",
     "back": "Three beats: (1) one-sentence definition, (2) how it works mechanically, (3) when it fits and its key tradeoff — then stop and invite the follow-up. Structure is what separates a senior answer from a recitation."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you handle 'design X' when you've never built X?",
     "back": "Fall back to the shapes you know: requirements first, then map X onto three-tier, queue, or event-driven patterns, stating the mapping aloud. Interviewers grade method transfer, not prior exposure. The interview line: 'I haven't built this exact system; here's how I'd reason about it from patterns I have built.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "What does the cloud mock’s architecture round score?",
     "back": "Requirements, reference shape, costed tradeoffs, failure modes — narrated, not drawn silently. The interview line: ‘the walkthrough is the product.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you prep one service deeply for the deep-dive?",
     "back": "Mechanism, limits, failure modes, and cost model for the services in your design — one level deeper than the console. The interview line: ‘depth in one service beats breadth in ten.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "The taper period’s rule is:",
     "back": "**No new material — redrill weak spots, mock timed, rest** — Performance comes from consolidation and recovery, not last-minute breadth."
    },
    {
     "front": "An SRE behavioral answer should emphasize:",
     "back": "**Decisions under pressure, communication, and systemic learning** — Reliability is a team sport; the graded behaviors are judgment and coordination."
    },
    {
     "front": "Offering a cheaper alternative with tradeoffs is strong because:",
     "back": "**It proves you optimize for value, not resume-driven architecture** — Judgment about cost vs benefit is an architect-level signal."
    },
    {
     "front": "Cloud deep dives test:",
     "back": "**Whether you know how the managed service actually works under the abstraction** — Abstractions leak; interviewers probe the layer below the console."
    }
   ],
