   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **Architecture-design round** — requirements → reference design → costed, with tradeoffs narrated: 'I can draw it and I can explain the boxes.'\n- **Cloud deep-dive round** — one service's internals: how the LB, the queue, the DB actually work — mechanism, not marketing.\n- **'Design X on AWS' patterns** — the recurring shapes: three-tier web, data pipeline, event-driven serverless — each with its cost story.\n- **Well-Architected rubric** — the six pillars as the interview's hidden grading sheet: reliability, security, cost, performance, operations, sustainability.\n- **Cost narration** — naming cost drivers honestly and offering the cheaper alternative with its tradeoff: the architect-level signal.\n- **48-hour checklist** — logistics, rest, no new topics: the loop-day performance plan from the SRE path, reused here."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Run the cloud mock circuit with a partner, timed.\n1. **Architecture round (45 min).** 'Design a photo-sharing service on AWS': requirements, estimation, three-tier design, failure modes, costed — Cadillac and Honda.\n2. **Deep-dive round (30 min).** Partner picks one component (the LB, the queue, the DB): explain its internals — mechanism, not marketing — until they say stop.\n3. **Cost cross-examination (15 min).** Partner attacks the bill: 'cut 30%'. Defend or concede each line with the tradeoff stated.\n4. **Behavioral round (20 min).** Two STAR stories: a production decision you owned, a time you were wrong about a design.\n5. **Score against the pillars.** Rate each Well-Architected pillar in your answers; the lowest pillar is next week's study.\n6. **Write the debrief.** The two weakest moments, their categories, and the redrill plan — then the 48-hour checklist."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: a cloud interview dossier.\n- **Three architecture one-pagers**: three-tier web, event-driven serverless, data pipeline — each costed with assumptions.\n- **Deep-dive sheets**: the internals of a load balancer, a managed queue, and a managed database — mechanism-level, one page each.\n- **The cost-cutting drill**: your architecture with 30% removed, and what each cut costs in reliability.\n- **Score sheets** against the Well-Architected pillars, with the weakest pillar's study plan.\n- **Reflection**: which 'Design X' pattern still isn't automatic, and the reps that will make it so.\nReference shelf (module sources): **AWS Well-Architected Framework** (https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/framework/welcome.html). **Azure Architecture Center** (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/)."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and mock scores don't predict real loops. Partners are kinder than panels; treat scores as triage data. Cost figures in your one-pagers are illustrative estimates — label them as such and never present them as quotes. The honest win of the circuit: automatic architecture shapes, cost narration as a reflex, and a debrief habit that aims every study hour at the weakest pillar."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: The taper period’s rule is:",
     "body": "**Problem.** The taper period’s rule is:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** No new material — redrill weak spots, mock timed, rest\n- **Why:** Performance comes from consolidation and recovery, not last-minute breadth.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Performance comes from consolidation and recovery, not last-minute breadth.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In Cloud Mock Circuit:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts Cloud Mock Circuit interviews probe."
    }
   ],
