   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "How do you discuss the OWASP Top 10 without just listing it?",
     "back": "As risk categories with a current example each: injection, broken access control, crypto failures, SSRF, and so on — then pivot to YOUR experience: ‘here’s how I found one in code review and what the fix was.’ Categories frame; stories score."
    },
    {
     "front": "XSS types and the fix?",
     "back": "Stored (persisted, served to victims), reflected (in a crafted URL), DOM-based (client-side sink). The fix: output encoding/contextual escaping, Content-Security-Policy as defense in depth, and frameworks’ auto-escaping — never innerHTML with untrusted data."
    },
    {
     "front": "What auth flaws do you hunt in code review?",
     "back": "Broken authentication (credential stuffing without rate limits, weak session handling), broken object-level authorization (IDOR), missing function-level checks, JWT ‘none’ algorithm or missing expiry validation. The interview line: ‘I check every endpoint for ‘who can call this’ — not just the login page.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What’s SSRF and why is it nasty in the cloud?",
     "back": "Server-Side Request Forgery: the server fetches attacker-chosen URLs — in cloud, that means the instance metadata endpoint (169.254.169.254) and credential theft. Fixes: allowlist egress, validate/sanitize URLs, block metadata IP, IMDSv2. (Deserialization: never unserialize untrusted data — it’s remote code execution.)"
    },
    {
     "front": "What do you look for in a security code review?",
     "back": "Injection sinks, auth checks on every route, crypto done right (no custom algorithms), secrets handling, error messages that don’t leak internals, and dependency versions. The interview line: ‘I review the trust boundaries first — where untrusted data enters.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you prioritize AppSec findings in an interview?",
     "back": "By exploitability times impact: reachable-by-attacker and data-touching first, theoretical second. Frame each as risk with a concrete fix and an effort estimate. The interview line: ‘a finding without a fix and a severity is just a complaint.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you discuss broken access control?",
     "back": "The OWASP #1: missing function-level checks, IDOR, privilege escalation — test every route as every role. The interview line: ‘the URL is not the authorization.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What do interviewers probe in security headers?",
     "back": "CSP, HSTS, frame-ancestors, and cookie flags (HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite) — cheap, high-leverage defenses. The interview line: ‘headers are the cheapest security you’ll ever ship.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "Error messages that include stack traces are a finding because:",
     "back": "**They leak internals (paths, versions, queries) that aid attackers** — Information disclosure sharpens every subsequent attack; production errors stay generic."
    },
    {
     "front": "SSRF’s cloud nightmare scenario is:",
     "back": "**Hitting the instance metadata service to steal temporary credentials** — The metadata endpoint is one hop from the vulnerable server — credentials included."
    },
    {
     "front": "IDOR (Insecure Direct Object Reference) means:",
     "back": "**Changing an object ID in a request accesses another user’s data — missing authorization check** — Authentication proves who you are; authorization must then prove you may touch this object."
    },
    {
     "front": "The root fix for XSS is:",
     "back": "**Context-appropriate output encoding of untrusted data (+ CSP in depth)** — XSS is code/data confusion in the browser; encoding keeps untrusted data as data."
    }
   ],
