   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **OWASP Top 10** — the risk categories, current edition: injection, broken access control, cryptographic failures, SSRF, and more — discussed as mechanisms with examples, never just listed.\n- **Injection** — untrusted input executed as code (SQLi and friends): the fix is parameterized queries, never string-built commands.\n- **XSS (Cross-Site Scripting)** — stored, reflected, DOM-based: untrusted data rendered as code in the browser. Fix: context-appropriate output encoding + CSP in depth.\n- **IDOR** — Insecure Direct Object Reference: changing an object ID accesses another user's data — the missing authorization check.\n- **Broken authentication** — credential stuffing without rate limits, weak sessions, JWT 'none' algorithm or missing expiry validation.\n- **SSRF** — Server-Side Request Forgery: the server fetches attacker-chosen URLs; in cloud, the nightmare is the instance metadata endpoint and credential theft.\n- **Deserialization** — unserializing untrusted data: treat as remote code execution; never do it.\n- **CSP (Content-Security-Policy)** — defense in depth for XSS: constrains what the browser may execute.\n- **Trust boundary** — where untrusted data enters: review these first in any code review."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Run a 30-minute security code review on a sample web app, hunting the Top 10.\n1. **Map the trust boundaries.** List every place untrusted data enters: forms, APIs, file uploads, webhooks. 'I review the trust boundaries first.'\n2. **Hunt injection sinks.** Every query, command, or template built from input: is it parameterized/encoded? Flag string-built SQL immediately.\n3. **Check every endpoint's auth.** Not just the login page: broken object-level authorization (IDOR) on each route — 'who can call this, and whose data can they touch?'\n4. **Audit the session and token handling.** JWT algorithm validation, expiry, rate limits on login — the broken-authentication checklist.\n5. **Look for SSRF and deserialization.** Any server-side fetch of user-influenced URLs (allowlist? metadata IP blocked?); any unserialize of untrusted data.\n6. **Write the findings.** Risk-ranked, each with the vulnerable code pattern, the fix, and the verification — the writeup that shows method."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: a code-review finding writeup — the portfolio piece.\n- **Pick one vulnerability class** (XSS, IDOR, SSRF, or injection) and write the full finding: the code pattern, why it's exploitable, the fix, and how you'd verify the fix.\n- **Add the Top 10 framing**: which risk category it belongs to and a second example of the same category.\n- **Include the review checklist** you used — the trust-boundary-first procedure from this module.\n- **Sanitize everything**: no real company code, no real hostnames — method transfers, specifics don't need to.\n- **Present it in 10 minutes** as 'here's how I found one in code review and what the fix was.'\nReference shelf (module sources): **OWASP** (https://owasp.org/) — the Top 10 and project pages. **OWASP Cheat Sheet Series** (https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/) — concise defensive guidance on auth, crypto, and injection."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and classroom findings are not professional security-assessment experience. Never present exercise writeups as client work or bug-bounty history you don't have; sanitized method writeups are the honest portfolio. The OWASP Top 10 evolves — verify the current edition against the linked sources as of their retrieval dates. Security knowledge carries responsibility: everything here is defensive — mechanisms taught so you can find and fix them."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Error messages that include stack traces are a finding because:",
     "body": "**Problem.** Error messages that include stack traces are a finding because:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** They leak internals (paths, versions, queries) that aid attackers\n- **Why:** Information disclosure sharpens every subsequent attack; production errors stay generic.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Information disclosure sharpens every subsequent attack; production errors stay generic.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In Application Security Fundamentals:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts Application Security Fundamentals interviews probe."
    }
   ],
