   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "Walk the TLS handshake at interview depth.",
     "back": "ClientHello (ciphers) → ServerHello + certificate → key exchange (ECDHE for forward secrecy) → encrypted session. Validate: cert chain to a trusted root, hostname match, expiry — and pinning for high-value mobile/API clients."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you talk about firewalls and segmentation?",
     "back": "Defense in depth through zones: public DMZ, app tier, data tier — traffic only flows where explicitly allowed, east-west as well as north-south. The interview line: ‘segmentation turns one breach into a contained incident instead of a network-wide event.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What does zero trust actually require?",
     "back": "‘Never trust, always verify’: identity-based access (not network location), least privilege, device posture, continuous verification, micro-segmentation. The interview line: ‘the VPN perimeter is dead; every request authenticates and authorizes.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What’s DNS security in practice?",
     "back": "DNSSEC for integrity (responses are signed), DoH/DoT for query privacy, monitoring for tunneling/exfiltration (long odd queries), and registrar hygiene (locks, MFA). The interview line: ‘DNS is trusted by everything and verified by almost nothing — I verify.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you discuss DDoS mitigation?",
     "back": "Layers: edge absorption (CDN/anycast scrubbing), rate limiting, WAF rules for L7, and capacity headroom. Distinguish volumetric (bandwidth) from application-layer (expensive requests) — they need different defenses. The interview line: ‘you don’t outrun a botnet; you absorb and filter at the edge.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What does TLS actually guarantee — and what doesn't it?",
     "back": "It guarantees confidentiality and integrity in transit to the authenticated endpoint — not that the endpoint is trustworthy, not that the app behind it is safe. Phishing sites have valid certs. The interview line: 'TLS secures the pipe; it says nothing about what's drinking from it.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you discuss certificate lifecycle?",
     "back": "Issuance, renewal automation (ACME), expiry monitoring — expired certs are the most embarrassing outage. The interview line: ‘certs expire; automation doesn’t forget.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What do interviewers probe in network segmentation beyond VLANs?",
     "back": "Microsegmentation and identity-aware policies: least-privilege east-west, not just a DMZ perimeter. The interview line: ‘the perimeter moved to the workload.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "Wireshark showing repeated TCP retransmissions indicates:",
     "back": "**Packet loss or an unresponsive peer — a network-path problem, not application logic** — Retransmits are the transport layer saying ‘my packets aren’t getting through.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "Application-layer (L7) DDoS differs from volumetric in that it:",
     "back": "**Uses expensive legitimate-looking requests — filtering needs behavioral/WAF rules, not just bandwidth** — A few thousand costly requests can hurt more than gigabits of junk; defense is semantic."
    },
    {
     "front": "DNS tunneling exfiltrates data by:",
     "back": "**Encoding data in DNS queries to an attacker-controlled domain — DNS is rarely filtered** — DNS’s ubiquity makes it a covert channel; monitoring query patterns catches it."
    },
    {
     "front": "Zero trust’s core shift is:",
     "back": "**Access decisions from identity and context, not network location** — Being inside the network grants nothing; every request proves who/what it is."
    }
   ],
