   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **TLS handshake** — ClientHello → ServerHello + certificate → key exchange (ECDHE for forward secrecy) → encrypted session. Validates: chain to a trusted root, hostname match, expiry.\n- **Forward secrecy** — compromise of long-term keys doesn't decrypt past sessions: the ECDHE property.\n- **Certificate pinning** — clients accept only specific certs/keys: for high-value mobile/API clients.\n- **Segmentation** — defense in depth through zones (DMZ, app tier, data tier): traffic flows only where explicitly allowed, east-west as well as north-south.\n- **Zero trust** — 'never trust, always verify': identity-based access, least privilege, device posture, continuous verification, micro-segmentation.\n- **DNSSEC / DoH / DoT** — integrity for responses (signed), privacy for queries (encrypted transport).\n- **DNS tunneling** — data encoded in DNS queries to an attacker domain: DNS is rarely filtered, so it becomes a covert channel.\n- **Volumetric vs L7 DDoS** — bandwidth floods vs expensive legitimate-looking requests: absorbed at the edge vs filtered by behavioral/WAF rules."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Defend a network design aloud, in 20 minutes, against an interviewer's attacks.\n1. **Draw the zones.** Public DMZ, app tier, data tier — traffic only where explicitly allowed, east-west as well as north-south.\n2. **Walk the TLS.** Handshake at interview depth, then the practice layer: TLS 1.2+ and cipher hygiene, cert lifecycle via ACME automation, expiry monitoring, HSTS.\n3. **Apply zero trust.** For each zone crossing: what identity authenticates, what context authorizes — 'being inside the network grants nothing.'\n4. **Harden DNS.** DNSSEC for integrity, DoH/DoT for query privacy, monitoring for tunneling patterns, registrar locks + MFA.\n5. **Plan the DDoS layers.** Edge absorption (CDN/anycast scrubbing), rate limiting, WAF rules for L7, capacity headroom — matched to volumetric vs application-layer.\n6. **Take the attacker's turn.** Partner plays attacker for 5 minutes: for each attack, name the control that contains it and the residual risk."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: a network security design review for a sample three-tier system.\n- **The zone diagram** with explicit allow-rules per boundary — east-west included.\n- **The TLS section**: handshake walkthrough, version/cipher policy, cert lifecycle (ACME + expiry monitoring), HSTS, and where pinning applies.\n- **The zero-trust overlay**: identity, device posture, and continuous verification per access path.\n- **DNS and DDoS sections**: the hardening checklist and the layered mitigation plan.\n- **Findings, consultant-style**: risk-ranked, business impact, concrete fixes with effort estimates.\n- **Present in 10 minutes**, ending with: 'the VPN perimeter is dead; every request authenticates and authorizes.'\nReference shelf (module sources): **CISA Resources & Tools** (https://www.cisa.gov/resources-tools) — hardening and best-practice guidance. **NIST Cybersecurity Framework** (https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework) — the Identify/Protect/Detect/Respond/Recover frame."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and paper networks don't get attacked. Real network defense involves legacy protocols, exceptions, and organizational friction that no diagram captures; the module teaches the structure — zones, TLS lifecycle, zero-trust decisions, layered DDoS — and the aloud-defense habit. Protocol and product specifics change; verify current TLS, DNS, and product guidance against the linked sources as of their retrieval dates."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Wireshark showing repeated TCP retransmissions indicates:",
     "body": "**Problem.** Wireshark showing repeated TCP retransmissions indicates:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** Packet loss or an unresponsive peer — a network-path problem, not application logic\n- **Why:** Retransmits are the transport layer saying ‘my packets aren’t getting through.’\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Retransmits are the transport layer saying ‘my packets aren’t getting through.’* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In Network Security in Practice:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts Network Security in Practice interviews probe."
    }
   ],
