   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **Symmetric crypto (AES)** — one shared key, fast: for bulk data. **Asymmetric (RSA/ECDH)** — key pairs, slow: for key exchange and signatures. Hybrid in practice: asymmetric establishes, symmetric encrypts.\n- **Hashing vs password storage** — fast hashes (MD5/SHA-256) are for integrity, never passwords: GPUs crack them. Passwords get Argon2id (or bcrypt) with unique per-user salts and a tuned work factor.\n- **Salt / pepper** — unique per-user salt defeats rainbow tables; a pepper via KMS/HSM adds defense in depth.\n- **Forward secrecy** — ECDHE key exchange: past sessions stay secret even if long-term keys leak.\n- **KMS/HSM** — keys live in managed hardware/services, never in code or repos; envelope encryption; rotation on schedule and on incident.\n- **CSPRNG** — cryptographic randomness (/dev/urandom, secrets module) for keys, tokens, nonces: predictable randomness is no randomness.\n- **The never-roll-your-own rule** — subtle flaws survive testing and review; even experts get it wrong. Use vetted primitives."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Give the crypto answers cold — drill them until they're reflex.\n1. **State the password-storage answer verbatim.** 'Argon2id with unique per-user salts and a tuned work factor; pepper via KMS as defense in depth. Never fast hashes alone, never reversible encryption.'\n2. **Draw the hybrid.** Asymmetric establishes trust (key exchange, signatures), symmetric does the bulk work — say when each runs.\n3. **Audit a key-management story.** Keys in KMS/HSM, envelope encryption, rotation scheduled and on incident, separate keys per environment, every use audited — 'if I can see the key in the repo, we've already lost.'\n4. **Kill the randomness bug.** Find `Math.random()` used for a token in sample code; replace with a CSPRNG and explain why predictable PRNGs fall to attackers.\n5. **Check the TLS lifecycle.** Version/cipher hygiene, ACME automation, expiry monitoring — 'handshake knowledge gets you hired; lifecycle hygiene keeps you employed.'\n6. **Say the rule.** 'I don't roll my own crypto — subtle flaws survive review; I use vetted primitives and put my cleverness into key management.'"
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: a crypto review one-pager for a sample service.\n- **The password-storage design**: algorithm, salt, work factor, pepper — with the 'why not SHA-256' reasoning.\n- **The TLS posture**: versions, ciphers, cert lifecycle, HSTS — handshake plus hygiene.\n- **The key-management design**: where keys live, rotation schedule and triggers, per-environment separation, audit.\n- **The randomness audit**: every token/nonce/key source checked against the CSPRNG rule.\n- **The never-build list**: what this service will never implement itself, and the vetted primitive for each.\n- **Present in 10 minutes**, ending with the never-roll-your-own rule and what it costs to break it.\nReference shelf (module sources): **OWASP Cheat Sheet Series** (https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/) — crypto and password-storage guidance. **NIST Cybersecurity Framework** (https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework) — the Protect function framing."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and crypto guidance must be verified against current standards. Algorithms, recommended work factors, and TLS versions evolve; the module teaches the decision structure (vetted primitives, proper password storage, real key management), not eternal parameters — verify specifics against the linked sources as of their retrieval dates. Never present yourself as a cryptographer; the interview line is knowing enough to choose correctly and defer to vetted implementations."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Custom crypto is dangerous because:",
     "body": "**Problem.** Custom crypto is dangerous because:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** Subtle flaws survive testing and review — even experts get it wrong; use vetted primitives\n- **Why:** Cryptographic failures are silent until exploited; peer-reviewed primitives are the only sane choice.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Cryptographic failures are silent until exploited; peer-reviewed primitives are the only sane choice.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In Cryptography in Practice:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts Cryptography in Practice interviews probe."
    }
   ],
