  {
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "How do you use MITRE ATT&CK in an interview?",
     "back": "As a shared map of adversary behavior: tactics (why — initial access, persistence, exfiltration) → techniques (how). Detection engineering maps log sources to techniques and writes detections per technique — coverage measured against the matrix, not vibes."
    },
    {
     "front": "What makes detection engineering real?",
     "back": "Detections mapped to ATT&CK techniques, built on log sources you’ve verified produce the needed telemetry, tuned for signal (precision/recall on alerts), and tested with simulated adversary activity. The interview line: ‘a detection without a tested log source is a hope.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "Walk the incident response phases.",
     "back": "Preparation → Detection & Analysis → Containment → Eradication → Recovery → Lessons Learned (NIST 800-61). The interview emphasis: contain before you eradicate (don’t tip off the adversary / destroy evidence), and lessons-learned feeds back into preparation."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you tell a breach story in an interview?",
     "back": "Blameless framing, the timeline as you understood it then, what the investigation actually showed, the action items with owners — and what you personally changed afterward. The interview line: ‘I talk about what the incident taught the system, not who failed.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "The interviewer asks you to go deep on 'ATT&CK as a map'. What answer structure makes you sound senior?",
     "back": "Three beats: (1) one-sentence definition, (2) how it works mechanically, (3) when it fits and its key tradeoff — then stop and invite the follow-up. Structure is what separates a senior answer from a recitation."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you measure detection coverage without vibes?",
     "back": "Against the ATT&CK matrix: techniques mapped to log sources mapped to tested detections — the uncovered techniques are the gaps, named explicitly. Coverage is a spreadsheet, not a feeling. The interview line: 'show me the matrix with the holes highlighted.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you discuss false positives in detection?",
     "back": "Tune with baselines, tier by fidelity, and measure precision — alert fatigue kills response. The interview line: ‘a detection nobody trusts is a decoration.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What do interviewers probe in log source verification?",
     "back": "Proving the telemetry exists before writing the rule: the event, the fields, the volume. The interview line: ‘detections are only as real as their log sources.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "A breach story should emphasize:",
     "back": "**Timeline, evidence-driven findings, and systemic fixes with owners** — Interviewers score learning and systems thinking — blame and heroics score nothing."
    },
    {
     "front": "Containment comes before eradication because:",
     "back": "**You stop the bleeding and preserve evidence before removing the threat** — Eradicating first can destroy forensic evidence and alert the adversary mid-investigation."
    },
    {
     "front": "A detection rule needs tuning because:",
     "back": "**Untuned rules alert on benign activity — alert fatigue kills the SOC’s trust** — Precision matters as much as recall: a noisy rule gets ignored, then the real alert dies in the noise."
    },
    {
     "front": "ATT&CK’s tactics vs techniques distinction is:",
     "back": "**Tactics are adversary goals; techniques are how they achieve them** — The hierarchy lets defenders map ‘what could happen’ to ‘what we’d see’ systematically."
    }
   ],
