   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **MITRE ATT&CK** — the shared map of adversary behavior: tactics (why — initial access, persistence, exfiltration) → techniques (how).\n- **Detection engineering** — detections mapped to ATT&CK techniques, built on verified log sources, tuned for precision/recall, tested with simulated adversary activity.\n- **Log sources** — the telemetry that matters: auth logs, process execution, network flows, DNS, cloud audit trails — each verified to produce what the detection needs.\n- **Precision vs recall** — precision: alerts that fire are real. Recall: real attacks fire alerts. Untuned rules destroy SOC trust.\n- **NIST 800-61 phases** — Preparation → Detection & Analysis → Containment → Eradication → Recovery → Lessons Learned.\n- **Contain before eradicate** — stop the bleeding and preserve evidence before removing the threat; eradicating first can tip off the adversary and destroy forensics."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Build one detection, end to end, on paper — the detection-engineering drill.\n1. **Pick the technique.** From ATT&CK: e.g. credential dumping — tactic: credential access. Write the technique ID from the framework.\n2. **Verify the log source.** Which log would show it (process execution, auth logs)? A detection without a tested log source is a hope — state what you'd check first.\n3. **Write the rule logic.** The event pattern in plain language: 'process X accessing LSASS memory outside approved tooling' — precise enough to test.\n4. **Tune for precision.** Name the benign activity that would false-positive, and the exclusion or threshold that handles it — 'untuned rules get ignored, then the real alert dies in the noise.'\n5. **Plan the test.** Simulated adversary activity that should fire the rule: the test is part of the detection.\n6. **Write the response play.** On fire: contain (isolate host, preserve memory/disk), then eradicate, then recover — contain before eradicate, always."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: a detection-engineering brief plus a breach-story rehearsal.\n- **The detection brief**: ATT&CK technique, log source verification, rule logic, tuning rationale (precision/recall), and the adversary-simulation test.\n- **Coverage map**: three techniques across three tactics, each mapped to its log source — coverage measured against the matrix, not vibes.\n- **The breach story**: a sanitized incident told blameless — timeline as understood then, what investigation showed, action items with owners, what you personally changed.\n- **The IR phase card**: the six NIST phases on one page, with the contain-before-eradicate rule highlighted.\n- **Present the detection in 10 minutes**, then tell the breach story in 5 — and take the follow-up questions.\nReference shelf (module sources): **MITRE ATT&CK** (https://attack.mitre.org/) — the tactic/technique matrix. **CISA Resources & Tools** (https://www.cisa.gov/resources-tools) — incident-response guidance."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and paper detections are not SOC experience. Real detection engineering is tuned against months of real telemetry and real adversary activity; the module teaches the method — map to ATT&CK, verify the source, tune, test. Never present exercise detections as production SOC work. ATT&CK evolves with adversary behavior; verify technique details against the linked framework as of its retrieval date."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: A breach story should emphasize:",
     "body": "**Problem.** A breach story should emphasize:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** Timeline, evidence-driven findings, and systemic fixes with owners\n- **Why:** Interviewers score learning and systems thinking — blame and heroics score nothing.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Interviewers score learning and systems thinking — blame and heroics score nothing.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In Detection and Response:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts Detection and Response interviews probe."
    }
   ],
