   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "How do you run a STRIDE threat model quickly?",
     "back": "Per component/data flow, ask: Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, Denial of service, Elevation of privilege. Enumerate threats, rank by risk, assign mitigations with owners. The interview line: ‘threat modeling is structured pessimism with a spreadsheet.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you review a sample architecture in an interview?",
     "back": "Systematically: trust boundaries, data flows, failure modes, scaling story, cost drivers — then findings risk-ranked with concrete fixes. The interview line: ‘I review like an attacker and report like a consultant.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you design authentication for a new service?",
     "back": "Threat-model first (what are we protecting from whom), then: phishing-resistant MFA where it matters, short-lived tokens, secure session handling, rate-limited login, and secrets in a manager — never in config. The interview line: ‘auth design starts from the threat model, not the library.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you present security findings like a consultant?",
     "back": "Risk-ranked, evidence-backed, and actionable: finding → impact in business terms → likelihood → concrete remediation with effort estimate. Lead with the criticals, never with a 200-item undifferentiated list."
    },
    {
     "front": "The interviewer asks you to go deep on 'Threat modeling: STRIDE lite'. What answer structure makes you sound senior?",
     "back": "Three beats: (1) one-sentence definition, (2) how it works mechanically, (3) when it fits and its key tradeoff — then stop and invite the follow-up. Structure is what separates a senior answer from a recitation."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you threat-model when you only have 10 minutes?",
     "back": "STRIDE-lite on the highest-risk data flow only: enumerate fast, rank by obvious impact, mitigate the top 3 with owners. A partial model of the riskiest flow beats a complete model of nothing. The interview line: 'threat modeling is triage with a threat vocabulary.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you discuss trust boundaries in a design review?",
     "back": "Every crossing is an attack surface: validate at the boundary, never trust the caller. The interview line: ‘the boundary is where the threat model lives.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What makes a security finding actionable?",
     "back": "Risk-ranked, evidence-backed, with a concrete fix and effort estimate — written for the builder, not the auditor. The interview line: ‘findings ship when the fix is obvious.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "A security review should lead with:",
     "back": "**Risk-ranked criticals with business impact and concrete fixes** — Decision-makers need prioritization: what burns first, what it costs, how to fix it."
    },
    {
     "front": "Short-lived access tokens limit damage because:",
     "back": "**A stolen token is useful only briefly — the exposure window shrinks** — Token lifetime bounds the value of theft; refresh tokens (rotated, bound) handle continuity."
    },
    {
     "front": "Leading the review with trust boundaries is strong because:",
     "back": "**Security architecture is about where untrusted data crosses — boundaries frame everything** — Every finding lives at a boundary; mapping them first organizes the review."
    },
    {
     "front": "In STRIDE, ‘Repudiation’ threats are about:",
     "back": "**An attacker denying their actions — fixed with non-repudiation (audit logs, signatures)** — Without tamper-evident logs, attackers can plausibly deny what they did."
    }
   ],
