   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **STRIDE** — Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, Denial of service, Elevation of privilege: the per-component, per-data-flow threat enumeration.\n- **Trust boundary** — where untrusted data crosses: every finding lives at a boundary; map them first.\n- **Threat model** — structured pessimism with a spreadsheet: enumerate threats, rank by risk, assign mitigations with owners.\n- **Repudiation** — attackers denying their actions: countered with non-repudiation (tamper-evident audit logs, signatures).\n- **Short-lived tokens** — stolen tokens are useful only briefly: lifetime bounds the value of theft; rotated, bound refresh tokens handle continuity.\n- **Security review document** — finding → business impact → likelihood → concrete remediation with effort estimate: risk-ranked, criticals first.\n- **Consultant presentation** — evidence-backed, actionable, business-termed: never a 200-item undifferentiated list."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Run a STRIDE-lite review of a sample architecture in 40 minutes.\n1. **Draw the data flows.** Components and the arrows between them; mark every trust boundary — 'boundaries frame everything.'\n2. **Enumerate per flow.** For each arrow, ask the six STRIDE questions. Write every threat, even the unlikely ones — ranking comes next.\n3. **Rank by risk.** Impact × likelihood in plain language; the top 5 are the review's headline.\n4. **Assign mitigations.** Per top threat: the control, its owner, and the effort — 'structured pessimism with a spreadsheet.'\n5. **Design the auth.** Threat-model first, then: phishing-resistant MFA where it matters, short-lived tokens, secure sessions, rate-limited login, secrets in a manager.\n6. **Write the review doc.** Finding → business impact → likelihood → fix with effort; criticals first. Present the top 3 in 5 minutes, like a consultant."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: the full security design review — the artifact security interviews revolve around.\n- **The threat model**: STRIDE-lite table per data flow, risk-ranked, mitigations with owners.\n- **The architecture review**: trust boundaries, data flows, failure modes, scaling story — findings risk-ranked with concrete fixes.\n- **The auth design**: for a new service, from the threat model — MFA, token lifetimes, session handling, rate limits, secrets.\n- **The review document**: consultant-style, criticals first, business impact in business terms.\n- **The 5-minute presentation**: top 3 findings, defended against pushback.\nReference shelf (module sources): **OWASP** (https://owasp.org/) — Top 10 and project pages. **HashiCorp Vault Documentation** (https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs) — secrets engines and dynamic credentials."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and a classroom review is not a professional security assessment. Real reviews involve stakeholder negotiation, incomplete information, and liability that no exercise carries; the module teaches the review structure and the consultant's reporting discipline. Never present it as client assessment experience. Threat landscapes evolve; verify current guidance against the linked sources as of their retrieval dates."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: A security review should lead with:",
     "body": "**Problem.** A security review should lead with:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** Risk-ranked criticals with business impact and concrete fixes\n- **Why:** Decision-makers need prioritization: what burns first, what it costs, how to fix it.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Decision-makers need prioritization: what burns first, what it costs, how to fix it.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In Secure Design Review:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts Secure Design Review interviews probe."
    }
