   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "How do you handle security mock rounds?",
     "back": "Secure design: threat-model aloud (STRIDE-lite), name mitigations per threat. Attacker mindset: think in exploit primitives from the code/architecture. Incident response: contain before eradicate, preserve evidence. The interview line: ‘I show the thinking, not just the answer.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you tell a breach story in an interview?",
     "back": "Blameless framing, the timeline as you understood it then, what the investigation actually showed, the action items with owners — and what you personally changed afterward. The interview line: ‘I talk about what the incident taught the system, not who failed.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What goes in a security engineer’s portfolio?",
     "back": "Writeups that show method: a threat model, a code-review finding with the fix, a detection rule with its tuning rationale, a post-incident review — sanitized, of course. The interview line: ‘I show how I think, not just what I found.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What’s the final two-week taper?",
     "back": "Stop learning new topics; redrill weak patterns from your debrief log, run full timed mocks, fix sleep, and rehearse your story bank aloud. The taper converts knowledge into performance — cramming new material now only adds noise."
    },
    {
     "front": "The interviewer asks you to go deep on 'Mock secure-design round'. What answer structure makes you sound senior?",
     "back": "Three beats: (1) one-sentence definition, (2) how it works mechanically, (3) when it fits and its key tradeoff — then stop and invite the follow-up. Structure is what separates a senior answer from a recitation."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you answer 'have you ever been breached?' honestly?",
     "back": "With the blameless story structure: what happened, the timeline as you understood it then, what the investigation actually showed, the systemic fixes with owners — and what you personally changed afterward. Never name others' failures; talk about what the incident taught the system. The interview line: 'I talk about what the incident taught the system, not who failed.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you prep the attacker-mindset round?",
     "back": "Think in abuse cases: how would you break this feature? Then flip to mitigations. The interview line: ‘attack first, defend second — in that order.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What does the secure-design mock score?",
     "back": "Threat modeling aloud, mitigations per threat, and tradeoff honesty — the STRIDE-lite walkthrough. The interview line: ‘the model is the message.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "The taper period’s rule is:",
     "back": "**No new material — redrill weak spots, mock timed, rest** — Performance comes from consolidation and recovery, not last-minute breadth."
    },
    {
     "front": "A portfolio writeup’s most valuable element is:",
     "back": "**The reasoning: how you found it, why it matters, how you’d fix and verify it** — Method transfers across jobs; any single finding doesn’t."
    },
    {
     "front": "A breach story should emphasize:",
     "back": "**Timeline, evidence-driven findings, and systemic fixes with owners** — Interviewers score learning and systems thinking — blame and heroics score nothing."
    },
    {
     "front": "Thinking aloud in security rounds is graded because:",
     "back": "**Security judgment is the product — the reasoning is what’s being evaluated** — Two candidates can name the same control; the reasoning separates them."
    }
   ],
