   "handsOnLab": {
    "id": "security-eng-interview-prep--sec-mock--lab",
    "title": "Operator's brief: Security Mock Circuit",
    "brief": "You will write the artifacts a real operator would produce for Security Mock Circuit — incident review, SLO spec, and tradeoff analysis — because interviews probe the seams.",
    "steps": [
     "Write a blameless incident review for a realistic Security Mock Circuit failure: timeline, impact in numbers, detection, response, root cause, action items with owners.",
     "Specify the SLOs: indicators, objectives, error budget, and what consumes the budget — with the math shown.",
     "Draw the architecture and annotate every tradeoff: what you chose, what you gave up, why it was worth it.",
     "Write the runbook excerpt: the alert, the first five diagnostic commands, the escalation path.",
     "Prepare the war story: a three-minute telling with numbers, your decisions, and what you'd do differently.",
     "Peer-review the brief as a hiring manager: which claims have evidence, which are buzzwords?"
    ],
    "deliverable": "incident-review.md, slo-spec.md (with math), architecture tradeoffs, runbook excerpt, and war-story script for Security Mock Circuit.",
    "checkpoint": "Incident review has numbers and owned action items; SLO math is shown; every architecture choice names its tradeoff; war story runs three minutes."
   },
   "id": "security-eng-interview-prep--sec-mock",
   "mockTask": {
    "id": "security-eng-interview-prep--sec-mock--mock",
    "type": "choice",
    "q": "A portfolio writeup’s most valuable element is:",
    "choices": [
     "The reasoning: how you found it, why it matters, how you’d fix and verify it",
     "Screenshots",
     "The vulnerability’s severity",
     "Length"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explain": "Method transfers across jobs; any single finding doesn’t."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **Secure-design round** — threat-model aloud (STRIDE-lite), name mitigations per threat: show the thinking, not just the answer.\n- **Attacker-mindset round** — think in exploit primitives from the code/architecture: where would you strike, and why there.\n- **Incident-response round** — contain before eradicate, preserve evidence: the defender's discipline under time.\n- **Breach story (behavioral)** — blameless framing, timeline as understood then, evidence-driven findings, systemic fixes with owners, what you personally changed.\n- **Security portfolio** — writeups that show method: a threat model, a code-review finding with the fix, a detection with its tuning rationale, a post-incident review — sanitized.\n- **Thinking aloud** — security judgment is the product; the reasoning is what's evaluated."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Run the security mock circuit with a partner, timed.\n1. **Secure-design round (40 min).** Given a sample architecture: STRIDE-lite aloud, mitigations per threat, the review doc's top 3 — narrated.\n2. **Attacker-mindset round (25 min).** Given sample code: think in exploit primitives — injection, auth gaps, SSRF — and rank targets by exploitability.\n3. **Incident-response round (25 min).** A seeded breach: contain (isolate, preserve evidence) before eradicate — narrate the phase discipline.\n4. **Behavioral round (20 min).** The breach story: blameless, timeline, systemic fixes, personal change — then the portfolio walkthrough.\n5. **Score it.** Design rigor, attacker creativity, IR discipline, story honesty, communication — name the lowest axis.\n6. **Write the debrief.** The weakest round's category, the redrill plan, and one portfolio piece to write this week."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: a security interview dossier.\n- **One threat-model writeup**, **one code-review finding with fix**, **one detection with tuning rationale** — sanitized, method-forward.\n- **The breach story**, written then rehearsed aloud in 5 minutes — blameless, evidence-driven.\n- **Mock score sheets** for all three technical rounds plus behavioral, with the weakest axis's redrill plan.\n- **The portfolio index**: what each piece demonstrates, and the story you'd tell walking through it.\n- **The taper note**: no new topics in the final two weeks — redrill, mock, rest.\nReference shelf (module sources): **OWASP** (https://owasp.org/). **MITRE ATT&CK** (https://attack.mitre.org/)."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and mock rounds are not real security work. Portfolio pieces must be sanitized and truthful: writeups of exercises are labeled as exercises, real findings stay confidential, and breach stories never name employers' incidents without permission. Fabricated security experience collapses under follow-up questions — and in security, credibility is the whole interview. Frameworks evolve; verify current OWASP and ATT&CK content against the linked sources as of their retrieval dates."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: The taper period’s rule is:",
     "body": "**Problem.** The taper period’s rule is:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** No new material — redrill weak spots, mock timed, rest\n- **Why:** Performance comes from consolidation and recovery, not last-minute breadth.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Performance comes from consolidation and recovery, not last-minute breadth.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In Security Mock Circuit:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts Security Mock Circuit interviews probe."
    }
   ],
