   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "When do you reach for two pointers?",
     "back": "Ordered data and pair-finding: opposite ends for sorted two-sum or container-with-most-water; same-direction fast/slow pointers for cycle detection or in-place dedup — O(n) time, O(1) space. Name which variant and why."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you speed-rep DSA fundamentals?",
     "back": "Pattern batches under time: trees/graphs/heaps one session (traversals, BFS/DFS, top-k), DP basics the next (1D ladder, knapsack shapes) — always stating complexity aloud. The interview line: ‘speed reps build the reflex; the debrief builds the skill.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you handle the 30-minute coding bar?",
     "back": "Budget aloud: 5 min clarify + plan, 20 min code, 5 min test. Start with brute force stated and its complexity (it buys time and shows honesty), then optimize the bottleneck. A working brute force beats a half-written optimal."
    },
    {
     "front": "Why say complexity out loud?",
     "back": "Because the interviewer can’t grade silent thought: stating ‘this is O(n) time, O(1) space because…’ at each step turns reasoning into evidence. It’s also your early-warning system — saying it forces you to check it."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you review wrong answers productively?",
     "back": "Classify the miss: wrong pattern, bug in implementation, complexity fumble, or time management — then redrill the class, not just the problem. The interview line: ‘I fix the category, not the instance.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you choose between BFS and DFS in an interview?",
     "back": "BFS for shortest path in unweighted graphs and level-order needs; DFS for connectivity, cycles, topological shapes, and when recursion depth is safe. State the choice and its complexity before coding — the choice is part of the graded reasoning. The interview line: 'BFS for distance, DFS for structure.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "What does a DSA speed-rep session look like?",
     "back": "Pattern batches under time: one pattern family per session, timed solves, miss-category logging. The interview line: ‘reps build pattern recognition; the timer builds the interview.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you review a timed solve that was correct but slow?",
     "back": "Find the hesitation point: recognition, implementation, or testing — then drill that phase. The interview line: ‘slow correct beats fast wrong, but the clock still counts.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "Redrilling the miss category (not just the problem) works because:",
     "back": "**Interviews test categories under new disguises — the instance never repeats** — The next problem will wear a different costume; the category is what transfers."
    },
    {
     "front": "Stating complexity aloud during coding:",
     "back": "**Makes your reasoning gradable and forces self-checks** — Verbalized reasoning is scorable reasoning — and it catches your own mistakes mid-stream."
    },
    {
     "front": "Stating the brute force first is strong because:",
     "back": "**It shows honest complexity reasoning and gives a working fallback while you optimize** — A correct slow solution on the board beats a blank editor; optimization then has a baseline."
    },
    {
     "front": "Speed reps should always include stating complexity aloud because:",
     "back": "**The interview grades the analysis as well as the code — rehearse both together** — Rehearsing code without narration trains half the interview."
    }
   ],
