   "id": "tech-mock-interview-circuit--mock-dsa",
   "mockTask": {
    "id": "tech-mock-interview-circuit--mock-dsa--mock",
    "type": "choice",
    "q": "Stating complexity aloud during coding:",
    "choices": [
     "Makes your reasoning gradable and forces self-checks",
     "Wastes time",
     "Is optional",
     "Slows you down"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explain": "Verbalized reasoning is scorable reasoning — and it catches your own mistakes mid-stream."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **Two pointers** — ordered data and pair-finding: opposite ends for sorted two-sum; same-direction fast/slow for cycle detection or in-place dedup. O(n) time, O(1) space.\n- **Sliding window** — a window that expands/contracts over arrays/strings: the shape for subarray and substring optima.\n- **BFS / DFS** — level-order vs depth-first traversal: BFS for shortest path in unweighted graphs, DFS for connectivity and topological shapes.\n- **Heap** — top-k and streaming medians: the 'k smallest/largest' reflex.\n- **Dynamic programming** — overlapping subproblems with optimal substructure: 1D ladder shapes, knapsack shapes — state the recurrence before coding.\n- **Complexity aloud** — 'this is O(n) time, O(1) space because…': stated at each step, it's your early-warning system and the interviewer's grading feed.\n- **The 30-minute bar** — 5 min clarify + plan, 20 min code, 5 min test: budget it aloud.\n- **Brute force first** — state it and its complexity: a working fallback that buys time and shows honesty.\n- **Miss categories** — wrong pattern, implementation bug, complexity fumble, time management: fix the category, not the instance."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Run a 45-minute DSA speed-rep session — the sport's training unit.\n1. **Pick the pattern batch.** Today: trees/graphs/heaps (traversals, BFS/DFS, top-k). Tomorrow: DP basics (1D ladder, knapsack shapes). One batch per session.\n2. **Set the timer: 30 minutes per problem.** Budget aloud before starting: 5 clarify + plan, 20 code, 5 test.\n3. **Clarify first.** Restate the problem, name the edge cases, state the pattern you suspect and why.\n4. **Brute force, stated.** Write it, state its complexity — the honest baseline — then optimize the bottleneck.\n5. **Narrate complexity at every step.** 'This loop is O(n) because…' — said out loud, every rep. Silent reps train half the interview.\n6. **Debrief by category.** For each miss: wrong pattern, bug, complexity fumble, or time — then redrill the category, not the problem."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: a personal DSA playbook — the pattern reference you'd trust in a loop.\n- **One page per pattern**: two pointers, sliding window, BFS/DFS, heaps, DP basics — the recognition triggers, the template shape, the complexity.\n- **The complexity cheat**: Big-O for the common operations, written from understanding — the analysis you state aloud.\n- **Your miss log**: every recent miss classified by category, with the redrill that fixed it.\n- **The 30-minute bar card**: the time budget, the brute-force rule, the test checklist — on one index card.\n- **Two timed sets per week**, debriefed by category, until the narration is reflex.\nReference shelf (module sources): **NeetCode** (https://neetcode.io/) — pattern-organized DSA practice for the speed reps. **Big-O Cheat Sheet** (https://www.bigocheatsheet.com/) — complexity quick-reference for the analysis-aloud drills."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and drill speed is not engineering judgment. Real work rewards choosing the right approach and maintaining it; interviews reward recognizing patterns under time. Train both honestly: the reps build the reflex, and the debrief builds the skill. Never present drill counts as experience; present the method — pattern recognition, complexity aloud, categorized redrills — which is what the reps actually teach."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Redrilling the miss category (not just the problem) works because:",
     "body": "**Problem.** Redrilling the miss category (not just the problem) works because:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** Interviews test categories under new disguises — the instance never repeats\n- **Why:** The next problem will wear a different costume; the category is what transfers.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *The next problem will wear a different costume; the category is what transfers.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In DSA Speed Reps:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts DSA Speed Reps interviews probe."
    }
   ],
