  {
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "How do you open a system design round?",
     "back": "Requirements before architecture: restate functional requirements (what the system does), then non-functional (scale, latency, availability, consistency). Scoping first proves you design the right system — candidates who draw boxes before asking get failed."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you do API + data-model design in a system-design rep?",
     "back": "Resources and verbs first, then the storage that serves the access patterns — the two designed together, since API shape constrains queries and data shape constrains the API. The interview line: ‘the API and the model are one decision, not two.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you talk about scaling levers?",
     "back": "The three moves: shard the data (split the load), cache the hot paths (skip the work), queue the spikes (defer the work). The interview line: ‘scaling is choosing which of the three each bottleneck needs.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you prep the classic designs (shortener, feed, chat)?",
     "back": "One page each: requirements, estimation, the 2–3 key decisions, the scaling story. The classics are classics because they each teach one lesson — shortener: IDs and caching; feed: fan-out; chat: real-time. The interview line: ‘I learn the lesson, not the diagram.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you do bottleneck analysis in a design round?",
     "back": "Follow the request path and ask ‘what breaks first as load grows 10x?’ at each hop — then apply the right lever (shard/cache/queue) to that hop. The interview line: ‘bottlenecks are found by walking the path, not by guessing.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you handle 'it depends' in a design round?",
     "back": "'It depends' is only a good answer when you then explain what it depends on: the workload's read/write ratio, consistency needs, and scale decide between the options — so name the deciding factors and pick for the stated case. The interview line: 'it depends on three things; here's how each would change my answer.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you prep the classic designs efficiently?",
     "back": "One page each: requirements, estimation, the 2–3 key decisions, the scaling story — the classics are patterns, not scripts. The interview line: ‘prep the decisions, not the diagrams.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What does bottleneck analysis sound like aloud?",
     "back": "‘At 10x, the database writes saturate first — so I’d shard by user_id and cache the hot reads.’ The interview line: ‘name the first thing that breaks.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "Labeling arrows with what flows matters because:",
     "back": "**Unlabeled arrows hide the data story — the labels are where the design lives** — Architecture is data in motion; unlabeled arrows are just boxes."
    },
    {
     "front": "Asking ‘what breaks first at 10x’ is the right question because:",
     "back": "**It forces ordered thinking about limits instead of vague ‘it scales’ claims** — Scaling claims need a named first failure; otherwise they’re wishes."
    },
    {
     "front": "Learning the lesson (not the diagram) matters because:",
     "back": "**Interviewers vary the problem — the transferable insight is what repeats** — The next question won’t be the shortener; the ID-generation insight will recur."
    },
    {
     "front": "Queues handle spikes while caches handle hot data because:",
     "back": "**Queues defer work in time; caches skip work entirely — different bottlenecks, different tools** — Time-shifting vs work-avoidance: match the lever to the bottleneck’s nature."
    }
   ],
