   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **Functional requirements** — what the system does: the features, the APIs, the behaviors.\n- **Non-functional requirements** — how well: scale, latency, availability, consistency — the constraints that shape architecture.\n- **Back-of-envelope estimation** — requests/sec, storage, bandwidth from stated assumptions: the arithmetic before the boxes.\n- **Sharding** — splitting data across nodes: the lever for data too big or hot for one machine.\n- **Caching** — skipping work for hot paths: the lever for read-heavy, slow-changing data, with an invalidation plan.\n- **Queues** — deferring work in time: the lever for spikes and async processing.\n- **Bottleneck analysis** — walking the request path asking 'what breaks first at 10×?' and applying the right lever per hop.\n- **The classic lessons** — shortener: IDs and caching; feed: fan-out; chat: real-time. Learn the lesson, not the diagram."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Do the back-of-envelope for a URL shortener — the estimation interviewers watch most closely.\n1. **State the assumptions.** 100M URLs shortened per month; 10:1 read/write ratio. Write them — assumptions are gradable.\n2. **Writes.** 100M ÷ 30 days ≈ 40 writes/sec average; plan for 10× peak: 400 writes/sec.\n3. **Reads.** 10 × 100M = 1B reads/month ≈ 400 reads/sec average; 4,000/sec at peak.\n4. **Storage.** 100M URLs × ~500 bytes metadata ≈ 50 GB — trivial for one database; say so, and say why that simplifies the design.\n5. **Bandwidth.** 4,000 reads/sec × ~500 bytes ≈ 2 MB/sec — the network is not the bottleneck; the ID generation and cache are the design.\n6. **Name the lesson.** 'The estimation says: this is a caching and ID-generation problem wearing a URL-shortener costume.'"
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: three classic design one-pagers — shortener, feed, chat.\n- **Each one-pager**: requirements (functional + non-functional), the worked estimation, the 2–3 key decisions, the scaling story, the bottleneck walk.\n- **The labeled diagram**: every arrow labeled with what flows — 'the labels are where the design lives.'\n- **The lesson line** per classic: what this design teaches that transfers to the next question.\n- **The follow-up bank**: the five hardest questions per design, answered in writing.\n- **Rehearse each aloud in 30 minutes**, then have a partner attack the bottlenecks.\nReference shelf (module sources): **System Design Primer** (https://github.com/donnemartin/system-design-primer) — the classic designs and tradeoffs. **AWS Well-Architected Framework** (https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/framework/welcome.html) — the architecture interview's rubric."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and the worked numbers are illustrative estimates. Real systems have messier requirements and the estimation assumptions here are stated, not factual — never quote them as real traffic figures. What transfers is the estimation method and the lesson-per-classic discipline. Design reps train interview reasoning, not production architecture judgment; present them as rehearsed method, which is exactly what design rounds grade."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Labeling arrows with what flows matters because:",
     "body": "**Problem.** Labeling arrows with what flows matters because:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** Unlabeled arrows hide the data story — the labels are where the design lives\n- **Why:** Architecture is data in motion; unlabeled arrows are just boxes.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Architecture is data in motion; unlabeled arrows are just boxes.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In System Design Reps:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts System Design Reps interviews probe."
    }
   ],
