   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "What’s the STAR structure, and why does it work for behavioral rounds?",
     "back": "Situation (2 sentences of context), Task (your responsibility), Action (what YOU did — the bulk), Result (measurable outcome + lesson). It works because interviewers score evidence of behavior, and STAR forces a complete evidence unit."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you cover conflict, failure, and leadership stories?",
     "back": "One banked STAR story each: conflict (disagreement handled professionally), failure (owned, with the fix), leadership (influence without authority counts). The interview line: ‘I bank all three before the loop — the questions are predictable.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you answer ‘Why this company?’ convincingly?",
     "back": "Specific, researched, and tied to you: their product/technical challenges, something you learned from an employee/blog/post, and how your background maps to their problems. Generic flattery fails; specifics signal genuine interest."
    },
    {
     "front": "What questions should you ask the interviewer?",
     "back": "Ones that reveal the job’s reality: ‘what does success look like in 6 months?’, ‘what’s the hardest technical problem the team faces?’, ‘how are on-call and incidents handled?’, ‘why is this role open?’ Never ask what the website answers."
    },
    {
     "front": "Why practice behavioral answers aloud — and record them?",
     "back": "Aloud reveals pacing, filler, and rambling that silent rehearsal hides; recording lets you review like an interviewer. The interview line: ‘my first recording is always 40% too long — that’s what the practice is for.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you keep a STAR story under 2 minutes?",
     "back": "Two sentences of situation, one of task, the bulk on your actions — and the result in one sentence plus the lesson. Everything else is cut in rehearsal, which is why you record: the timer is the editor. The interview line: 'my first recording is always 40% too long — that's what the practice is for.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you handle the weakness question?",
     "back": "A real, non-fatal weakness plus the system you built to manage it — growth with evidence. The interview line: ‘the weakness is the setup; the system is the answer.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you prep stories for SRE/DevOps behavioral rounds?",
     "back": "On-call and incident stories: the page, the decision, the blameless lesson — ownership under pressure. The interview line: ‘the pager stories are the portfolio.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "Recording yourself works because:",
     "back": "**You hear what the interviewer hears — pacing and filler invisible from inside** — Self-perception lies about delivery; the recording doesn’t."
    },
    {
     "front": "The strongest interviewer question reveals:",
     "back": "**The role’s reality: success criteria, hard problems, team health** — You’re interviewing them too: these answers predict your actual day-to-day."
    },
    {
     "front": "‘Why this company?’ fails when the answer:",
     "back": "**Could apply to any company — no specifics about them** — Interchangeable answers reveal no research; specifics prove interest."
    },
    {
     "front": "Banking all three story types before the loop works because:",
     "back": "**Behavioral questions are predictable — preparation converts them to free points** — The question set barely varies; rehearsed stories answer them all."
    }
   ],
