   "handsOnLab": {
    "id": "tech-mock-interview-circuit--mock-behavioral--lab",
    "title": "Operator's brief: The Behavioral Story Bank",
    "brief": "You will write the artifacts a real operator would produce for The Behavioral Story Bank — incident review, SLO spec, and tradeoff analysis — because interviews probe the seams.",
    "steps": [
     "Write a blameless incident review for a realistic The Behavioral Story Bank failure: timeline, impact in numbers, detection, response, root cause, action items with owners.",
     "Specify the SLOs: indicators, objectives, error budget, and what consumes the budget — with the math shown.",
     "Draw the architecture and annotate every tradeoff: what you chose, what you gave up, why it was worth it.",
     "Write the runbook excerpt: the alert, the first five diagnostic commands, the escalation path.",
     "Prepare the war story: a three-minute telling with numbers, your decisions, and what you'd do differently.",
     "Peer-review the brief as a hiring manager: which claims have evidence, which are buzzwords?"
    ],
    "deliverable": "incident-review.md, slo-spec.md (with math), architecture tradeoffs, runbook excerpt, and war-story script for The Behavioral Story Bank.",
    "checkpoint": "Incident review has numbers and owned action items; SLO math is shown; every architecture choice names its tradeoff; war story runs three minutes."
   },
   "id": "tech-mock-interview-circuit--mock-behavioral",
   "mockTask": {
    "id": "tech-mock-interview-circuit--mock-behavioral--mock",
    "type": "choice",
    "q": "The strongest interviewer question reveals:",
    "choices": [
     "Salary immediately",
     "Their personal life",
     "Stock price",
     "The role’s reality: success criteria, hard problems, team health"
    ],
    "answer": 3,
    "explain": "You’re interviewing them too: these answers predict your actual day-to-day."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **STAR** — Situation (2 sentences of context), Task (your responsibility), Action (what YOU did — the bulk), Result (measurable outcome + lesson): the complete evidence unit.\n- **Story bank** — ten rehearsed STAR stories covering conflict, failure, leadership, ownership, and learning: banked before the loop.\n- **Conflict story** — a disagreement handled professionally: what each side wanted, what decided it, what you did.\n- **Failure story** — owned, with the fix: what broke, your part, what changed afterward.\n- **Leadership story** — influence without authority counts: the outcome you drove through others.\n- **'Why this company'** — specific, researched, tied to you: their product and challenges, something you learned, how your background maps. Generic flattery fails.\n- **Recording review** — aloud reveals pacing, filler, and rambling that silent rehearsal hides: 'my first recording is always 40% too long.'"
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Build the story bank in one focused weekend.\n1. **List ten prompts.** Conflict, failure, leadership, ownership, disagreement, mistake, tight deadline, difficult stakeholder, mentoring, proudest work.\n2. **Draft each in STAR.** Two sentences of situation, your task, the bulk on YOUR actions and decisions, a measurable result plus the lesson.\n3. **Time each to 2 minutes.** Behavioral answers ramble past 3; the timer is the editor.\n4. **Record all ten aloud.** Listen back like an interviewer: cut filler, cut throat-clearing, keep the decisions.\n5. **Write 'why this company' with specifics.** Their product, a technical challenge, something from an employee or post, your mapping to it — interchangeable answers fail.\n6. **Prepare your questions.** The four reality-revealing questions; practice hearing team health in the answers."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: the behavioral dossier.\n- **Ten STAR stories**, each timed to 2 minutes, recorded and trimmed — the bank.\n- **The 'why this company' page**: specifics per target company, refreshed before each loop.\n- **Your interviewer questions** with notes on what good vs worrying answers sound like.\n- **The recording log**: date, story, length, and the one cut you made — proof of the practice.\n- **One full behavioral mock** with a partner, scored on evidence completeness and concision.\nReference shelf (module sources): **Indeed Career Guide: Interviewing** (https://www.indeed.com/career-advice) — STAR format and preparation checklists."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and rehearsed stories must stay truthful. Never invent employers, outcomes, or heroics: interviewers probe stories with follow-ups, and fabricated ones collapse — in behavioral rounds, credibility is the entire evaluation. Rehearse real experience until it sounds unrehearsed; that's the whole game. Bank stories from your actual work; if an experience is thin, say what you learned, not what you wish had happened."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Recording yourself works because:",
     "body": "**Problem.** Recording yourself works because:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** You hear what the interviewer hears — pacing and filler invisible from inside\n- **Why:** Self-perception lies about delivery; the recording doesn’t.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Self-perception lies about delivery; the recording doesn’t.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In The Behavioral Story Bank:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts The Behavioral Story Bank interviews probe."
    }
   ],
