   "handsOnLab": {
    "id": "tech-mock-interview-circuit--mock-domains--lab",
    "title": "Operator's brief: Domain Deep Dives",
    "brief": "You will write the artifacts a real operator would produce for Domain Deep Dives — incident review, SLO spec, and tradeoff analysis — because interviews probe the seams.",
    "steps": [
     "Write a blameless incident review for a realistic Domain Deep Dives failure: timeline, impact in numbers, detection, response, root cause, action items with owners.",
     "Specify the SLOs: indicators, objectives, error budget, and what consumes the budget — with the math shown.",
     "Draw the architecture and annotate every tradeoff: what you chose, what you gave up, why it was worth it.",
     "Write the runbook excerpt: the alert, the first five diagnostic commands, the escalation path.",
     "Prepare the war story: a three-minute telling with numbers, your decisions, and what you'd do differently.",
     "Peer-review the brief as a hiring manager: which claims have evidence, which are buzzwords?"
    ],
    "deliverable": "incident-review.md, slo-spec.md (with math), architecture tradeoffs, runbook excerpt, and war-story script for Domain Deep Dives.",
    "checkpoint": "Incident review has numbers and owned action items; SLO math is shown; every architecture choice names its tradeoff; war story runs three minutes."
   },
   "id": "tech-mock-interview-circuit--mock-domains",
   "mockTask": {
    "id": "tech-mock-interview-circuit--mock-domains--mock",
    "type": "choice",
    "q": "Reading a JD as an exam means:",
    "choices": [
     "Applying everywhere",
     "Turning each bullet into predicted interview questions with prepared stories",
     "Ignoring it",
     "Memorizing it"
    ],
    "answer": 1,
    "explain": "Interviewers probe what’s on the JD; each requirement maps to questions you can pre-answer."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **Domain deep dive** — the round that probes one level deeper than the job post: mechanism, tradeoffs, and judgment on your claimed expertise.\n- **One level deeper** — for each listed skill: the mechanism, the tradeoff, and a story. 'I prepare the layer below the buzzword.'\n- **JD as exam** — every bullet a predicted question: 'led migration to K8s' → 'walk me through that migration's hardest decision.'\n- **Teach-back test** — explain it as if to a junior: if you can't produce a clear, ordered explanation with an example, you don't know it deeply enough.\n- **Tradeoff question** — every technique has a cost: what you gain, what you pay, when you'd choose the alternative.\n- **Blank-moment recovery** — state what you know precisely, name the boundary, reason from first principles aloud: honesty plus reasoning beats confident fabrication."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Run a domain deep-dive prep session for your target role.\n1. **Extract the JD's bullets.** Each becomes a predicted question with a prepared story and a deep-dive — the JD is the question list in disguise.\n2. **Go one level deeper per bullet.** Mechanism, tradeoff, story. For 'Kubernetes': how the scheduler places pods, what breaks at scale, your hardest K8s decision.\n3. **Teach-back each.** Explain aloud as if to a junior, with an example. Gaps you feel are gaps to fill.\n4. **Pre-answer the tradeoff.** For every technique: what you gain, what you pay, when you'd choose the alternative — before the interviewer asks.\n5. **Drill the blank moment.** Partner asks something you don't know; practice: 'here's what I do know precisely; here's the boundary; here's how I'd reason about it.'\n6. **Log the gaps.** Every teach-back stumble becomes a study card; the log aims every hour at real weakness."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: the deep-dive dossier for your target role.\n- **The JD exam**: each bullet mapped to predicted questions, with your story and deep-dive per bullet.\n- **One-level-deeper sheets**: mechanism, tradeoff, and story for the top 8 skills — teach-back ready.\n- **The tradeoff bank**: the cost side of every technique you claim, in your own words.\n- **The gap log**: teach-back stumbles converted to study cards, worked until the explanation is clean.\n- **One full deep-dive mock**: 45 minutes on your domain, follow-ups until you hit the boundary — then the honest recovery, practiced.\nReference shelf (module sources): **AWS Documentation** (https://docs.aws.amazon.com/) — service primitives for depth. **OWASP** (https://owasp.org/) — security depth for the security domain."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and depth must be honest depth. The blank-moment recovery exists because fabrication is the worst strategy: interviewers score honesty plus reasoning, and a precise partial answer with clear boundaries beats a confident invention every time. Claim only the depth you have; the dossier's job is to make your real depth interview-ready, not to manufacture depth you don't."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: The teach-back test works because:",
     "body": "**Problem.** The teach-back test works because:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** Producing a clear explanation exposes gaps that recognition hides\n- **Why:** Recognition ≠ recall ≠ teaching; each level demands deeper organization of knowledge.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Recognition ≠ recall ≠ teaching; each level demands deeper organization of knowledge.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In Domain Deep Dives:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts Domain Deep Dives interviews probe."
    }
   ],
