  {
   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "How do you simulate a full interview loop?",
     "back": "Four timed rounds back-to-back (coding, design, domain deep-dive, behavioral) with a partner playing interviewer, scored against a rubric, then a weakness-targeting week on the lowest scores. The point: stamina and context-switching are skills you train."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you warm up on loop day morning?",
     "back": "One easy problem you have solved before (confidence, not learning), your story bank skimmed aloud once, and logistics handled the night before. No new topics — the morning is about activation, not acquisition. The interview line: ‘loop day is a performance; the rehearsal ended last night.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you score yourself against an interview rubric?",
     "back": "Fixed axes per round type: correctness, complexity analysis, communication, time management, tradeoffs named. Score honestly after every mock; the rubric turns feelings into data. The interview line: ‘I grade myself the way they’ll grade me.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What’s a weakness-targeting week?",
     "back": "One week between full-loop simulation and the real loop, spent only on the lowest rubric scores: targeted drills, one more mock per weak area, then rest. The interview line: ‘the week has one job — raise the floor.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What’s the final two-week taper?",
     "back": "Stop learning new topics; redrill weak patterns from your debrief log, run full timed mocks, fix sleep, and rehearse your story bank aloud. The taper converts knowledge into performance — cramming new material now only adds noise."
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you reset between rounds on loop day?",
     "back": "Fifteen minutes, same ritual: water, notes closed, one breath — the last round's mistakes don't get a vote in this one. Context-switching is a trained skill, and the reset is the training. The interview line: 'each round starts clean; I carry the rubric, not the regret.'"
    },
    {
     "front": "How do you review a full-loop simulation?",
     "back": "Score each round against its rubric, find the lowest axis, and spend the week there — the loop is only as strong as its weakest round. The interview line: ‘the simulation finds the leak; the week fixes it.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "What goes on the loop-day packing list?",
     "back": "Logistics confirmed, story bank skimmed, weak rounds redrilled, sleep protected — the 48 hours are choreography. The interview line: ‘loop day is won the week before.’"
    },
    {
     "front": "The highest-value activity right after a mock is:",
     "back": "**A written debrief naming what leaked, plus a redrill plan** — Improvement comes from the feedback loop: name the leak, target the redrill, verify next time."
    },
    {
     "front": "The taper period’s rule is:",
     "back": "**No new material — redrill weak spots, mock timed, rest** — Performance comes from consolidation and recovery, not last-minute breadth."
    },
    {
     "front": "Targeting the floor (not the ceiling) is right because:",
     "back": "**Offers are lost on weak rounds, not won on strong ones — the minimum decides** — Interview loops grade the weakest link; raising it raises the whole loop."
    },
    {
     "front": "Rubric scoring beats gut feeling because:",
     "back": "**It makes weaknesses comparable across mocks — triage needs data** — You can’t triage what you haven’t measured."
    }
   ],
