   "handsOnLab": {
    "id": "tech-mock-interview-circuit--mock-full-loop--lab",
    "title": "Operator's brief: Full-Loop Simulation",
    "brief": "You will write the artifacts a real operator would produce for Full-Loop Simulation — incident review, SLO spec, and tradeoff analysis — because interviews probe the seams.",
    "steps": [
     "Write a blameless incident review for a realistic Full-Loop Simulation failure: timeline, impact in numbers, detection, response, root cause, action items with owners.",
     "Specify the SLOs: indicators, objectives, error budget, and what consumes the budget — with the math shown.",
     "Draw the architecture and annotate every tradeoff: what you chose, what you gave up, why it was worth it.",
     "Write the runbook excerpt: the alert, the first five diagnostic commands, the escalation path.",
     "Prepare the war story: a three-minute telling with numbers, your decisions, and what you'd do differently.",
     "Peer-review the brief as a hiring manager: which claims have evidence, which are buzzwords?"
    ],
    "deliverable": "incident-review.md, slo-spec.md (with math), architecture tradeoffs, runbook excerpt, and war-story script for Full-Loop Simulation.",
    "checkpoint": "Incident review has numbers and owned action items; SLO math is shown; every architecture choice names its tradeoff; war story runs three minutes."
   },
   "id": "tech-mock-interview-circuit--mock-full-loop",
   "mockTask": {
    "id": "tech-mock-interview-circuit--mock-full-loop--mock",
    "type": "choice",
    "q": "The taper period’s rule is:",
    "choices": [
     "Stop practicing",
     "Learn new topics",
     "Memorize solutions",
     "No new material — redrill weak spots, mock timed, rest"
    ],
    "answer": 3,
    "explain": "Performance comes from consolidation and recovery, not last-minute breadth."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "- **Loop day** — four timed rounds back-to-back (coding, design, domain deep-dive, behavioral): the stamina and context-switching test.\n- **Rotating roles** — trading interviewer and candidate with a partner: you learn the rubric from the grading side.\n- **Rubric scoring** — fixed axes per round: correctness, complexity analysis, communication, time management, tradeoffs named.\n- **Weakness-targeting week** — the week between simulation and the real loop, spent only on the lowest scores: raise the floor.\n- **Debrief template** — what leaked, its category, the redrill plan, the verification: written within the hour.\n- **Final checklist** — logistics, rest, story bank, no new topics: the last 48 hours are about the performer."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Run the full-loop simulation — the dress rehearsal.\n1. **Schedule it like a real loop.** Four 45-minute rounds, 15-minute breaks, same order as your target companies: coding, design, deep-dive, behavioral.\n2. **Brief your partner.** They interview first: timed, no pausing, think-aloud required, scoring against the rubric during the round.\n3. **Run all four.** Stamina is the skill: each round starts clean — water, notes closed, one breath.\n4. **Trade roles.** You interview them next loop: grading teaches what gradable answers look like.\n5. **Debrief within the hour.** Score every axis honestly; name the two lowest; write the weakness-targeting week's plan.\n6. **Execute the week, then taper.** One week on the floor-raising drills, then the final checklist: no new topics, redrill, mock, rest."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Your deliverable: the loop-day dossier — the convergence of the whole course.\n- **The schedule**: your four-round loop with times, roles, and rubrics.\n- **Score sheets**: every axis, every round, scored honestly.\n- **The debrief**: the two lowest axes, miss categories, the weakness-targeting week's day-by-day plan.\n- **The final checklist**: logistics, sleep, story bank, the explicit not-study list.\n- **The reflection**: which round shape still isn't automatic — and the drill that makes it so. Then rest: the taper has started.\nReference shelf (module sources): **System Design Primer** (https://github.com/donnemartin/system-design-primer). **NeetCode** (https://neetcode.io/)."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "Preparation only — this course confers no certificate, and simulation scores are triage data, not predictions. Home loops lack real-loop adrenaline and real-panel skepticism; treat the dossier as a preparation instrument. Rest is part of the plan: the taper exists because performance is trained, then protected. You've done the work across five paths — SRE, DevOps, cloud, security, and the circuit. Trust the structure on loop day: declare, measure, narrate, decide."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: The highest-value activity right after a mock is:",
     "body": "**Problem.** The highest-value activity right after a mock is:\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** A written debrief naming what leaked, plus a redrill plan\n- **Why:** Improvement comes from the feedback loop: name the leak, target the redrill, verify next time.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Improvement comes from the feedback loop: name the leak, target the redrill, verify next time.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "SRE and platform interviews reward operators who can reason, not recite. In Full-Loop Simulation:\n- **Buzzword answers without mechanics.** Saying 'we used Kubernetes' without explaining the failure domain, the rollout strategy, or what broke. Mechanics or it didn't happen.\n- **No numbers.** SLOs, capacity, and incidents are quantitative. 'High availability' means nothing; '99.9% with a 43-minute monthly budget' is an answer.\n- **Unstructured incident stories.** Rambling war stories lose the plot. Structure every incident narrative: impact, detection, response, root cause, action items.\n- **Ignoring tradeoffs.** Every architecture choice costs something. Name what you gave up and why it was worth it — interviewers are listening for the tradeoff, not the choice.\n- **No prepared war stories.** 'Tell me about a hard outage' with no story ready is a missed layup. Bank three incidents you can tell cold: yours, with numbers.\n- **Theory without toil.** Never having been on call shows. Even homelab on-call rotations — alerting, runbooks, postmortems — build the instincts Full-Loop Simulation interviews probe."
    }
   ],
