 "cheatSheets": [
  {
   "title": "SLO math",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "h": "Definitions",
     "body": "**SLI** = measured ratio (good events / total). **SLO** = target on the SLI (99.9% / 30d). **Error budget** = 1 − SLO. **Burn rate** = budget consumption speed vs the SLO window."
    },
    {
     "h": "Budget arithmetic",
     "body": "Allowed failures = total requests × (1 − SLO). Consumed % = failed ÷ allowed × 100. 99.9% over 1M requests → 1,000 allowed failures. Exhausted budget → freeze risky releases."
    },
    {
     "h": "Alerting",
     "body": "Fast-burn: page (multiwindow, e.g. 1h + 5m at 14x). Slow-burn: ticket. Alert on **symptoms** (burn rate, golden signals), never on causes."
    },
    {
     "h": "Golden signals",
     "body": "Latency, traffic, errors, saturation. **USE** for resources (Utilization, Saturation, Errors). **RED** for services (Rate, Errors, Duration)."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Kubernetes triage",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "h": "Core objects",
     "body": "**Pod** (smallest unit) → **Deployment** (replicas + rolling updates) → **Service** (stable DNS/LB) → **Ingress** (external HTTP routing). **ConfigMap/Secret** for config; never bake config into images."
    },
    {
     "h": "Failure modes",
     "body": "**CrashLoopBackOff** — container can’t stay alive (bad cmd/config/probe). **ImagePullBackOff** — registry/auth. **OOMKilled** — limits vs reality. **Pending** — no schedulable node."
    },
    {
     "h": "Triage order",
     "body": "- `kubectl get pods` — what’s happening\n- `kubectl describe` — events narrate\n- `kubectl logs --previous` — why it died\n- `kubectl top` — resource pressure\n- `rollout history` — what changed",
     "code": "kubectl get pods -n prod -l app=api\nkubectl describe pod <pod> -n prod\nkubectl logs <pod> -n prod --previous --tail=100\nkubectl top pods -n prod -l app=api",
     "lang": "bash"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Incident command checklist",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "h": "Roles",
     "body": "**IC**: decides, coordinates — doesn’t debug. **Comms**: status updates. **Scribe**: timeline. **SMEs**: debug."
    },
    {
     "h": "Lifecycle",
     "body": "Detect → respond (declare, assemble) → **mitigate first** → resolve → blameless postmortem. Mitigation before root cause, always."
    },
    {
     "h": "Comms cadence",
     "body": "One channel (#incident), every ~15 min even with ‘no update’, impact-first: who’s affected, what we’re doing, when’s next."
    },
    {
     "h": "Postmortem",
     "body": "Blameless. Timeline, plural root causes (five whys with evidence), action items **with owners and dates**. The action items are the product."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Troubleshooting under pressure",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "h": "The method",
     "body": "Hypothesis → test → observe → update — **said out loud**. Time-box each hypothesis; narrate what each command tests."
    },
    {
     "h": "Network bisection",
     "body": "DNS resolves? → TCP connects? → TLS handshakes? → HTTP responds? Each layer’s test isolates the fault.",
     "code": "dig api.example.com\ncurl -v https://api.example.com/healthz\nopenssl s_client -connect api.example.com:443",
     "lang": "bash"
    },
    {
     "h": "First suspects",
     "body": "What changed? (deploys, config, traffic). Correlate metric spikes with deploy annotations. Check the four golden signals before theorizing."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Reliability math — SLIs, SLOs, error budgets",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "h": "SLI → SLO",
     "body": "SLI: the measurement (success ratio, p99 latency). SLO: the target (99.9% over 30d). SLA: the contract with consequences."
    },
    {
     "h": "Budget table",
     "body": "99% → 7h18m/mo · 99.5% → 3h39m · 99.9% → 43m · 99.95% → 22m · 99.99% → 4m23s. Memorize 99.9 and 99.99."
    },
    {
     "h": "Burn rate",
     "body": "How fast you're consuming budget vs. time elapsed. Alert on fast burn (paging) vs slow burn (ticket). Multi-window alerts avoid flapping."
    },
    {
     "h": "Policy",
     "body": "Budget remaining → ship. Budget exhausted → reliability work only. Blameless postmortems with owned action items."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Incident command — the first 30 minutes",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "h": "Roles",
     "body": "Incident commander (coordinates), comms lead (status), ops (fixes), scribe (timeline). Commander doesn't type fixes."
    },
    {
     "h": "Triage",
     "body": "Impact → scope → recent changes → mitigate (rollback/feature-flag/scale) → then root-cause. Mitigation beats diagnosis under pressure."
    },
    {
     "h": "Comms",
     "body": "Status page updates every 15–30 min: what's broken, who's affected, what you're doing, next update time. Internal + external channels."
    },
    {
     "h": "Review",
     "body": "Blameless: timeline, contributing factors (never 'human error'), action items with owners and dates. Publish widely."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Troubleshooting aloud — the method",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "h": "Narrate the model",
     "body": "'Traffic flows LB → app → cache → DB. Latency spiked at the app tier, so I suspect...' — interviewers score the mental model, not just the fix."
    },
    {
     "h": "Bisect",
     "body": "Halve the system: is it app or data? Which tier? Which deploy? Binary search the blast radius."
    },
    {
     "h": "Change correlation",
     "body": "'What changed?' — deploys, config, traffic, certs, cron. Most incidents are caused by a change; find it."
    },
    {
     "h": "Evidence ladder",
     "body": "Metrics → logs → traces → repro. Each step narrows. State what would falsify your current hypothesis."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "answerBank": [
  {
   "q": "What is an SLO, and why does the error budget matter?",
   "a": "An SLO is a target on a measured indicator — e.g. 99.9% of requests succeed over 30 days. The error budget (1 − SLO) turns reliability into arithmetic: budget remaining means ship freely; budget exhausted means freeze risky changes. It replaces arguments about ‘reliable enough’ with a number both product and engineering agree on, and burn-rate alerting pages us only when the objective is actually at risk."
  },
  {
   "q": "Walk me through your incident response.",
   "a": "Declare the incident and assume command — the IC coordinates, doesn’t debug. Open the bridge and #incident, assign comms and scribe. Mitigate first (rollback, shed load), understand second. Comms go out every ~15 minutes, impact-first. Afterward: blameless postmortem with a timeline, plural root causes from evidence, and action items with owners and dates — because the action items are the actual product."
  },
  {
   "q": "How would you design a canary deployment?",
   "a": "Ship the new version to a small percentage first, with automated promotion gated on error-rate and latency comparisons against the baseline — and automated rollback on breach. I’d keep the previous immutable artifact one command away, make DB migrations backward-compatible via expand-contract, and rehearse the rollback. Canary bounds the blast radius: problems meet 1% of users before 100%."
  },
  {
   "q": "Latency just spiked. How do you troubleshoot it live?",
   "a": "Hypothesis-driven and narrated: check the four golden signals to scope it (which service, which endpoints via RED), correlate with deploy annotations — ‘what changed?’ is the first question — then bisect: is it downstream, the data layer (slow queries, pool exhaustion), or resource saturation (USE)? Each command tests one hypothesis; I time-box and update out loud rather than guessing silently."
  },
  {
   "q": "Blue-green vs canary vs rolling — how do you choose?",
   "a": "By blast-radius tolerance and cost. Blue-green: instant cutover and rollback, but double capacity. Canary: safest for risky user-facing changes — progressive exposure with metric gates. Rolling: simplest, slowest rollback. I’d default to canary for the user-facing API, and I always name the rollback story for whichever I pick — a strategy without a rollback plan is a hope."
  },
  {
   "q": "How would you secure a cloud account from scratch?",
   "a": "Identity first: SSO federation, no long-lived keys, workload roles, least-privilege policies with no wildcards. Guardrails: SCPs blocking the dangerous actions, Config rules detective. Data: KMS encryption at rest, TLS everywhere, secrets in a manager — never in repos or images. Logging: CloudTrail on with an audit trail nobody can quietly disable. Then policy-as-code so the guardrails are compiler errors, not wiki pages."
  },
  {
   "q": "Tell me about an outage you handled.",
   "a": "STAR with SRE texture: the stakes fast (what was down, who was affected), the investigation as hypotheses tested — including the wrong call I made and what it cost — and the landing as a changed practice: what went into the runbook and the evidence it stuck. I keep it blameless and systems-focused; the lesson with proof is the whole point of the story."
  }
 ],
